        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Afamelanotide (Scenesse) Chemical name [Nle⁴,D-Phe⁷]-α-MSH Molecular formula C₇₈H₁₁₁N₂₁O₁₉ Molecular weight 1,646.87 g/mol CAS number 75921-69-6 Sequence Ac-Ser-Tyr-Ser-Nle-Glu-His-D-Phe-Arg-Trp-Gly-Lys-Pro-Val-NH₂ Length 13 amino acids Source Synthetic (α-MSH analog) Half-life ~30 minutes (plasma); prolonged by implant (subcutaneous depot ~10 days) Targets Melanocortin 1 receptor (MC1R) Bioavailability Subcutaneous implant (controlled release) Legal status FDA-approved (2019) / EMA-approved (2014) for EPP; Rx only Afamelanotide (trade name Scenesse ) is a synthetic tridecapeptide analog of alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone (α-MSH) that acts as a potent and selective melanocortin 1 receptor (MC1R) agonist . It is the first melanocortin receptor agonist approved as a pharmaceutical, receiving authorization from the European Medicines Agency (EMA) in December 2014 and the U.S.",
        "evidence": "Two Phase III, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trials (CUV039 in Europe, CUV040 in the US) provided the basis for regulatory approval: CUV039 (n=74): Afamelanotide significantly increased median daily duration of direct sunlight exposure without pain (69.4 vs. 40.5 minutes/day) and increased quality of life scores CUV040 (n=94): Significant increase in total time in direct sunlight with reduced phototoxicity severity Grimes et al. (2013) demonstrated that afamelanotide combined with narrowband UVB phototherapy produced faster and more complete repigmentation in vitiligo patients compared to NB-UVB alone, particularly in skin types IV-VI. Studies in healthy volunteers have demonstrated that afamelanotide-induced melanization provides meaningful photoprotection equivalent to approximately SPF 3-4, with the additional benefit of enhanced DNA repair capacity. [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] [ 5 ] [ 6 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Langendonk JG, Balwani M, Anderson KE, et al. (2015). \"Afamelanotide for erythropoietic protoporphyria.\" New England Journal of Medicine . 373(1):48-59.",
