        "summary": "AHK-Cu (alanyl-histidyl-lysyl copper complex, also known as Copper Tripeptide-3) is a synthetic copper-binding tripeptide structurally related to GHK-Cu but with a distinct amino acid sequence - alanine replaces glycine at the N-terminal position. This single substitution meaningfully shifts the compound's biological selectivity. While GHK-Cu operates as a broad-spectrum tissue regenerative signal, AHK-Cu is heavily biased toward endothelial cell activation and dermal papilla cell support, making it the preferred copper peptide for hair follicle regeneration and microvascular integrity. At the cellular level, AHK-Cu promotes dermal papilla cell (DPC) survival and proliferation, inhibits DHT-mediated follicle miniaturization by modulating androgen receptor sensitivity in follicular tissue, and stimulates angiogenesis around the hair bulb - the perifollicular vascular supply that determines follicle size and growth-phase duration.",
