        "summary": "An oral orexin receptor 2 (OX2R) agonist developed by Alkermes for narcolepsy type 1 (NT1). Phase 2 clinical trials demonstrated improvements in excessive daytime sleepiness and cataplexy with once-daily dosing. Represents a novel approach to restore orexin signaling in patients who have lost orexin-producing neurons.",
        "evidence": "Moderate evidence; 12 human studies listed. two adequately-powered Phase 2 RCTs (Vibrance-1 NT1 n=92; Vibrance-2 NT2) hit primary endpoints, but data are topline/conference-stage, not peer-reviewed full text. Mechanism: OX2R target validated by knockout/ knockdown abolishing phenotype, full Gq pathway mapped. Plausibility: complete chain replacing the orexin signal lost in NT1, fully coherent with disease biology. Global coverage: single developer (Alkermes), under FDA investigation, modest compound-specific literature. Community experience: negligible real-world use outside trials, no user reports. Effectiveness basis clinical: quantified MWT/ ESS gains vs placebo, dose-dependent and clinically meaningful, no head-to-head.",
