        "sourceId": "pep-university",
        "url": "https://pepuniversity.com/peptide-codex.php#alprostadil",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "Alprostadil is synthetic prostaglandin E1 (PGE1) - a naturally occurring 20-carbon eicosanoid lipid mediator derived from dihomo-γ-linolenic acid through the cyclooxygenase pathway. Endogenous PGE1 is produced locally in penile vascular and cavernosal smooth muscle tissue where it acts on EP2 and EP4 prostaglandin receptors (Gs-coupled GPCRs) to activate adenylyl cyclase, increase intracellular cAMP, and activate protein kinase A - producing smooth muscle relaxation, vasodilation of the helicine arteries of the corpora cavernosa, and venous outflow restriction through sinusoidal dilation. This vascular mechanism produces erection through direct local hemodynamic control: blood flows into the corpora cavernosa faster than it drains, producing the intracorporeal pressure elevation that constitutes erection.",
        "evidence": "Peptide University labels its records as aggregated from empirical research and anecdotal community data. This record is retained as supplied and is not upgraded to clinical evidence.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Alprostadil is synthetic prostaglandin E1 (PGE1) - a naturally occurring 20-carbon eicosanoid lipid mediator derived from dihomo-γ-linolenic acid through the cyclooxygenase pathway. Endogenous PGE1 is produced locally in penile vascular and cavernosal smooth muscle tissue where it acts on EP2 and EP4 prostaglandin receptors (Gs-coupled GPCRs) to activate adenylyl cyclase, increase intracellular cAMP, and activate protein kinase A - producing smooth muscle relaxation, vasodilation of the helicine arteries of the corpora cavernosa, and venous outflow restriction through sinusoidal dilation. This vascular mechanism produces erection through direct local hemodynamic control: blood flows into the corpora cavernosa faster than it drains, producing the intracorporeal pressure elevation that constitutes erection.",
        "limitations": [
          "Sickle cell anemia and sickle cell trait - the red blood cell sickling triggered by the hypoxic, acidotic environment of sustained erection dramatically increases the risk of ischemic priapism in sickle cell subjects; absolute contraindication.",
          "Leukemia and other conditions with abnormal blood viscosity or hypercoagulability - abnormal blood cell populations obstructing cavernosal venous outflow create priapism risk independent of dose.",
          "Multiple myeloma and polycythemia vera - hyperviscosity states increase intracavernosal stasis and priapism risk.",
          "Thrombocytopenia or bleeding disorders - intracavernosal injection in subjects with impaired hemostasis creates risk of uncontrolled intracorporeal hematoma.",
          "Concurrent anticoagulant therapy (warfarin, novel oral anticoagulants, therapeutic heparin) - same mechanism as bleeding disorder risk; must be weighed carefully.",
          "Concurrent use with other vasoactive erectile agents (PDE5 inhibitors, PT-141 within 24 hours) - additive vasodilatory effects dramatically increase priapism risk; combination of intracavernosal alprostadil with oral PDE5 inhibitors is specifically warned against.",
          "Penile anatomical abnormalities (severe Peyronie's disease with plaques, penile fibrosis, penile angulation) - intracavernosal injection into abnormal tissue carries injection complication risk and may not produce physiological erection.",
          "Hypersensitivity to alprostadil, prostaglandin E1, or formulation excipients."
        ],
        "safety": [
          "Penile pain at injection site - the most commonly reported adverse effect; occurs in approximately 30-50% of subjects with intracavernosal injection; caused by direct PGE1 nociceptor activation in cavernosal tissue (EP1 receptor stimulation); typically mild to moderate; the primary reason men discontinue.",
          "Priapism (prolonged erection > 4 hours) - the most serious adverse effect; ischemic priapism destroys cavernosal smooth muscle through hypoxia and acidosis; frequency approximately 1-5% per injection; higher with excessive doses; constitutes a urological emergency requiring immediate intervention; the driver of the.",
          "Penile fibrosis and Peyronie's disease development - repeated intracavernosal injections at the same site cause chronic micro-trauma and fibrotic healing; produces penile curvature, plaques, and eventually impairs the erectile mechanism.",
          "Hematoma at injection site - subcutaneous or intracorporeal bleeding from the injection; more common with poor technique or anticoagulation; managed with sustained pressure.",
          "Hypotension - systemic absorption of PGE1 from intracavernosal administration produces peripheral vasodilation at higher doses; typically mild but symptomatic in subjects with cardiovascular disease or baseline hypotension.",
          "Dizziness and lightheadedness - secondary to the mild hypotensive effect; positional changes during the active erection period should be managed carefully.",
          "Urethral burning - if injection inadvertently contacts periurethral tissue; not characteristic of correct intracavernosal technique.",
          "Prolonged or recurrent erection not reaching priapism threshold - erections lasting 2-4 hours that spontaneously resolve with activity, cold application, or mild exercise; a warning sign that dose reduction is warranted.",
          "Penile ecchymosis - bruising along the penile shaft from extravasation of blood during injection; cosmetic; self-resolving.",
          "Rare: systemic PGE1 effects (flushing, diaphoresis) at highest doses - more common with excessive doses or accidental intravascular injection."
        ],
