        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Anamorelin Chemical name Anamorelin hydrochloride Molecular formula C₃₁H₄₂N₆O₃ Molecular weight 546.7 g/mol CAS number 249921-19-5 Sequence Non-peptide ghrelin receptor agonist (peptidomimetic) Length Small molecule (peptidomimetic) Source Synthetic Half-life 7-12 hours Targets Growth hormone secretagogue receptor (GHSR1a / Ghrelin receptor) Bioavailability Oral Legal status Approved (Japan, 2021) Anamorelin (brand name Adlumiz in Japan) is an orally active, selective ghrelin receptor (GHSR1a) agonist developed by Helsinn Healthcare for the treatment of cancer-related anorexia-cachexia syndrome. It is a non-peptide peptidomimetic that mimics the appetite-stimulating and growth hormone-releasing effects of endogenous ghrelin . Anamorelin was approved in Japan in January 2021 for cancer cachexia in patients with non-small cell lung cancer, gastric cancer, pancreatic cancer, and colorectal cancer.",
        "evidence": "ROMANA 1 (n=484): Anamorelin vs placebo in NSCLC cachexia - significant LBM increase (+1.10 kg, p<0.0001) at 12 weeks ROMANA 2 (n=495): Confirmatory trial - significant LBM increase (+0.75 kg, p<0.0001) ROMANA 3 : Extension study - sustained LBM gains over 24 weeks Japanese Phase 3 : Led to approval in Japan; similar efficacy with acceptable safety Combination with exercise interventions for cachexia Investigation in other cachexia etiologies (COPD, heart failure, chronic kidney disease) Biomarker-driven patient selection strategies [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Temel JS, et al. \"Anamorelin in patients with non-small-cell lung cancer and cachexia (ROMANA 1 and ROMANA 2).\" Lancet Oncol . 2016;17(4):519-531. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Currow DC, et al. \"ROMANA 3: a phase 3 safety extension study of anamorelin in advanced non-small-cell lung cancer.\" Ann Oncol . 2017;28(8):1949-1956. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Hamauchi S, et al. \"Phase 2 study of anamorelin in Japanese patients with cachexia and NSCLC.\" Jpn J Clin Oncol . 2019.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Anamorelin activates the ghrelin receptor (GHSR1a) with high selectivity: Appetite Stimulation : Activates hypothalamic GHSR1a → increases NPY/AgRP neuron activity → stimulates food intake Growth Hormone Release : Stimulates pituitary GH secretion via GHSR1a on somatotroph cells Anabolic Effects : Increases lean body mass through GH/IGF-1 axis activation Anti-Inflammatory : Reduces pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF-alpha, IL-6) implicated in cachexia GI Motility : Promotes gastric motility and gastric emptying Metabolic Effects : Improves nitrogen balance and protein synthesis Unlike ibutamoren (MK-677) , anamorelin has a shorter half-life and was specifically developed for cancer cachexia with a focused clinical development program.",
        "limitations": [
          "No significant improvement in handgrip strength in ROMANA 1/2 (co-primary endpoint missed) This led to FDA regulatory challenges - LBM gain alone may not be clinically meaningful"
        ],
