        "evidence": "preliminary. AOD-9604 is a modified C-terminal fragment of human growth hormone (hGH 177-191) studied for obesity treatment and, subsequently, cartilage repair. Animal studies established lipolytic activity through antilipogenic and metabolic mechanisms of the hGH 177-191 sequence, including reduction of adipose tissue in obese rodent models. A randomized, placebo-controlled Phase IIb clinical program involving 536 participants failed to demonstrate sufficient weight-loss efficacy to support Phase III advancement for obesity, as documented in FDA Pharmacy Compounding Advisory Committee briefing materials. AOD-9604 achieved GRAS (Generally Recognized as Safe) food additive status in Australia before that classification was withdrawn. Subsequent research has examined osteoarthritis cartilage regeneration with exploratory signals. No successful human efficacy program has been completed. The two previously stored PubMed identifiers (PMID 11238494 and PMID 10997923) were confirmed as entity mismatches and have been removed.",
