        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia AOD-9604 Chemical name HGH Fragment 176-191 Molecular formula C78H123N23O23S2 Molecular weight 1815.08 g/mol CAS number 221231-10-3 Sequence Tyr-Leu-Arg-Ile-Val-Gln-Cys-Arg-Ser-Val-Glu-Gly-Ser-Cys-Gly-Phe Length 16 amino acids Source Synthetic HGH fragment Half-life ~30 minutes Targets Growth hormone receptors (adipocytes) Bioavailability Subcutaneous injection Legal status Research compound only AOD-9604 (Anti-Obesity Drug 9604) is a synthetic peptide fragment derived from the C-terminus of human growth hormone, specifically amino acids 176-191. Originally developed by Metabolic Pharmaceuticals in Australia as a potential obesity treatment, this peptide was engineered to retain the fat-burning properties of growth hormone while eliminating unwanted side effects like elevated blood sugar and tissue growth.",
        "evidence": "Accelerated fat loss, particularly around the midsection Increased energy levels and metabolic rate No impact on blood sugar or insulin sensitivity Preservation of muscle mass during caloric restriction Enhanced recovery when combined with exercise Phase I Studies : Established safety profile with no serious adverse events Phase II Trials : Mixed results led to development discontinuation in 2007 Efficacy Findings : Some studies showed modest fat loss, others failed to meet endpoints Safety Profile : Generally well-tolerated across different populations No longer in clinical development for obesity treatment Available for research purposes only Ongoing studies in metabolic research and aging Not approved by FDA or other regulatory agencies for therapeutic use [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Ng, F.M., et al. (2000). \"Growth hormone treatment of the obese zucker rat.\" Endocrinology, 141(12), 4413-4419. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Heffernan, M.A., et al. (2001). \"The effects of human GH and its lipolytic fragment (AOD9604) on lipid metabolism.\" European Journal of Endocrinology, 145(3), 319-329.",
