        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Apelin-13 Chemical name [Pyr¹]-Apelin-13 (pyroglutamyl form) Molecular formula C₆₉H₁₀₈N₂₂O₁₆S Molecular weight 1,533.82 g/mol (pyroglutamyl form) CAS number 217082-60-5 (pyroglutamyl); 217082-58-1 (standard) Sequence pGlu-Arg-Pro-Arg-Leu-Ser-His-Lys-Gly-Pro-Met-Pro-Phe Length 13 amino acids Source Endogenous (encoded by APLN gene) Half-life ~5 minutes (plasma; degraded by ACE2 and neprilysin) Targets APJ receptor (Apelin receptor, APLNR) Bioavailability Low oral (research: IV, SC, intranasal) Legal status Research compound (not approved as drug; clinical trials ongoing) Apelin-13 is a thirteen-amino acid endogenous peptide and the most physiologically potent isoform of the apelin family, a group of bioactive peptides encoded by the APLN gene that serve as endogenous ligands for the APJ receptor (also called the apelin receptor or APLNR), a class A (rhodopsin-like) G protein-coupled receptor.",
        "evidence": "Apelin-13 is extensively used in cardiovascular, metabolic, and pulmonary research: Heart failure pathophysiology and therapeutics Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) research Glucose metabolism and insulin signaling studies Angiogenesis and vascular biology Neuroendocrine regulation ACE2 substrate and apelin-angiotensin crosstalk studies Intravenous: Bolus (0.1-30 nmol/kg) or continuous infusion (0.01-1 nmol/kg/min) for cardiovascular studies Subcutaneous: Osmotic minipump (0.1-2 μmol/kg/day) for chronic studies in rodent models Intracerebroventricular (ICV): 0.1-10 nmol for central nervous system studies Reconstitution: Dissolve in sterile water or saline; acidified solutions (0.1% TFA in water) may be used for stock preparation Stock solutions: Prepare at 1-10 mM; aliquot and store at -80°C Ashley et al. (2005) demonstrated that apelin-13 produces potent positive inotropic effects in isolated human heart tissue, with efficacy maintained in failing myocardium - a critical distinction from other inotropic agents that often show reduced efficacy in heart failure. Dray et al.",
