        "sourceId": "wikipep",
        "url": "https://wikipep.org/wiki/ARA-290",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia ARA-290 Chemical name ARA 290 (Cibinetide) Molecular formula C₅₅H₈₇N₁₇O₁₇ Molecular weight ~1,258 Da CAS number 1208243-50-8 Sequence QEQLERALNSS (11 amino acids) Length 11 amino acids Source Synthetic (derived from EPO helix B) Half-life ~2 minutes (IV); longer tissue residence Targets Innate repair receptor (EPOR/βcR heterodimer) Bioavailability Subcutaneous or intravenous Legal status Investigational (Phase 2/3) ARA-290 (also known as cibinetide ) is a synthetic 11-amino acid peptide derived from the B helix of erythropoietin (EPO) that selectively activates the innate repair receptor (IRR) - a heterodimer of the EPO receptor (EPOR) and the beta common receptor (βcR/CD131). Developed by Araim Pharmaceuticals, ARA-290 was designed to harness EPO's tissue-protective and anti-inflammatory properties without its erythropoietic (blood cell-stimulating) effects, which cause dangerous polycythemia at the doses required for tissue protection.",
        "evidence": "[ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Brines M, et al. \"ARA 290, a nonerythropoietic peptide engineered from erythropoietin, improves metabolic control and neuropathic symptoms in patients with type 2 diabetes.\" Mol Med . 2014;20:658-666. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Brines M, Cerami A. \"The receptor that tames the innate immune response.\" Mol Med . 2012;18:486-496. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Dahan A, et al. \"ARA 290 improves symptoms in patients with sarcoidosis-associated small nerve fiber loss.\" Mol Med . 2013;19:334-345. ↑ Leist M, et al. \"Derivatives of erythropoietin that are tissue protective but not erythropoietic.\" Science . 2004;305:239-242. P Pep Dex Track saturation levels, half-life decay curves, and injection schedules for 50+ peptides. AI coaching, blood work correlation, and side effect monitoring. Includes MacroDex (4.39M foods) & FitDex (1,356 exercises).",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "ARA-290 activates the innate repair receptor to promote tissue protection and regeneration: EPOR/βcR Heterodimer Activation - Selectively binds the innate repair receptor (EPOR/βcR), triggering JAK2/STAT5 signaling that is qualitatively different from classical EPOR homodimer activation used in erythropoiesis No Erythropoietic Activity - Does not activate the classical (EPOR)₂ homodimer at any tested concentration; does not stimulate red blood cell production, avoiding polycythemia risk Anti-inflammatory Signaling - EPOR/βcR activation suppresses NF-κB nuclear translocation and downstream pro-inflammatory gene expression (TNF-α, IL-1β, IL-6), providing potent anti-inflammatory effects Neuroprotection - Protects neurons and Schwann cells from apoptosis through PI3K/Akt survival signaling; promotes nerve regeneration and remyelination in small fiber neuropathy models Small Fiber Nerve Regeneration - Stimulates corneal and epidermal nerve fiber regrowth in diabetic neuropathy; demonstrated clinically by increased corneal nerve fiber density (CNFD) on confocal microscopy",
