        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Bombesin Chemical name Bombesin Molecular formula C₇₁H₁₁₀N₂₄O₁₈S Molecular weight 1619.9 g/mol CAS number 31362-50-2 Sequence pGlu-Gln-Arg-Leu-Gly-Asn-Gln-Trp-Ala-Val-Gly-His-Leu-Met-NH₂ (14 aa) Length 14 amino acids Source Skin of European fire-bellied toad (Bombina bombina); human homologs: GRP (gastrin-releasing peptide) and neuromedin B Half-life ~2-5 minutes (rapid enzymatic degradation) Targets GRP receptor (GRPR/BB2), neuromedin B receptor (NMBR/BB1), BRS-3 (BB3) Bioavailability IV, SC (research); not orally bioavailable Legal status Research compound (natural amphibian peptide) Bombesin is a 14-amino acid neuropeptide originally isolated from the skin of the European fire-bellied toad ( Bombina bombina ) by Anastasi, Erspamer, and Bucci in 1971.",
        "evidence": "GRP/bombesin are potent satiety signals; GRPR knockout mice develop late-onset obesity GRPR agonists under investigation for obesity treatment (but GI side effects limit clinical utility) Experimental compound - not used therapeutically in native form Pharmacological effects include transient hypotension, gastric acid secretion, and hypothermia Radiolabeled analogs (⁶⁸Ga, ¹⁷⁷Lu) follow standard nuclear medicine safety protocols GRPR antagonists preferred over agonists for clinical imaging to minimize pharmacological side effects [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] [ 5 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Anastasi A, et al. \"Isolation and Structure of Bombesin and Alytesin, Two Analogous Active Peptides from the Skin of the European Amphibians Bombina and Alytes.\" Experientia . 1971;27:166-167. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Jensen RT, et al. \"International Union of Pharmacology. LXVIII. Mammalian Bombesin Receptors.\" Pharmacol Rev . 2008;60:1-42. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Mansi R, et al. \"Bombesin-Related Peptides for Cancer Imaging: Clinical Translation and Current Development.\" J Nucl Med . 2021;62:1538-1544. ↑ Gonzalez N, et al.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "GRPR (BB2) Activation : Primary high-affinity receptor; Gq-coupled → PLC → IP3/DAG → intracellular Ca²⁺ mobilization → smooth muscle contraction, gastrin release, cell proliferation Gastrin Release : Stimulates antral G cells to release gastrin → gastric acid secretion via H⁺/K⁺-ATPase (hence \"gastrin-releasing peptide\") Satiety Signal : Central and peripheral bombesin/GRP administration potently reduces food intake in all species tested (one of the first identified satiety peptides) Thermoregulation : Central injection produces dose-dependent hypothermia; modulates brown adipose tissue activity via sympathetic nervous system Circadian Rhythm : GRP neurons in the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) modulate circadian clock function; GRP mediates photic resetting of the circadian pacemaker Mitogenic Signaling : GRPR activation promotes cell proliferation via MAPK/ERK, PI3K/Akt, and NF-κB pathways - the key cancer-relevant mechanism Autocrine Growth Loop : Many tumors both produce GRP and express GRPR, creating a self-sustaining autocrine proliferative loop Fetal Lung Development :",
