        "mechanism": "BPC-157 (Body Protective Compound-157) is a synthetic pentadecapeptide - a 15 amino acid sequence derived from a larger protein found in human gastric juice. It is entirely lab-synthesized and does not exist at functional concentrations in endogenous physiology, but it is structurally derived from the native gastric protein BPC, which is part of the stomach's intrinsic cytoprotective system. BPC-157 has the largest preclinical research base of any compound in this database, with hundreds of published animal studies spanning musculoskeletal repair, gastrointestinal healing, neurological protection, and cardiovascular effects. Its primary mechanisms operate through two major pathways: upregulation of VEGF (Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor) signaling via VEGFR2 receptor activation, which drives angiogenesis (new blood vessel formation) and accelerates microvascular repair; and activation of the Akt/eNOS pathway to increase endothelial nitric oxide production, which promotes vasodilation, reduces inflammation, and stabilizes endothelial integrity.",
