        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Bradykinin Chemical name Arg-Pro-Pro-Gly-Phe-Ser-Pro-Phe-Arg Molecular formula C₅₀H₇₃N₁₅O₁₁ Molecular weight 1,060.22 g/mol CAS number 58-82-2 Sequence Arg-Pro-Pro-Gly-Phe-Ser-Pro-Phe-Arg (RPPGFSPFR) Length 9 amino acids (nonapeptide) Source Endogenous (kallikrein-kinin system) Half-life ~15-30 seconds (plasma; rapidly degraded by kininases) Targets B₂ receptor (constitutive) and B₁ receptor (inducible) Bioavailability Negligible oral (peptide; rapidly degraded) Legal status Research compound; endogenous mediator Bradykinin is a nine-amino acid endogenous peptide (nonapeptide) and the principal effector molecule of the kallikrein-kinin system (KKS), one of the oldest and most conserved signaling cascades in mammalian physiology.",
        "evidence": "Many of the beneficial effects of ACE inhibitors (beyond angiotensin II suppression) are mediated by bradykinin accumulation: Enhanced endothelium-dependent vasodilation Tissue-protective effects in myocardial infarction Improved insulin sensitivity Anti-fibrotic effects in the heart and kidney Bradykinin is one of the most extensively studied peptides in biomedical research: Inflammation research: Standard mediator for studying inflammatory mechanisms Pain research: Model agonist for nociceptor activation and inflammatory pain Vascular biology: Studying endothelium-dependent vasodilation and vascular permeability ACE inhibitor pharmacology: Understanding kinin-mediated drug effects and side effects Hereditary angioedema: Pathophysiology and drug development Drug screening: Testing B₂ and B₁ receptor antagonists In vitro vasorelaxation: 10⁻¹⁰ to 10⁻⁶ M (dose-response in organ bath or myograph) In vitro pain assays: 10⁻⁸ to 10⁻⁶ M for nociceptor activation In vivo edema/pain: 1-10 nmol intradermal or intraplantar injection (rodent models) In vivo cardiovascular: 0.1-10 nmol/kg IV",
