        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Buserelin Chemical name Buserelin acetate (Suprefact, Suprecur) Molecular formula C₆₀H₈₆N₁₆O₁₃ Molecular weight 1239.4 g/mol CAS number 57982-77-1 Sequence pGlu-His-Trp-Ser-Tyr-D-Ser(tBu)-Leu-Arg-Pro-NHEt (modified GnRH analog) Length 10 amino acids (GnRH agonist with D-Ser(tBu)⁶ and NHEt substitutions) Source Synthetic (modified gonadotropin-releasing hormone) Half-life ~80 minutes (SC); prolonged with depot formulations (1-3 months) Targets GnRH receptor agonist → initial LH/FSH surge (\"flare\") → subsequent receptor downregulation → chemical castration Bioavailability SC injection, intranasal spray, depot implant Legal status Approved (EU, Canada, Australia; not FDA-approved in US) Buserelin (brand names Suprefact , Suprecur ) is a synthetic gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonist analog used clinically for the treatment of hormone-sensitive prostate cancer, endometriosis, uterine fibroids, and as part of controlled ovarian stimulation",
        "evidence": "Prostate Cancer : Medical castration without surgical orchiectomy; comparable oncological outcomes Endometriosis : Induces hypoestrogenic state → regression of endometrial implants and pain relief IVF/Assisted Reproduction : Prevents premature LH surge during controlled ovarian stimulation Uterine Fibroids : Pre-surgical shrinkage of fibroids by reducing estrogen drive Central Precocious Puberty : Suppresses premature activation of puberty Intranasal Option : Unique among GnRH agonists in offering a non-injectable nasal spray formulation [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Conn PM, Crowley WF. \"Gonadotropin-Releasing Hormone and Its Analogues.\" N Engl J Med . 1991;324(2):93-103. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Broekmans FJ. \"GnRH Agonists and Antagonists in Assisted Reproduction.\" Hum Reprod Update . 1998;4(6):851-867. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Klotz L, et al. \"Nasal Buserelin vs Leuprolide Acetate in Prostate Cancer.\" Eur Urol . 2019;75(5):755-762. ↑ Suprefact (buserelin) Product Monograph. Sanofi-Aventis Canada, 2021. P Pep Dex Track saturation levels, half-life decay curves, and injection schedules for 50+ peptides.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "GnRH Receptor Binding : Buserelin has ~50-100x greater affinity for the GnRH receptor compared to native GnRH, due to the D-Ser(tBu)⁶ substitution that resists enzymatic degradation and the C-terminal NHEt modification Initial Flare Phase (Days 1-14) : Continuous receptor stimulation initially increases LH and FSH release → transient rise in testosterone (men) or estradiol (women) Receptor Downregulation (Weeks 2-4) : Sustained agonism causes GnRH receptor internalization, desensitization, and downregulation → loss of pulsatile GnRH signaling Chemical Castration : Gonadotropin suppression → testosterone drops to castrate levels (<50 ng/dL in men); estradiol drops to postmenopausal levels (<20 pg/mL in women) Reversibility : Hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis recovers within 4-12 weeks after treatment discontinuation",
