        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "Cagrilintide is a long-acting amylin analog - and amylin is a hormone most people have never heard of despite it being one of the body's most important satiety signals. Amylin is co-secreted with insulin from pancreatic beta cells every time you eat, and its job is to signal the brain that food has arrived: it slows gastric emptying, suppresses glucagon, and most critically, activates appetite-regulating centers in the area postrema and hindbrain to create a durable feeling of fullness. Cagrilintide is an engineered analog of this hormone - modified to last a full week on a single subcutaneous injection, versus native amylin which clears in minutes. What makes cagrilintide pharmacologically distinct from every other compound in the incretin category is that it does not touch the GLP-1 receptor at all. It operates on an entirely separate hormonal pathway, which means it produces satiety through a completely different mechanism than semaglutide, tirzepatide, or any other GLP-1 compound.",
