        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Cardiogen Chemical name Cardiogen (AED; Ala-Glu-Asp) Molecular formula C₁₂H₁₉N₃O₇ Molecular weight 333.3 g/mol CAS number Research compound (Khavinson) Sequence Ala-Glu-Asp (AED) Length 3 amino acids (tripeptide bioregulator targeting cardiac tissue) Source Synthetic (Khavinson bioregulator peptide; derived from cardiac tissue extract analysis) Half-life Minutes (tripeptide; rapid tissue-specific uptake) Targets Cardiomyocyte gene regulation → contractile protein synthesis, anti-apoptotic pathways, cardiac repair Bioavailability Sublingual, oral, intranasal (small peptide crosses cell membranes) Legal status Research compound / dietary supplement Cardiogen ( Ala-Glu-Asp , AED ) is a synthetic tripeptide bioregulator developed by Professor Vladimir Khavinson, targeting cardiac tissue. It is designed to normalize gene expression in cardiomyocytes during aging and cardiovascular disease, promoting cardiac repair and functional restoration.",
        "evidence": "Cardiac Fibrosis : Cardiogen treatment reduced collagen I and III expression in aging rat myocardium → decreased fibrotic remodeling Cardiomyocyte Protection : Protected cultured cardiomyocytes from hydrogen peroxide-induced apoptosis (60% reduction in cell death) Gene Expression : Microarray analysis showed upregulation of 28 cardiac-specific genes and downregulation of 14 pro-fibrotic genes after 10-day treatment Life Extension : When combined with other bioregulators (Epithalon, Vilon), contributed to increased lifespan in Khavinson's animal studies 🧪 Vial Dex The World's Most Advanced Peptide Calculator 100% FREE 💧 Reconstitution Vial + BAC water mixing 📉 Dose & Units Syringe units to draw 🔀 Blend Multi-peptide vial ratios 📚 Stack Protocol Multi-vial dose planning 💨 Nasal Spray Spray bottle concentrations 50+ Peptides 7 Calculators 0 Signup Required Peptide: Cardiogen Open Free Calculator → [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Khavinson VK. \"Peptides and Ageing.\" Neuroendocrinol Lett . 2002;23 Suppl 3:11-144. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Khavinson VK, Malinin VV.",
        "halfLife": "",
