        "url": "https://pepuniversity.com/peptide-codex.php#cardiogen",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "Cardiogen is a synthetic tetrapeptide (Ala-Glu-Asp-Arg) Khavinson bioregulator derived from cardiac muscle polypeptide extracts - identified as the bioactive core sequence responsible for the cardioprotective and cardiomyocyte regenerative activity of the native heart tissue extract. The heart is a post-mitotic organ in which adult cardiomyocytes have extremely limited regenerative capacity - the human heart replaces approximately 1% of cardiomyocytes per year, declining further with age, meaning that ischemic cardiomyocyte loss and age-related cardiomyocyte functional decline cannot be meaningfully compensated by endogenous regeneration alone. This regenerative deficit underlies the progressive nature of heart failure following myocardial injury and the cumulative functional decline of cardiac output with aging.",
        "evidence": "Peptide University labels its records as aggregated from empirical research and anecdotal community data. This record is retained as supplied and is not upgraded to clinical evidence.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Cardiogen is a synthetic tetrapeptide (Ala-Glu-Asp-Arg) Khavinson bioregulator derived from cardiac muscle polypeptide extracts - identified as the bioactive core sequence responsible for the cardioprotective and cardiomyocyte regenerative activity of the native heart tissue extract. The heart is a post-mitotic organ in which adult cardiomyocytes have extremely limited regenerative capacity - the human heart replaces approximately 1% of cardiomyocytes per year, declining further with age, meaning that ischemic cardiomyocyte loss and age-related cardiomyocyte functional decline cannot be meaningfully compensated by endogenous regeneration alone. This regenerative deficit underlies the progressive nature of heart failure following myocardial injury and the cumulative functional decline of cardiac output with aging.",
        "limitations": [
          "Active myocarditis or infectious endocarditis - the cardiomyocyte-level bioregulator activity of Cardiogen during active cardiac infection/inflammation has not been evaluated; the inflammatory cardiac milieu of myocarditis represents an unpredictable context for cardiomyocyte gene program modulation.",
          "Recent cardiac surgery (within 4 weeks) - the acute post-surgical healing phase involves specific cardiomyocyte stress response programs; exogenous bioregulator activity during the immediate post-operative window has not been characterized.",
          "Severe decompensated heart failure requiring inotropic support - in subjects with cardiogenic shock or acutely decompensated heart failure, the priority is hemodynamic stabilization; Cardiogen is a chronic bioregulator not an acute cardiac support agent.",
          "Known hypersensitivity to Cardiogen (Ala-Glu-Asp-Arg tetrapeptide) or formulation excipients.",
          "Pregnancy - cardiomyocyte-level bioregulator effects during the significant cardiac remodeling of pregnancy are not characterized.",
          "Severe hepatic or renal impairment - peptide clearance and metabolic processing in severely impaired organ function has not been evaluated for Cardiogen.",
          "Concurrent use with high-dose cardiotoxic chemotherapy (anthracyclines, trastuzumab) - while Cardiogen's cardioprotective mechanism might theoretically be beneficial in this context, the interaction between cardiomyocyte bioregulator activity and chemotherapy-induced cardiomyocyte injury has not been characterized and"
        ],
        "safety": [
          "Mild fatigue on initial cycle days - some subjects report mild lethargy in the first 2-3 days of a new Cardiogen cycle; likely reflects the metabolic shift as cardiomyocyte energy utilization patterns normalize; typically resolves by day 4-5.",
          "Mild bradycardia (rare) - a small subset of subjects reports slightly reduced resting heart rate during Cardiogen cycles, consistent with improved cardiac efficiency and vagal tone normalization; typically benign and asymptomatic.",
          "Injection site mild reaction - standard subcutaneous peptide administration response.",
          "Transient mild chest awareness - a minority of subjects reports a mild non-painful cardiac awareness in the early days of a cycle; likely represents cardiomyocyte-level bioregulator activity being subjectively perceived; not associated with ECG abnormalities in clinical observation.",
          "No serious cardiac adverse events documented in the Khavinson literature - across multiple decades of clinical use in subjects with ischemic heart disease, cardiomyopathy, and aging-related cardiac decline, Cardiogen has not been associated with serious arrhythmias, hemodynamic instability, or adverse cardiac events.",
          "Unknown interaction profile with modern cardiac pharmacology (ARNI, SGLT2 inhibitors with cardiac benefit, novel antiarrhythmics) - Cardiogen predates these therapeutic classes; formal interaction data does not exist."
        ],
