        "url": "https://wikipep.org/wiki/Casomorphin-7",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Casomorphin-7 Chemical name β-Casomorphin-7 (BCM-7) Molecular formula C₃₈H₅₁N₇O₉ Molecular weight 789.9 g/mol CAS number 80466-77-9 Sequence Tyr-Pro-Phe-Pro-Gly-Pro-Ile (YPFPGPI) Length 7 amino acids Source Exogenous - released from β-casein A1 variant during gastrointestinal digestion of cow's milk Half-life ~20-30 minutes (resists proline-rich peptidases; eventually cleared by DPP-IV and other peptidases) Targets μ-Opioid receptor (MOR) agonist - weak to moderate affinity Bioavailability Oral (generated during digestion of A1 β-casein); limited systemic absorption in healthy adults Legal status Food-derived bioactive peptide (not regulated as a drug) β-Casomorphin-7 ( BCM-7 ) is a 7-amino acid opioid peptide released during gastrointestinal digestion of A1 β-casein , a milk protein variant found predominantly in milk from Holstein-Friesian and other European cattle breeds.",
        "evidence": "GI Symptoms : Ho et al. (2014) - 41 subjects; A1 milk produced softer stools and increased transit time vs. A2 milk Jianqin et al. (2016) - 45 subjects; A1 milk increased GI inflammation markers and slowed transit; A2 milk did not Lactose Intolerance Misattribution : Some \"lactose intolerance\" symptoms may actually be BCM-7-mediated [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] [ 5 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Brantl V, et al. \"Novel Opioid Peptides Derived from Casein (β-Casomorphins). I. Isolation from Bovine Casein Peptone.\" Hoppe-Seyler's Z Physiol Chem . 1979;360:1211-1216. ↑ 2.0 2.1 EFSA. \"Review of the Potential Health Impact of β-Casomorphins and Related Peptides.\" EFSA Scientific Report . 2009;231:1-107. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Jianqin S, et al. \"Effects of Milk Containing Only A2 Beta Casein versus Milk Containing Both A1 and A2 Beta Casein Proteins on Gastrointestinal Physiology.\" Nutr J . 2016;15:35. ↑ Nguyen DD, et al. \"Formation and Degradation of Beta-Casomorphins in Dairy Processing.\" Crit Rev Food Sci Nutr . 2015;55:1955-1967. ↑ Pal S, et al. \"Milk Intolerance, Beta-Casein and Lactose.\" Nutrients . 2015;7:7285-7297.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "μ-Opioid Receptor Activation : Moderate affinity for MOR (Ki ~1-10 μM); activates Gi signaling GI Motility Reduction : Slows intestinal transit via enteric nervous system MOR activation - may explain bloating/constipation with A1 milk Mucus Secretion : Stimulates mucin production in intestinal goblet cells Immune Modulation : Modulates T-cell function and cytokine production in vitro DPP-IV Substrate : Dipeptidyl peptidase IV cleaves the Tyr-Pro N-terminal dipeptide, inactivating BCM-7 (DPP-IV deficiency may increase exposure) Proline Resistance : Three prolines in the sequence (Pro², Pro⁴, Pro⁶) confer resistance to most aminopeptidases",
