        "sourceId": "wikipep",
        "url": "https://wikipep.org/wiki/Catestatin",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Catestatin Chemical name Catestatin (Chromogranin A₃₅₂₋₃₇₂) Molecular formula C₁₀₆H₁₆₁N₃₁O₂₆S₂ Molecular weight 2370.7 g/mol CAS number 205650-65-3 Sequence SSMKLSFRARAYGFRGPGPQL (21 amino acids, human) Length 21 amino acids Source Endogenous (chromaffin cells, adrenergic neurons - derived from chromogranin A) Half-life ~15-20 minutes (plasma) Targets Nicotinic cholinergic receptor (nAChR - inhibits); histamine H1 receptor; multiple autocrine/paracrine targets Bioavailability IV/SC (research) Legal status Research compound (not approved) Catestatin is an endogenous 21-amino acid peptide derived from proteolytic cleavage of chromogranin A (CgA₃₅₂₋₃₇₂) in chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla and sympathetic neurons. It was discovered in 1997 by Sushil Mahata and Daniel O'Connor as the first identified endogenous nicotinic cholinergic receptor antagonist, functioning as a physiological \"brake\" on catecholamine secretion.",
        "evidence": "[ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Mahata SK, et al. \"Novel autocrine feedback control of catecholamine release: A discrete chromogranin A fragment is a noncompetitive nicotinic cholinergic antagonist.\" J Clin Invest . 1997;100:1623-1633. ↑ 2.0 2.1 O'Connor DT, et al. \"Catestatin, a novel endogenous peptide, in cardiovascular and metabolic regulation.\" Trends Endocrinol Metab . 2008;19:237-242. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Mahata SK, et al. \"Catestatin: a multifunctional peptide from chromogranin A.\" Regul Pept . 2010;162:33-43. P Pep Dex Track saturation levels, half-life decay curves, and injection schedules for 50+ peptides. AI coaching, blood work correlation, and side effect monitoring. Includes MacroDex (4.39M foods) & FitDex (1,356 exercises). Saturation Tracking Half-Life Curves AI Coach 24hr Free Trial Try Free for 24 Hours → Retrieved from \" https://wikipep.org/index.php?title=Catestatin&oldid=3865 \" Categories : Peptides Endogenous Peptides Cardiovascular Peptides Antimicrobial Peptides Research Compounds Catestatin Add topic",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Nicotinic Receptor Antagonism : Non-competitive inhibitor of nAChR on chromaffin cells → blocks acetylcholine-stimulated catecholamine (epinephrine/norepinephrine) exocytosis Blood Pressure Regulation : Reduces sympathetic tone → vasodilation → blood pressure lowering Cardioprotection : Activates PI3K/Akt and ERK1/2 → protects cardiomyocytes from ischemia-reperfusion injury Vasodilation : Stimulates endothelial NO synthase (eNOS) → NO-mediated vasodilation; also activates histamine H1 receptors → mast cell histamine release → vasodilation Anti-Inflammatory : Suppresses NF-κB activation and reduces TNF-α, IL-6, and MCP-1 production by macrophages Antimicrobial : Direct antimicrobial activity against S. aureus, C. albicans; cathelicidin-like membrane disruption Insulin Sensitization : Improves hepatic and adipose insulin sensitivity; reduces hepatic glucose output Anti-Obesity : Reduces adipose tissue inflammation and promotes lipolysis in animal models",
        "limitations": [],
        "safety": [
          "Endogenous peptide - expected to be well-tolerated at physiological concentrations Hypotension : Dose-dependent blood pressure lowering (desired in hypertension; risk in normotensive) Histamine release : May trigger mast cell degranulation → flushing, urticaria at high doses Catecholamine suppression : Excessive."
        ],
