        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia CCK-8 Chemical name Cholecystokinin Octapeptide (CCK-8, Sincalide) Molecular formula C₄₉H₆₂N₁₀O₁₆S₂ Molecular weight 1143.2 g/mol CAS number 25126-32-3 (sulfated CCK-8) Sequence Asp-Tyr(SO₃H)-Met-Gly-Trp-Met-Asp-Phe-NH₂ (8 aa; C-terminal octapeptide of CCK-33) Length 8 amino acids (the minimum fully active fragment of cholecystokinin) Source Endogenous (I cells of duodenal and jejunal mucosa; CNS neurons - cortex, hippocampus, amygdala) Half-life ~2-3 minutes (rapid carboxypeptidase and sulfatase degradation) Targets CCK1R (peripheral - gallbladder, pancreas, gut; Gq-coupled) and CCK2R (central - brain, gastric parietal cells; Gq-coupled) Bioavailability IV injection (sincalide - FDA-approved diagnostic); no oral bioavailability Legal status FDA-approved as sincalide (Kinevac) for diagnostic gallbladder contraction and pancreatic function testing CCK-8 ( Cholecystokinin Octapeptide ) is the C-terminal 8-amino acid fragment of cholecystokinin",
        "evidence": "Satiety Research : Gold standard peptide for studying gut-brain appetite signaling Panic Disorder : CCK-4 (tetrapeptide fragment) reliably induces panic attacks in clinical studies - used to study panic disorder neurobiology Pain Research : CCK is a potent anti-opioid peptide - blocks opioid analgesia and promotes hyperalgesia; CCK antagonists enhance morphine analgesia [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Gibbs J, Young RC, Smith GP. \"Cholecystokinin Decreases Food Intake in Rats.\" J Comp Physiol Psychol . 1973;84:488-495. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Liddle RA. \"Cholecystokinin Cells.\" Annu Rev Physiol . 1997;59:221-242. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Rehfeld JF. \"Cholecystokinin - From Local Gut Hormone to Ubiquitous Messenger.\" Front Endocrinol . 2017;8:47. ↑ FDA Label: Kinevac (sincalide) for Injection. Bracco Diagnostics. P Pep Dex Track saturation levels, half-life decay curves, and injection schedules for 50+ peptides. AI coaching, blood work correlation, and side effect monitoring. Includes MacroDex (4.39M foods) & FitDex (1,356 exercises).",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "CCK1R Activation (Peripheral) : Gq → PLC → IP3/DAG → Ca²⁺ → gallbladder contraction, pancreatic enzyme secretion, sphincter of Oddi relaxation Satiety Signal : CCK1R activation on vagal afferents in gut wall → NTS → brainstem satiety centers → meal termination (short-acting - reduces meal size but not meal frequency) CCK2R Activation (Central) : Gq → Ca²⁺ signaling in cortex, hippocampus, amygdala → anxiogenic effects, enhanced memory, nociceptive modulation Gastric Acid : CCK2R on parietal cells and ECL cells → stimulates gastric acid secretion (CCK2R is identical to the gastrin receptor) Pancreatic Secretion : Most potent stimulator of pancreatic enzyme secretion (lipase, amylase, trypsin) - 1,000x more potent than secretin for enzyme release GI Motility : Inhibits gastric emptying, stimulates small intestinal and colonic motility",
