        "summary": "Cerluten is a Khavinson cytomedin - peptide complex A-5 - extracted from the brain and spinal cord tissue of young animals, developed as the oral central nervous system bioregulator of the Khavinson framework. It works by delivering a mixture of CNS tissue-derived low-molecular-weight peptides that interact with neurons, astrocytes, and oligodendrocytes at the transcriptional level - normalizing the gene expression programs governing neuronal survival, neurotrophic factor production (BDNF, NGF, GDNF), mitochondrial energy supply to neurons, anti-apoptotic neuroprotection, and the myelin maintenance programs of oligodendrocytes. In practical terms, Cerluten signals aging brain cells to restore the gene activity patterns of younger, more resilient neural tissue - slowing the accumulation of neuronal damage and supporting the brain's inherent repair capacity. Cerluten's clinical research evidence spans multiple neurological application domains.",
