        "mechanism": "Chitomur is a member of the Khavinson \"Cytomax\" class: a natural peptide bioregulator obtained by isolating low-molecular-weight peptide fractions from the urinary-bladder tissue of young animals at the St. Petersburg Institute of Bioregulation and Gerontology [3][6]. Unlike conventional peptide hormones, these ultrashort 2-to-4-residue peptides are not thought to act on cell-surface receptors. The prevailing hypothesis is that they are small and charged enough to enter the cytoplasm and nucleus of bladder epithelial and detrusor smooth-muscle cells, where they bind specific 3-to-6-nucleotide sequences in gene promoter regions, locally destabilize the DNA double helix, and modulate transcription of a defined, tissue-appropriate set of genes [2][4][7].",
