        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "Chonluten is a synthetic tripeptide (Lys-Glu-Asp) Khavinson bioregulator derived from lung and colon tissue polypeptide extracts - uniquely, the only Khavinson compound in this database with two distinct primary tissue targets. This dual-target architecture reflects the shared embryological origin of pulmonary and gastrointestinal epithelium from the foregut endoderm - the same primitive tissue layer that differentiates into both bronchial and intestinal epithelium - and the functional parallel between these two epithelial systems as mucosal barrier surfaces exposed to external environments that require coordinated immune surveillance, mucociliary or peristaltic clearance, and rapid regenerative response to injury. The alveolar epithelium of the lung parenchyma (the Chonluten lung target) is composed of Type I alveolar cells that form the ultra-thin gas-exchange surface and Type II pneumocytes that produce surfactant and serve as the alveolar progenitor cell population.",
        "evidence": "Peptide University labels its records as aggregated from empirical research and anecdotal community data. This record is retained as supplied and is not upgraded to clinical evidence.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Chonluten is a synthetic tripeptide (Lys-Glu-Asp) Khavinson bioregulator derived from lung and colon tissue polypeptide extracts - uniquely, the only Khavinson compound in this database with two distinct primary tissue targets. This dual-target architecture reflects the shared embryological origin of pulmonary and gastrointestinal epithelium from the foregut endoderm - the same primitive tissue layer that differentiates into both bronchial and intestinal epithelium - and the functional parallel between these two epithelial systems as mucosal barrier surfaces exposed to external environments that require coordinated immune surveillance, mucociliary or peristaltic clearance, and rapid regenerative response to injury. The alveolar epithelium of the lung parenchyma (the Chonluten lung target) is composed of Type I alveolar cells that form the ultra-thin gas-exchange surface and Type II pneumocytes that produce surfactant and serve as the alveolar progenitor cell population.",
        "limitations": [
          "Active pulmonary malignancy or colorectal carcinoma - Chonluten's dual epithelial bioregulator activity in the context of malignant alveolar or colonic epithelial cells has not been evaluated; oncological evaluation required.",
          "Active inflammatory bowel disease in acute flare (Crohn's disease, ulcerative colitis) - colonic epithelial gene program normalization during active transmural or mucosal inflammation of IBD has not been characterized; the priority is anti-inflammatory medical management of the flare.",
          "Active pulmonary infection requiring antibiotic/antiviral treatment - alveolar bioregulator activity during active pulmonary infection should be deferred until antimicrobial treatment is complete.",
          "Recent colonic or pulmonary surgery (within 4 weeks) - the acute surgical healing phase involves specific tissue repair programs; exogenous bioregulator activity during this window has not been evaluated.",
          "Known hypersensitivity to Chonluten (Lys-Glu-Asp tripeptide) or formulation excipients.",
          "Pregnancy - dual epithelial bioregulator effects on pulmonary and colonic tissue during pregnancy have not been evaluated.",
          "Active lung transplantation immunosuppression - alveolar epithelial bioregulator activity in immunosuppressed recipients with donor lung tissue requires transplant pulmonology evaluation.",
          "Severe active Clostridioides difficile colitis - colonic epithelial bioregulator activity during active C. difficile infection with significant mucosal injury should be deferred until antimicrobial treatment and clinical resolution."
        ],
        "safety": [
          "Mild increased mucus production - same mechanism as Bronchogen; as alveolar and bronchial surfactant and secretory programs normalize, some subjects notice mildly increased respiratory secretions in the first cycle days.",
          "Mild GI changes - as colonic epithelial activity normalizes, minor changes in bowel habit (stool frequency, consistency) are occasionally reported in the first week; typically transient and self-limiting.",
          "Mild bloating on initiation - uncommon; related to the shift in colonic epithelial function and altered interaction with the microbiome as gut barrier function improves.",
          "Mild fatigue on initial cycle days - related to the metabolic demand of simultaneous dual-tissue bioregulator activity; more pronounced than single-target bioregulators; typically resolves by days 3-5.",
          "Injection site mild reaction - standard subcutaneous peptide response.",
          "No serious pulmonary or GI adverse events documented in the Khavinson research literature at standard protocol doses - Chonluten's safety profile in the available published research is clean.",
          "Theoretical interaction with colonic drug absorption - as colonic epithelial function and tight junctions normalize, the absorption of oral medications that are absorbed in the colon could theoretically be affected; subjects on rectally-administered or colonic-absorption-dependent medications should be aware."
        ],
