        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-10",
        "summary": "CJC-1295 (no DAC), also called Modified GRF (1-29), is a synthetic analog of growth-hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH). It binds the GHRH receptor on the pituitary to stimulate pulsatile GH and IGF-1 release. The 'no DAC' form is short-acting (~30 minutes), which preserves natural pulsatility, in contrast to CJC-1295 with DAC (half-life of several days). It is not FDA-approved and is prohibited in sport. Native GHRH(1-29) is sermorelin. Modified GRF (1-29) introduces four amino-acid substitutions (positions 2, 8, 15, 27) to resist enzymatic degradation, extending half-life from a few minutes to at least ~30 minutes while keeping the short-acting, pulsatile profile. This is distinct from CJC-1295 'with DAC,' where a Drug Affinity Complex binds albumin to extend half-life to roughly 6-8 days for once-weekly dosing. The no-DAC form is favored in non-clinical use for being more physiologic and easier to titrate, and is commonly paired with a selective GHS such as ipamorelin.",
        "evidence": "The CJC-1295 (with DAC) form was studied in early human trials showing prolonged GH/IGF-1 elevation. Evidence specific to the no-DAC Mod GRF(1-29) form and to body-composition outcomes is limited; no approved indication exists.",
