        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "The skin's extracellular matrix (ECM) is composed primarily of collagen, elastin, and glycosaminoglycans. As skin ages, collagen production declines (~1% per year after age 30), elastin degrades, and UV-induced matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) break down existing structural proteins. Cosmetic peptides address aging through four distinct mechanisms: Signal peptides - Stimulate fibroblasts to produce collagen, elastin, and other ECM components Neurotransmitter-inhibitor peptides - Reduce muscle contraction at the skin surface, softening expression lines Carrier peptides - Deliver trace elements (especially copper) to skin cells for enzymatic processes Enzyme-inhibitor peptides - Block proteases that degrade collagen or inhibit pathological proteins For a detailed comparison of anti-wrinkle peptides, see Anti-Wrinkle_Peptides_Comparison .",
