        "mechanism": "Cotadutide engages two complementary metabolic receptor pathways: GLP-1 Receptor Agonism - Activates GLP-1R on pancreatic β-cells (glucose-dependent insulin secretion), hypothalamic appetite centers (food intake reduction), and gastric smooth muscle (delayed emptying); the GLP-1 component provides the glucose-lowering counterbalance to glucagon's hyperglycemic tendency Glucagon Receptor Agonism - Activates GCGR on hepatocytes, driving increased hepatic fatty acid β-oxidation, reduced hepatic lipogenesis (via AMPK and ACC inhibition), increased energy expenditure through uncoupled thermogenesis, and enhanced ketogenesis Hepatic Fat Reduction - Glucagon's GCGR activation is the primary driver of liver fat mobilization; glucagon promotes lipolysis and fatty acid oxidation in the liver while suppressing de novo lipogenesis - directly targeting the pathophysiology of MASH/NASH Energy Expenditure Increase - Glucagon increases resting energy expenditure by 5-15% through thermogenic effects (brown/beige adipose tissue activation, hepatic futile cycling) - an effect not achieved by GLP-1",
