        "url": "https://wikipep.org/wiki/Decapeptide-12",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Decapeptide-12 Chemical name Decapeptide-12 (Lumixyl peptide) Molecular formula C₅₆H₈₁N₁₅O₁₅ Molecular weight 1,208.3 g/mol (approximate) CAS number 137665-91-9 Sequence Tyr-Arg-Ser-Arg-Lys-Tyr-Ser-Ser-Trp-Tyr (10 amino acids) Length 10 amino acids Source Synthetic (identified by phage display screening against tyrosinase) Half-life Topical (acts locally in skin; not systemically absorbed) Targets Tyrosinase enzyme (competitive inhibition at the active site) Bioavailability Topical (cosmeceutical) Legal status Cosmetic ingredient (INCI registered); not a regulated drug Decapeptide-12 (marketed as the active ingredient in Lumixyl products) is a synthetic decapeptide identified through phage display library screening as a potent and selective inhibitor of human tyrosinase - the rate-limiting enzyme in melanin biosynthesis.",
        "evidence": "Split-face studies: significant improvement in melasma and post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation at 12-16 weeks Non-irritating - suitable for sensitive skin and post-procedure use Compatible with retinoids, chemical peels, and laser treatments [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Abu Ubeid A, et al. \"Discovery of a potent peptide inhibitor of human tyrosinase using phage display.\" Pigment Cell Melanoma Res . 2009;22:223-229. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Hantash BM, et al. \"A randomized controlled trial of Decapeptide-12 cream for melasma.\" J Drugs Dermatol . 2011;10:1098-1103. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Lumixyl Technical Monograph, Envy Medical. P Pep Dex Track saturation levels, half-life decay curves, and injection schedules for 50+ peptides. AI coaching, blood work correlation, and side effect monitoring. Includes MacroDex (4.39M foods) & FitDex (1,356 exercises).",
        "halfLife": "",
