        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Degarelix Chemical name Ac-D-2-Nal-D-4-Cpa-D-3-Pal-Ser-4-Aph(Hor)-D-4-Aph(Cbm)-Leu-Lys(iPr)-Pro-D-Ala-NH₂ Molecular formula C₈₂H₁₀₃ClN₁₈O₁₆ Molecular weight 1632.26 g/mol CAS number 214766-78-6 Sequence Ac-D-Nal-D-Cpa-D-Pal-Ser-Aph(Hor)-D-Aph(Cbm)-Leu-Lys(iPr)-Pro-D-Ala-NH₂ Length 10 amino acids (decapeptide) Source Synthetic GnRH antagonist Half-life ~53 days (after SC depot injection at maintenance dose) Targets GnRH receptor (GnRHR) competitive antagonist Bioavailability Subcutaneous depot injection (~30-40%) Legal status FDA Approved (2008 Firmagon); EMA Approved (2009); Rx only Degarelix (brand name Firmagon ) is a synthetic linear decapeptide that functions as a competitive antagonist of the gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) receptor.",
        "evidence": "CS21 (pivotal phase III): Randomized, open-label trial (n=610) comparing degarelix 240/80 mg and 240/160 mg with leuprolide 7.5 mg in advanced prostate cancer over 12 months. Demonstrated non-inferiority for testosterone suppression. Degarelix achieved faster castration (96% at day 3 vs. 0% for leuprolide) and showed improved PSA progression-free survival in patients with PSA >20 ng/mL (p=0.04). CS28 (extension study): 5-year extension demonstrating maintained efficacy and safety with long-term degarelix treatment. CS30 (cross-over study): Demonstrated that patients switching from leuprolide to degarelix achieved sustained testosterone suppression with lower FSH levels. Meta-analyses: Several meta-analyses suggest potential cardiovascular benefit of GnRH antagonists vs. agonists, though definitive evidence from prospective randomized trials is still emerging. HERO trial (relugolix): While testing a different GnRH antagonist (oral relugolix), this trial provided further evidence supporting the cardiovascular safety advantage of the antagonist mechanism.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Competitive GnRH receptor antagonism: Degarelix directly and reversibly binds to GnRH receptors on anterior pituitary gonadotroph cells, blocking the binding and action of endogenous GnRH. This immediately suppresses the release of both luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH). No testosterone flare: Unlike GnRH agonists, degarelix does not cause an initial surge of LH, FSH, and testosterone. This is particularly important in patients with metastatic prostate cancer where testosterone flare can cause acute exacerbation of symptoms including bone pain, urinary obstruction, and spinal cord compression. Rapid castration: Achieves castrate testosterone levels (<50 ng/dL) in 96% of patients within 3 days of the first injection, compared to 2-4 weeks required by GnRH agonists. Sustained suppression: Forms a subcutaneous depot at the injection site that provides slow, continuous drug release maintaining testosterone suppression for approximately 28 days per maintenance dose.",
        "limitations": [],
        "safety": [
          "Injection site reactions: Pain, erythema, swelling, induration, and nodule formation at the injection site (40%). Most reactions are mild to moderate and resolve within 1-2 weeks. More common with the loading dose. Hot flashes: 26% incidence (expected consequence of androgen deprivation). Weight gain: 11% incidence.",
          "Injection site reactions (severe): Rare cases of injection site abscess or necrosis. Long QT syndrome: Androgen deprivation therapy may prolong the QT interval. Obtain baseline and periodic ECGs. Osteoporosis: Long-term androgen deprivation increases fracture risk.",
          "Known hypersensitivity to degarelix or any excipient Women and pediatric patients (not indicated) Severe hepatic impairment (not studied)."
        ],
