        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Dihexa Chemical name N-Hexanoic-Tyr-Ile-(6)-aminohexanoic amide; PNB-0408 Molecular formula C₂₇H₄₄N₄O₅ Molecular weight 504.67 g/mol CAS number 1401708-83-5 Sequence Hexanoyl-Tyr-Ile-Ahx-NH₂ (modified oligopeptide) Length 3 amino acid residues (modified) Source Synthetic (angiotensin IV analog) Half-life Several hours (estimated; orally active) Targets Hepatocyte growth factor (HGF) / c-Met receptor Bioavailability Orally active; crosses blood-brain barrier Legal status Research compound only; not approved for therapeutic use Dihexa (also designated PNB-0408 ) is a synthetic oligopeptide drug derived from angiotensin IV that was developed at Washington State University by Joseph Harding and colleagues. It is an orally active, blood-brain barrier-permeable compound that binds to hepatocyte growth factor (HGF) with high affinity and potentiates HGF signaling through its receptor, c-Met (mesenchymal-epithelial transition factor).",
        "evidence": "Preclinical Only : All published data from animal models and in vitro systems No Clinical Trials : No registered human clinical trials as of current date Academic Research : Primarily studied at Washington State University Tool Compound : Used to investigate HGF/c-Met role in neural plasticity and cognition Patent Protected : Covered by multiple patents from WSU research group Oral Administration : Active when given orally in animal studies (key advantage) Subcutaneous : Also administered SC in some research protocols Intranasal : Explored for direct CNS delivery Animal Dosing : Typically 0.5-2 mg/kg in rat studies (oral and SC) Solubility : Soluble in DMSO, with further dilution into aqueous vehicles for in vivo use [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] [ 5 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 McCoy AT, et al. \"Evaluation of metabolically stabilized angiotensin IV analogs as procognitive/antidementia agents.\" J Pharmacol Exp Ther . 2013;344(1):141-154. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Benoist CC, et al. \"Dihexa-facilitated rescue of cognitive deficits and synaptic function in a mouse model of Alzheimer's disease.\" J Pharmacol Exp Ther .",
