        "url": "https://wikipep.org/wiki/DSIP",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia DSIP Chemical name Delta Sleep-Inducing Peptide Molecular formula C35H48N10O15 Molecular weight 848.81 g/mol CAS number 62568-57-4 Sequence Trp-Ala-Gly-Gly-Asp-Ala-Ser-Gly-Glu Length 9 amino acids Source Synthetic neuropeptide Half-life 15-30 minutes Targets Sleep regulation, stress response Bioavailability Intravenous, subcutaneous Legal status Research compound only DSIP (Delta Sleep-Inducing Peptide) is a naturally occurring nonapeptide that was first isolated from the blood of sleeping rabbits in 1977 by the Swiss research team of Schoenenberger and Monnier. This unique neuropeptide plays a crucial role in sleep regulation, stress response, and various physiological processes. DSIP is notable for inducing natural delta wave sleep patterns rather than simply acting as a sedative, making it particularly valuable for research into sleep disorders and circadian rhythm optimization.",
