        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-10",
        "summary": "DSIP (delta sleep-inducing peptide) is a naturally occurring nonapeptide first isolated from the cerebral venous blood of sleeping rabbits, associated with slow-wave (delta) EEG activity. It is found endogenously in human plasma and tissues, with levels correlated to circadian rhythm. Its gene, precursor, and receptor have never been identified, and modern reproducibility of its original sleep-inducing effects is limited. It is not FDA-approved. DSIP has the sequence Trp-Ala-Gly-Gly-Asp-Ala-Ser-Gly-Glu (WAGGDASGE), isolated and characterized by Schoenenberger and Monnier in 1977 after intraventricular infusion into rabbits produced spindle and delta EEG activity with reduced motor activity.",
        "evidence": "Original findings are from 1970s-80s animal studies (rabbit intraventricular infusion) and small, dated human investigations without modern controlled-trial standards. No gene, precursor, or receptor has been identified, and the review literature explicitly describes DSIP's physiological role as unresolved. Treat sleep-improvement claims as unproven.",
