        "url": "https://www.peptidedosage.org/peptides/dulaglutide/dosage",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-06-21",
        "summary": "Dulaglutide (Trulicity) is a long-acting glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) receptor agonist developed by Eli Lilly under the code LY2189265 for type 2 diabetes. Unlike small-peptide GLP-1 drugs, it is a recombinant fusion biologic: two modified human GLP-1(7-37) analog chains, each carrying Ala8-to-Gly, Gly22-to-Glu and Arg36-to-Gly substitutions, joined by a peptide linker to a modified human immunoglobulin G4 (IgG4) Fc fragment. This roughly 63 kDa design resists DPP-4 cleavage and exploits neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn) recycling to give an elimination half-life of about 5 days, enabling once-weekly subcutaneous injection. The standard Dulaglutide dosage starts at 0.75 mg once weekly, with a label maintenance dose of 1.5 mg and optional escalation to 3.0 mg and a maximum of 4.5 mg weekly, each step held for at least 4 weeks (AWARD-11).",
        "evidence": "Gastrointestinal events are the dominant adverse effects: nausea, diarrhea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dyspepsia and decreased appetite. They are dose-related, usually mild-to-moderate, concentrated during initiation and dose escalation, and tend to subside within 1-2 weeks of a stable dose; in AWARD-11 nausea occurred in roughly 13-16% and vomiting in 6-9% across doses [1][4].; Boxed warning: dulaglutide causes thyroid C-cell (medullary) tumors in rodents.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Dulaglutide is not a small peptide but a recombinant fusion protein of roughly 63 kDa. Each molecule contains two identical chains, each made of a modified human GLP-1(7-37) analog joined through a short peptide linker to a modified human immunoglobulin G4 (IgG4) Fc fragment [2]. The GLP-1 portion shares about 90% sequence homology with native GLP-1 and carries three engineered substitutions: Ala8-to-Gly confers resistance to dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) cleavage, while Gly22-to-Glu and Arg36-to-Gly reduce immunogenicity and improve solubility. The IgG4 Fc backbone is mutated to minimize antibody effector functions and serves two pharmacokinetic roles: its large size slows subcutaneous absorption and limits renal filtration, and its engagement of the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn) drives receptor-mediated recycling that rescues the molecule from lysosomal degradation [2].\\n\\nFunctionally, dulaglutide is a full agonist at the GLP-1 receptor.",
