        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Efruxifermin Chemical name Efruxifermin (EFX / AKR-001) Molecular formula Fc-FGF21 fusion protein Molecular weight ~80,000 g/mol (Fc fusion homodimer) CAS number Not assigned (biologic) Sequence Human IgG1 Fc domain fused to modified FGF21 (N-terminal Fc, C-terminal FGF21) Length Biologic fusion protein Source Synthetic (Akero Therapeutics) Half-life ~3-3.5 days Targets FGFR1c/β-Klotho complex (same as native FGF-21) Bioavailability Subcutaneous injection (once weekly) Legal status FDA Breakthrough Therapy Designation (NASH/MASH); Phase 2b/3 Efruxifermin ( EFX , formerly AKR-001 ) is an Fc-FGF21 fusion protein developed by Akero Therapeutics as a long-acting FGF-21 analog for the treatment of non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH/MASH). By fusing the Fc domain of human IgG1 to a modified FGF-21, efruxifermin achieves a half-life of ~3-3.5 days (vs ~1-2 hours for native FGF-21), enabling once-weekly subcutaneous dosing.",
