        "url": "https://pepuniversity.com/peptide-codex.php#eloralintide",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "Eloralintide (development code LY3841136) is a synthetic long-acting amylin analog developed by Eli Lilly as a selective amylin receptor agonist - its defining pharmacological distinction from earlier amylin-class compounds being its 12-fold preferential potency at AMY1R (amylin receptor 1) over the calcitonin receptor (CTR). Amylin receptors are heterodimeric complexes formed from the calcitonin receptor paired with one of three receptor activity-modifying proteins: RAMP1+CTR = AMY1R, RAMP2+CTR = AMY2R, RAMP3+CTR = AMY3R. Earlier amylin analogs - pramlintide (native sequence, multiple daily dosing), cagrilintide (Novo Nordisk, once-weekly), and petrelintide (Zealand/Roche) - activate both amylin receptor complexes and the calcitonin receptor with comparable potency; calcitonin receptor activation governs calcium homeostasis, bone metabolism, and is hypothesized to contribute to the tolerability limitations seen with non-selective amylin analogs.",
        "evidence": "Peptide University labels its records as aggregated from empirical research and anecdotal community data. This record is retained as supplied and is not upgraded to clinical evidence.",
        "halfLife": "",
