        "mechanism": "Eloralintide (development code LY3841136) is a synthetic long-acting amylin analog developed by Eli Lilly as a selective amylin receptor agonist - its defining pharmacological distinction from earlier amylin-class compounds being its 12-fold preferential potency at AMY1R (amylin receptor 1) over the calcitonin receptor (CTR). Amylin receptors are heterodimeric complexes formed from the calcitonin receptor paired with one of three receptor activity-modifying proteins: RAMP1+CTR = AMY1R, RAMP2+CTR = AMY2R, RAMP3+CTR = AMY3R. Earlier amylin analogs - pramlintide (native sequence, multiple daily dosing), cagrilintide (Novo Nordisk, once-weekly), and petrelintide (Zealand/Roche) - activate both amylin receptor complexes and the calcitonin receptor with comparable potency; calcitonin receptor activation governs calcium homeostasis, bone metabolism, and is hypothesized to contribute to the tolerability limitations seen with non-selective amylin analogs.",
        "limitations": [
          "Personal or family history of Medullary Thyroid Carcinoma (MTC) - the theoretical thyroid C-cell tumor risk associated with calcitonin receptor-family agonism applies to this class; although eloralintide's AMY1R selectivity substantially reduces calcitonin receptor activation relative to cagrilintide, residual AMY3R",
          "Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 2 (MEN2) - MEN2 patients have near-universal MTC predisposition; the calcitonin receptor-family class signal warrants avoidance pending definitive eloralintide-specific thyroid safety data from Phase 3.",
          "Type 1 or Type 2 diabetes - excluded from all eloralintide clinical trials to date; the amylin receptor-mediated glucagon suppression in the context of existing diabetes management (particularly insulin therapy) carries hypoglycemia risk; the glycemic effects of eloralintide in dysglycemic populations have not been",
          "Concurrent insulin therapy - the amylin class carries a precedent for severe hypoglycemia when co-administered with insulin, established by the FDA boxed warning on pramlintide (Symlin); eloralintide's amylin receptor agonism in the context of insulin-dependent subjects carries this class-level hypoglycemia risk.",
          "Concurrent insulin secretagogues (sulfonylureas, meglitinides) - increased hypoglycemia risk from the combination of enhanced glucose-dependent insulin secretion amplification and amylin receptor-mediated appetite/caloric suppression.",
          "Concurrent use of other amylin receptor agonists (cagrilintide, pramlintide, petrelintide) or CagriSema - redundant amylin receptor loading produces additive adverse effects without additional therapeutic benefit; CagriSema already contains cagrilintide as the amylin component.",
          "Pre-existing gastroparesis - while eloralintide's Phase 1 data suggests the gastric emptying delay may be transient rather than sustained, the initial gastric motility effect at cycle initiation in subjects with pre-existing gastroparesis warrants caution; this contraindication may be revised as Phase 3 gastric",
          "Chronic heart disease, hepatic impairment, or renal impairment - excluded from Phase 1 and Phase 2 trials; pharmacokinetics and safety in these populations have not been characterized."
        ],
        "safety": [
          "Nausea - the most common adverse event in Phase 2; highly dose-dependent, ranging from 11% at 1mg to 64% at 6mg (fixed dose without titration) versus 14% in placebo; the Phase 2 escalation arms (3→9mg and 6→9mg) produced lower nausea rates than the fixed 6mg and 9mg arms, confirming that gradual titration.",
          "Fatigue and asthenia - dose-dependent; 0% at 1mg to 46% at 9mg in Phase 2 versus 12% placebo; related to both central AMY1R CNS effects and the metabolic consequences of rapid weight loss; typically most pronounced in the first 4-8 weeks of each dose escalation step and attenuates with continued dosing.",
          "Decreased appetite - the intended pharmacological effect; reported as an adverse event in 19% of Phase 1 subjects (11% in Phase 1b at doses up to 12mg); reflects the appetite suppression mechanism that drives the weight loss benefit; classified as adverse only in subjects who found the degree of appetite reduction.",
          "Headache - reported in 12% of Phase 1b subjects overall; more common at higher dose levels (6mg and 12mg); mechanism unclear; typically mild and transient; attenuates with dose stabilization.",
          "Pulse rate reduction - a notable and dose-dependent pharmacodynamic effect: mean decreases from baseline of −7.1 bpm at 3mg, −8.3 bpm at 6mg, and −14.4 bpm at 12mg in Phase 1b; the mechanism reflects AMY1R-mediated cardiac chronotropic modulation; no symptomatic bradycardia was reported in Phase 1 or Phase 2 trials at.",
          "Injection site reactions - dose-dependent; injection site pain, erythema, and edema more common at 3mg and above; typically mild; the slow subcutaneous absorption profile (slow tmax, flat concentration curve) means the injection depot persists longer than short-half-life peptides, which may contribute to the injection.",
          "Diarrhea - reported in approximately 10% of Phase 1b subjects; more common at higher doses (12mg: 11.1%); typically mild.",
          "Vomiting - reported in approximately 4% of Phase 1b subjects and up to 8.3% at 12mg; less common than nausea; dose-dependent; managed by dose reduction and gradual titration.",
          "Constipation - reported at 6mg in Phase 1b; consistent with the amylin class effect on gastrointestinal motility; less pronounced than with GLP-1 receptor agonists based on the Phase 2 tolerability comparison.",
          "Abdominal pain - reported in approximately 8.3% of the 12mg Phase 1b cohort; abdominal discomfort is consistent with GI motility changes at higher doses."
        ],
