        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Endomorphin-1 Chemical name Endomorphin-1 (EM-1) Molecular formula C₃₄H₃₇N₅O₅ Molecular weight 611.7 g/mol CAS number 178306-97-1 Sequence Tyr-Pro-Trp-Phe-NH₂ (YPWF-NH₂) Length 4 amino acids (tetrapeptide amide) Source Endogenous (CNS - thalamus, hypothalamus, nucleus accumbens, PAG, spinal cord) Half-life ~1-3 minutes (rapid enzymatic degradation) Targets μ-Opioid receptor (MOR, MOP) - highest selectivity and affinity of any known endogenous peptide Bioavailability Not orally bioavailable; ICV, intrathecal, intranasal (research) Legal status Research compound (endogenous opioid peptide) Endomorphin-1 ( EM-1 , YPWF-NH₂ ) is a tetrapeptide amide that represents the most potent and selective endogenous agonist of the μ-opioid receptor (MOR) yet discovered.",
        "evidence": "Most μ-Selective Endogenous Ligand : Enables precise study of μ-receptor function without δ/κ confounds Biased Agonism : May produce analgesia with less respiratory depression and tolerance than morphine Reduced Tolerance Development : Animal studies show slower tolerance onset compared to morphine at equianalgesic doses Cardiovascular Effects : Hypotensive and bradycardic effects suggesting role in autonomic regulation Stress Response : EM-1 release increases during acute stress - may be the endogenous mediator of stress-induced analgesia Template for Drug Design : Structure serves as scaffold for developing novel μ-selective analgesics with improved stability EM-1 levels in CSF are altered in chronic pain conditions Genetic variations affecting EM-1 processing enzymes may explain individual differences in pain sensitivity EM-1/EM-2 ratio in different brain regions maps to distinct aspects of pain processing EM-1 in the nucleus accumbens contributes to natural reward processing Understanding endomorphin signaling may inform strategies to treat opioid use disorder EM-1 reward",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "μ-Opioid Receptor (MOR) Activation : Binds MOR with Ki ~0.36 nM; Gi/Go-coupled → inhibits adenylyl cyclase → decreases cAMP Ultra-High Selectivity : μ:δ:κ selectivity ratio >4000:1:1 - the most μ-selective endogenous opioid known GIRK Channel Opening : Activates G-protein-coupled inwardly rectifying K⁺ channels → neuronal hyperpolarization Calcium Channel Inhibition : Blocks voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels → reduces neurotransmitter release from nociceptive terminals Supraspinal Analgesia : Potent analgesia when administered ICV (ED50 ~0.75 nmol, comparable to morphine) Spinal Analgesia : Intrathecal EM-1 produces dose-dependent antinociception in the tail-flick and hot-plate tests Biased Agonism : EM-1 shows preferential activation of G-protein signaling over β-arrestin recruitment compared to synthetic opioids - a property associated with analgesia with reduced tolerance and respiratory depression Rapid Degradation : Dipeptidyl peptidase IV (DPPIV) cleaves the Tyr¹-Pro² bond; aminopeptidases attack N-terminal Tyr; half-life ~1-3 minutes in CSF",
