        "evidence": "While ET-1 itself is not used therapeutically, understanding its biology has been essential for: ET-1 is an indispensable research tool for studying: Vascular tone regulation Endothelial function and dysfunction Pulmonary vascular biology Cardiac hypertrophy Renal physiology ET-1 is one of the most widely used peptides in cardiovascular research: Vasoconstriction studies: Standard agonist for studying vascular smooth muscle contraction in wire myography, organ bath, and perfusion experiments Hypertrophy models: Used to induce cardiac myocyte hypertrophy in cell culture (standard in vitro model) Fibrosis research: Induces fibroblast-to-myofibroblast transition for fibrosis studies Pulmonary hypertension: ET-1 models for studying pulmonary vascular remodeling Endothelial function: Studying the balance between ET-1 (constriction) and NO (dilation) Drug development: Screening and characterization of endothelin receptor antagonists In vitro vasoconstriction: 10⁻¹⁰ to 10⁻⁷ M (typically 10⁻⁸ M for maximal contraction) Cardiac hypertrophy (cell culture): 10-100 nM for 24-48 hours In vivo:",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "ET-1 acts through two G protein-coupled receptors: ET_A Receptor: Expressed primarily on vascular smooth muscle cells Mediates vasoconstriction, cell proliferation, and fibrosis Signals through Gq/11 → PLC → IP₃/DAG → intracellular calcium release → sustained contraction Also activates RhoA/Rho kinase pathway for calcium sensitization High affinity for ET-1 > ET-2 >> ET-3 ET_B Receptor: Expressed on both endothelial cells and smooth muscle cells On endothelial cells: Mediates vasodilation through NO and prostacyclin release; clearance of circulating ET-1 On smooth muscle cells: Mediates vasoconstriction (similar to ET_A) Equal affinity for ET-1, ET-2, and ET-3",
