        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-10",
        "summary": "Epitalon is a synthetic tetrapeptide (Ala-Glu-Asp-Gly) developed in Russia as a synthetic analog of epithalamin, a bovine pineal-gland extract. It is studied in longevity research for reported telomerase activation, telomere maintenance, and circadian/melatonin regulation. Nearly all published research originates from a single Russian research group, and it has not undergone Western-standard randomized controlled trials. It is not FDA-approved. Epitalon (also spelled Epithalon) was developed by Vladimir Khavinson and colleagues at the St. Petersburg Institute of Bioregulation and Gerontology, based on the active components of epithalamin. It is a short tetrapeptide with sequence Ala-Glu-Asp-Gly (AEDG) and molecular weight ~390.4. Its most-cited mechanism is activation of telomerase (induction of hTERT expression) with reported telomere elongation in human somatic cell cultures, alongside effects on melatonin rhythm and antioxidant systems.",
        "evidence": "Over 25 years of in vitro, in vivo, and Khavinson-group human cohort research report geroprotective and telomerase effects, but the evidence is dominated by a single research group and lacks large independent Western randomized controlled trials. Treat longevity claims as unproven.",
