        "mechanism": "Telomerase Activation Epithalon's primary researched mechanism is the activation of telomerase - the enzyme responsible for adding protective DNA sequences to telomere caps. Epithalon is one of only a small number of compounds that has demonstrated telomerase activation in both in vitro cell studies and animal models. Pineal Gland Normalization Derived from epithalamin, a natural pineal gland polypeptide, epithalon has demonstrated the ability to restore melatonin secretion in aged animals, normalizing disrupted circadian rhythms. Melatonin is itself a potent antioxidant, and its restoration may account for some of epithalon's observed anti-aging effects. Neuroendocrine Regulation Research spanning over 30 years (primarily from Russian institutes) has documented epithalon's broader neuroendocrine modulation effects, including antioxidant activity, tumor incidence reduction in animal models, and improvement in physiological aging markers. Human clinical studies remain limited.",
