        "lastReviewed": "Not stated",
        "summary": "GHK-Cu (glycyl-L-histidyl-L-lysine copper) is a naturally occurring copper-binding tripeptide found in human plasma, saliva, and urine at concentrations that decline significantly with age - from approximately 200 ng/mL at age 20 to 80 ng/mL by age 60. Its mechanisms are multi-directional: GHK-Cu activates approximately 31% of human genes associated with tissue remodeling and repair, upregulates collagen and elastin synthesis, promotes antioxidant enzyme activity, modulates TGF-β to reduce fibrosis, and inhibits inflammatory cytokines. In skin research, it has demonstrated acceleration of wound healing, reduction of fine lines and wrinkles through collagen restoration, and improvement in skin laxity and firmness. In hair research, it promotes hair follicle size and has shown potential for androgenic alopecia. It also stimulates nerve outgrowth and has shown bone repair potential through increased osteoblast activity. For research and educational purposes only.",
