        "url": "https://wikipep.org/wiki/Ghrelin",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Ghrelin Chemical name Lenomorelin; Growth Hormone-Releasing Peptide (endogenous) Molecular formula C₁₄₉H₂₄₉N₄₇O₄₂ Molecular weight 3370.86 g/mol CAS number 258279-04-8 Sequence Gly-Ser-Ser(n-octanoyl)-Phe-Leu-Ser-Pro-Glu-His-Gln-Arg-Val-Gln-Gln-Arg-Lys-Glu-Ser-Lys-Lys-Pro-Pro-Ala-Lys-Leu-Gln-Pro-Arg Length 28 amino acids Source Endogenous (stomach fundus, hypothalamus) Half-life 30-60 minutes (acylated form) Targets Growth hormone secretagogue receptor 1a (GHS-R1a) Bioavailability Intravenous, subcutaneous injection Legal status Research compound; not approved for therapeutic use Ghrelin is a 28-amino acid orexigenic peptide hormone predominantly produced by enteroendocrine cells (X/A-like cells) of the gastric fundus. Discovered in 1999 by Masayasu Kojima and colleagues, ghrelin is the endogenous ligand for the growth hormone secretagogue receptor 1a (GHS-R1a) and is the only known circulating hormone that stimulates appetite.",
        "evidence": "Cachexia : Investigated for cancer-associated cachexia and anorexia Anorexia Nervosa : Studied for dysregulated ghrelin levels in eating disorders GH Deficiency Testing : Ghrelin analogs used as GH provocative test agents Gastroparesis : Ghrelin agonists (e.g., relamorelin) investigated for delayed gastric emptying Postoperative Ileus : Studied for restoring gut motility after surgery Reconstitution : Lyophilized ghrelin reconstituted in sterile water or PBS Route : Typically administered intravenously or subcutaneously in research settings IV Dosing : Research studies commonly use 1-5 mcg/kg IV bolus or infusion SC Dosing : Subcutaneous administration at various research doses Acylation Verification : Ensure the acylated (active) form is used for GHS-R1a studies Des-Acyl Ghrelin : Available separately for studies of non-GHS-R1a-mediated effects [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] [ 5 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Kojima M, et al. \"Ghrelin is a growth-hormone-releasing acylated peptide from stomach.\" Nature . 1999;402(6762):656-660. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Müller TD, et al. \"Ghrelin.\" Mol Metab . 2015;4(6):437-460.",
