        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia GHRP-4 Chemical name Growth Hormone Releasing Peptide-4 Molecular formula C₄₆H₅₆N₁₂O₆ Molecular weight ~869 Da CAS number 158861-67-7 Sequence D-Trp-D-Phe-Lys-Trp-D-Phe-Lys Length 6 amino acids Source Synthetic Half-life 15-30 minutes Targets Ghrelin receptor (GHS-R1a), hypothalamus, pituitary somatotrophs Bioavailability Subcutaneous/intramuscular Legal status Research compound (unscheduled) GHRP-4 ( Growth Hormone Releasing Peptide-4 ) is a synthetic hexapeptide growth hormone secretagogue (GHS) that stimulates the release of growth hormone (GH) from the anterior pituitary gland by activating the ghrelin receptor (GHS-R1a) . Developed as part of the GHRP peptide family pioneered by Cyril Bowers in the 1980s, GHRP-4 shares the core D-amino acid-containing hexapeptide structure common to all GHRPs but with specific substitutions that modulate its receptor binding kinetics and GH release profile.",
        "evidence": "Application Model Key Finding Reference GH release comparison Rat pituitary Intermediate potency: GHRP-2 > GHRP-4 > GHRP-6 for peak GH Bowers et al., 1991 GHRH synergy Human volunteers GHRP + GHRH produced 3-5× greater GH than either alone Bowers et al., 1990 Age-related GH decline Elderly subjects GHRPs partially restore blunted GH secretion in aging Arvat et al., 1997 Obesity effects Obese subjects Reduced GH response to GHRPs (reversible with weight loss) Cordido et al., 1993 Protocol Dose Route Frequency Notes GHRP-4 standalone 100-200 μg Subcutaneous 2-3 times daily (before meals/bedtime) Dose on empty stomach for optimal GH pulse GHRP-4 + GHRH analog 100 μg GHRP-4 + 100 μg CJC-1295 (no DAC) Subcutaneous 2-3 times daily Synergistic protocol; most effective combination Pre-sleep pulse 100-200 μg Subcutaneous Bedtime only Amplifies natural nocturnal GH surge Saturation dose ~1 μg/kg - - Higher doses do not further increase GH (plateau effect) For detailed community-tested protocols and dosing guidance, see PepDex Wiki . [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Bowers CY, et al.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "GHRP-4 stimulates GH release through both pituitary and hypothalamic pathways: GHS-R1a (Ghrelin Receptor) Activation - Binds the growth hormone secretagogue receptor 1a (GHS-R1a) on anterior pituitary somatotroph cells, triggering intracellular calcium mobilization and GH vesicle exocytosis via Gq/PLC/IP3 signaling Hypothalamic GHRH Stimulation - Activates GHS-R1a on hypothalamic neurons, promoting GHRH (growth hormone releasing hormone) release from the arcuate nucleus, which provides an additional indirect stimulus for pituitary GH secretion Somatostatin Suppression - Reduces somatostatin (growth hormone inhibiting hormone) tone from the periventricular nucleus, releasing the tonic inhibition on somatotrophs and widening the GH secretory window Synergy with GHRH - GHRP-4 + GHRH co-administration produces GH release that exceeds the sum of either agent alone (true pharmacological synergy), because GHRPs and GHRH activate different intracellular signaling cascades (Gq vs Gs) on somatotrophs Pulsatile GH Release - Amplifies the natural pulsatile GH secretory pattern rather than",
