        "sourceId": "wikipep",
        "url": "https://wikipep.org/wiki/GIP",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia GIP Chemical name Glucose-dependent Insulinotropic Polypeptide Molecular formula C₂₀₅H₃₂₅N₅₇O₆₃S Molecular weight 4983.6 g/mol CAS number 7487-76-3 Sequence YAEGTFISDYSIAMDKIHQQDFVNWLLAQKGKKNDWKHNITQ Length 42 amino acids Source Intestinal K-cells (duodenum, jejunum) Half-life 5-7 minutes (rapidly cleaved by DPP-4) Targets GIP receptor (GIPR) Bioavailability Endogenous Legal status Endogenous hormone GIP ( Glucose-dependent Insulinotropic Polypeptide , formerly known as Gastric Inhibitory Polypeptide ) is a 42-amino acid incretin hormone secreted by enteroendocrine K-cells of the duodenum and proximal jejunum in response to nutrient ingestion, particularly fats and carbohydrates. Together with GLP-1 , GIP mediates the \"incretin effect\" - the augmentation of insulin secretion in response to oral versus intravenous glucose (accounting for 50-70% of postprandial insulin release).",
        "evidence": "[ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Campbell JE, Drucker DJ. \"Pharmacology, physiology, and mechanisms of incretin hormone action.\" Cell Metab . 2013;17:819-837. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Nauck MA, Meier JJ. \"The incretin effect in healthy individuals and those with type 2 diabetes.\" J Clin Endocrinol Metab . 2016;101:778-786. ↑ 3.0 3.1 El K, Campbell JE. \"The role of GIP in alpha-cells and glucagon secretion.\" Peptides . 2020;125:170213. P Pep Dex Track saturation levels, half-life decay curves, and injection schedules for 50+ peptides. AI coaching, blood work correlation, and side effect monitoring. Includes MacroDex (4.39M foods) & FitDex (1,356 exercises). Saturation Tracking Half-Life Curves AI Coach 24hr Free Trial Try Free for 24 Hours → Retrieved from \" https://wikipep.org/index.php?title=GIP&oldid=3921 \" Categories : Peptides Incretin Hormones Endogenous Peptides Gastrointestinal Hormones GIP Add topic",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Incretin Effect : Potentiates glucose-dependent insulin secretion from pancreatic beta cells via GIPR/cAMP/PKA signaling Beta-Cell Trophic : Promotes beta-cell proliferation and survival via CREB and Akt pathways Glucagon Regulation : Stimulates glucagon secretion during hypoglycemia (glucose-dependent dual action) Fat Metabolism : Promotes lipogenesis and fatty acid uptake in adipose tissue (lipogenic hormone) Bone Metabolism : Reduces bone resorption; GIP receptor knockout mice have reduced bone mass Neuroprotection : GIPR expressed in hippocampus; potential role in cognition and neuroprotection",
