        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Glatiramer Acetate Chemical name Glatiramer acetate (Copolymer-1 / Cop-1) Molecular formula Random copolymer of L-glutamic acid, L-alanine, L-tyrosine, L-lysine Molecular weight 4,700-11,000 g/mol (average ~6,400 Da; heterogeneous mixture) CAS number 147245-92-9 Sequence Random sequence of Glu:Ala:Tyr:Lys in molar ratio 1.4:4.3:1.0:3.4 Length Variable (40-100 amino acids per chain; mixture of different lengths) Source Synthetic (random polymerization) Half-life Rapid degradation at injection site (not systemically absorbed intact) Targets MHC Class II (competitive binding); shifts Th1→Th2 immune response Bioavailability SC injection (local immune modulation) Legal status FDA Approved (1996 - relapsing multiple sclerosis) Glatiramer acetate (brand name Copaxone ) is an immunomodulatory drug approved for the treatment of relapsing forms of multiple sclerosis (MS).",
        "evidence": "[ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Johnson KP, et al. \"Copolymer 1 reduces relapse rate in relapsing-remitting MS.\" Neurology . 1995;45:1268-1276. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Khan O, et al. \"Three times weekly glatiramer acetate in RRMS (GALA).\" Ann Neurol . 2013;73:705-713. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Ford CC, et al. \"Glatiramer acetate in MS: mechanisms of action.\" Int J MS Care . 2020;22:173-182. P Pep Dex Track saturation levels, half-life decay curves, and injection schedules for 50+ peptides. AI coaching, blood work correlation, and side effect monitoring. Includes MacroDex (4.39M foods) & FitDex (1,356 exercises). Saturation Tracking Half-Life Curves AI Coach 24hr Free Trial Try Free for 24 Hours → Retrieved from \" https://wikipep.org/index.php?title=Glatiramer_Acetate&oldid=3928 \" Categories : Peptides Immunomodulators FDA Approved Multiple Sclerosis Therapeutics Glatiramer Acetate Add topic",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "MHC Class II Competition : Competes with myelin basic protein (MBP) fragments for binding to MHC Class II molecules on antigen-presenting cells → reduces presentation of myelin autoantigens Th1 → Th2 Immune Shift : Induces anti-inflammatory Th2 cells (secreting IL-4, IL-10, TGF-β) that cross-react with myelin antigens → bystander suppression of autoimmune attack Regulatory T Cell Induction : Expands CD4⁺CD25⁺FoxP3⁺ regulatory T cells → enhanced immune tolerance Monocyte/Macrophage Modulation : Shifts monocytes from M1 (pro-inflammatory) to M2 (anti-inflammatory) phenotype Neuroprotection : Glatiramer-reactive T cells secrete brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) in the CNS → neuroprotection independent of anti-inflammatory effects B Cell Modulation : Reduces pro-inflammatory B cells; increases IL-10-producing regulatory B cells No Immunosuppression : Does not deplete lymphocytes or suppress general immunity - fundamentally different from most MS drugs",
