        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia GLP-2 Chemical name Glucagon-Like Peptide-2 Molecular formula C₁₅₅H₂₄₉N₄₃O₄₆S Molecular weight 3765.5 g/mol CAS number 125441-19-6 Sequence HADGSFSDEMNTILDNLAARDFINWLIQTKITD Length 33 amino acids Source Intestinal L-cells (proglucagon processing) Half-life ~7 minutes Targets GLP-2 receptor Bioavailability Endogenous (rapidly degraded by DPP-4) Legal status Endogenous hormone Glucagon-Like Peptide-2 ( GLP-2 ) is a 33-amino acid peptide hormone produced by intestinal L-cells through post-translational processing of proglucagon by prohormone convertase 1/3. It is the primary intestinotrophic hormone in humans, uniquely responsible for stimulating intestinal mucosal growth, enhancing nutrient absorption, and maintaining gut barrier integrity. GLP-2 is co-secreted with GLP-1 in response to nutrient ingestion, particularly fats and carbohydrates.",
        "evidence": "Biomarker of intestinal L-cell function and enteroendocrine adaptation Tool compound for studying intestinal adaptation and mucosal biology Target for combination therapies in intestinal failure [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Drucker DJ, et al. \"Regulation of the biological activity of glucagon-like peptide 2 in vivo by dipeptidyl peptidase IV.\" Nat Biotechnol . 1997;15:673-677. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Jeppesen PB, et al. \"Glucagon-like peptide 2 improves nutrient absorption and nutritional status in short-bowel patients with no colon.\" Gastroenterology . 2001;120:806-815. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Brubaker PL. \"Glucagon-like peptide-2 and the regulation of intestinal growth and function.\" Compr Physiol . 2018;8:1185-1210. ↑ Drucker DJ. \"The Discovery of GLP-2 and Development of Teduglutide for Short Bowel Syndrome.\" ACS Pharmacol Transl Sci . 2019;2:6-12. P Pep Dex Track saturation levels, half-life decay curves, and injection schedules for 50+ peptides. AI coaching, blood work correlation, and side effect monitoring. Includes MacroDex (4.39M foods) & FitDex (1,356 exercises).",
