        "sourceId": "pep-university",
        "url": "https://pepuniversity.com/peptide-codex.php#gonadorelin",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "Gonadorelin is synthetic gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) - a decapeptide (pGlu-His-Trp-Ser-Tyr-Gly-Leu-Arg-Pro-Gly-NH₂) with an identical amino acid sequence to endogenous human GnRH, making it the only compound in Category VII that acts at the apex of the HPG axis: the hypothalamic level. Endogenous GnRH is synthesized in GnRH neurons of the arcuate nucleus and preoptic area and released in discrete pulses - typically every 60-120 minutes in men - into the hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal blood supply to reach GnRH receptors (GnRHR) on anterior pituitary gonadotroph cells. Each GnRH pulse triggers a corresponding LH pulse (and to a lesser extent FSH), which then drives Leydig cell testosterone synthesis in a pulsatile pattern that underlies the normal diurnal testosterone rhythm.",
        "evidence": "Peptide University labels its records as aggregated from empirical research and anecdotal community data. This record is retained as supplied and is not upgraded to clinical evidence.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Gonadorelin is synthetic gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) - a decapeptide (pGlu-His-Trp-Ser-Tyr-Gly-Leu-Arg-Pro-Gly-NH₂) with an identical amino acid sequence to endogenous human GnRH, making it the only compound in Category VII that acts at the apex of the HPG axis: the hypothalamic level. Endogenous GnRH is synthesized in GnRH neurons of the arcuate nucleus and preoptic area and released in discrete pulses - typically every 60-120 minutes in men - into the hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal blood supply to reach GnRH receptors (GnRHR) on anterior pituitary gonadotroph cells. Each GnRH pulse triggers a corresponding LH pulse (and to a lesser extent FSH), which then drives Leydig cell testosterone synthesis in a pulsatile pattern that underlies the normal diurnal testosterone rhythm.",
        "limitations": [
          "Pituitary adenoma - gonadorelin acts directly on pituitary gonadotroph cells; a pituitary tumor may respond abnormally to GnRH stimulation, potentially growing or producing uncontrolled gonadotropin secretion; in GnRH-responsive pituitary adenomas, gonadorelin is absolutely contraindicated.",
          "Continuous or high-frequency administration protocol (non-pulsatile use) - this is a use-pattern contraindication rather than a patient characteristic: continuous gonadorelin administration triggers GnRHR desensitization and paradoxical HPG suppression; any protocol that does not maintain adequate inter-dose intervals",
          "History of hypersensitivity to gonadorelin, synthetic GnRH, or formulation excipients.",
          "Hormone-dependent tumors (prostate cancer, estrogen receptor-positive breast cancer, LH/FSH-responsive malignancies) - gonadorelin-driven LH/FSH and sex hormone restoration is contraindicated in any hormone-sensitive malignancy.",
          "Polycystic ovarian syndrome (PCOS) in women being treated for anovulation - gonadorelin-driven LH hypersecretion could worsen the LH/FSH ratio dysregulation that characterizes PCOS.",
          "Pregnancy - GnRH receptor activation during pregnancy has unpredictable effects on luteal support and placental function.",
          "Ovarian hyperstimulation risk in women - FSH stimulation from gonadorelin-driven pituitary activation in PCOS or high-responder states can produce OHSS.",
          "Severe GnRH receptor mutations (Kallmann syndrome variant with receptor-null phenotype) - gonadorelin will be ineffective in subjects with complete GnRHR loss-of-function mutations."
        ],
        "safety": [
          "HPG axis suppression from incorrect dosing - the most serious and mechanistically important adverse effect; non-pulsatile, continuous, or excessively frequent administration converts gonadorelin from an HPG stimulator to an HPG suppressor through GnRHR desensitization; clinical manifestation is worsening hypogonadism.",
          "Headache - commonly reported after GnRH pulse injections; related to the rapid LH/FSH surge and downstream testosterone pulse produced by each dose.",
          "Nausea - mild; transient; reported in a subset of subjects particularly at higher doses.",
          "Injection site reactions - mild redness, tenderness, and bruising at the subcutaneous administration site; more frequent with the 3x-daily injection schedule.",
          "Flushing - transient; from the acute hormonal pulse produced by each gonadorelin injection.",
          "Mood and libido fluctuations - from the pulsatile testosterone oscillations between doses; subjects may experience mood variability correlated with the inter-dose hormonal troughs.",
          "Antibody formation against gonadorelin (rare) - repeated GnRH peptide injection can rarely trigger low-level immune sensitization; clinically significant antibodies neutralizing gonadorelin's activity are rare but documented with prolonged exposure.",
          "Ovarian hyperstimulation (women) - FSH pulsatility from gonadorelin-driven pituitary activation in high-responder women creates OHSS risk comparable to exogenous FSH administration.",
          "Testicular sensitivity and ache - from the increased Leydig and Sertoli cell stimulation from LH/FSH pulses."
        ],
