        "summary": "Gotratix (also sold as Muscle Cytomax and labeled A-18) is an oral skeletal-muscle peptide bioregulator from the Khavinson \"Cytomax\" family, developed within Vladimir Khavinson's peptide-regulation program at the St. Petersburg Institute of Bioregulation and Gerontology. Its active ingredient is peptide complex A-18, a low-molecular-weight peptide fraction extracted from the triceps muscle of young calves (under 11 months). Like other Khavinson bioregulators, it is hypothesized to act not as a hormone but as a tissue-specific signal that enters the cell nucleus and modulates expression of muscle genes governing protein synthesis, satellite-cell activity, and mitochondrial function [1][2]. Manufacturers market it to support muscle recovery, reduce fatigue under heavy training, and counter age-related muscle loss, though no peer-reviewed human trials of Gotratix itself have been published.",
