        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia HCG (Human Chorionic Gonadotropin) Chemical name Choriogonadotropin Molecular formula Glycoprotein (α and β subunits) Molecular weight ~36,700 g/mol CAS number 9002-61-3 Sequence Alpha subunit: 92 amino acids, Beta subunit: 145 amino acids Length 237 amino acids total Source Pregnant women's urine or recombinant Half-life 24-36 hours Targets LH/hCG receptors (LHCGR) Bioavailability Subcutaneous, intramuscular injection Legal status Prescription only (FDA approved) HCG (Human Chorionic Gonadotropin) is a glycoprotein hormone naturally produced during pregnancy by the placenta, but has extensive medical applications in reproductive medicine and hormone therapy. Structurally similar to luteinizing hormone (LH), HCG binds to the same receptors and mimics LH's action, making it invaluable for treating hypogonadism, maintaining testicular function during testosterone replacement therapy (TRT), and preserving fertility in men.",
