        "evidence": "Master Iron Regulator : Controls dietary iron absorption, macrophage iron recycling, and hepatocyte iron storage Disease Biomarker : Hepcidin levels diagnostic/prognostic for iron disorders, anemia of inflammation, and chronic kidney disease Therapeutic Target : Both hepcidin agonists (for iron overload) and antagonists (for anemia of inflammation) are in clinical development Evolutionary Conserved : Present in all vertebrates from zebrafish to humans, underscoring fundamental importance Connects Iron to Immunity : Explains the ancient link between infection, inflammation, and iron metabolism Biomarker : Serum hepcidin measurement (ELISA/mass spectrometry) used in iron disorder diagnosis, CKD anemia management, and athletic monitoring Nutritional Immunity : Understanding how pathogens evade hepcidin-mediated iron restriction (siderophore systems) Cancer : Tumor-derived hepcidin may contribute to cancer-associated anemia; ferroptosis (iron-dependent cell death) connects to hepcidin biology COVID-19 : Hyperferritinemia and elevated hepcidin observed in severe COVID-19; may contribute",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Hepcidin functions as the key hormonal regulator of iron homeostasis through a remarkably elegant mechanism: Ferroportin Binding : Hepcidin binds to ferroportin on the cell surface of enterocytes, macrophages, and hepatocytes - the cells responsible for dietary iron absorption, iron recycling from senescent red blood cells, and iron storage, respectively Ferroportin Degradation : Hepcidin-ferroportin binding triggers Janus kinase 2 (JAK2) phosphorylation of ferroportin → ubiquitination → endocytosis → lysosomal degradation Iron Sequestration : Loss of cell-surface ferroportin traps iron inside cells, reducing serum iron levels within hours Feedback Regulation (Iron) : Elevated serum/liver iron → BMP6/BMP2 secretion → SMAD signaling in hepatocytes → increased hepcidin transcription via BMP-SMAD pathway Inflammation Induction : IL-6 (and other cytokines) → JAK-STAT3 pathway → rapid hepcidin upregulation; this is the molecular basis of anemia of chronic disease/inflammation Erythropoietic Suppression : Erythroferrone (ERFE), produced by erythroid precursors during active",
