        "sourceId": "pep-university",
        "url": "https://pepuniversity.com/peptide-codex.php#hgh-191aa",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "HGH 191AA (somatropin) is bio-identical human growth hormone - a 191 amino acid single-chain polypeptide with a molecular weight of approximately 22 kDa, structurally identical to the growth hormone produced by the anterior pituitary gland. The \"191AA\" designation distinguishes it from obsolete 192AA formulations (which contained an extra methionine residue that increased immunogenicity) and confirms structural identity with endogenous GH. Unlike all GH secretagogues in this database (CJC-1295, ipamorelin, GHRP-2, sermorelin, etc.) which stimulate the pituitary to produce more of its own GH, HGH 191AA directly replaces or supplements endogenous GH without requiring pituitary participation. It binds directly to GH receptors in the liver, adipose tissue, muscle, and bone to produce its downstream effects, including the stimulation of IGF-1 production from the liver - the primary mediator of most of HGH's anabolic effects.",
        "evidence": "Peptide University labels its records as aggregated from empirical research and anecdotal community data. This record is retained as supplied and is not upgraded to clinical evidence.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "HGH 191AA (somatropin) is bio-identical human growth hormone - a 191 amino acid single-chain polypeptide with a molecular weight of approximately 22 kDa, structurally identical to the growth hormone produced by the anterior pituitary gland. The \"191AA\" designation distinguishes it from obsolete 192AA formulations (which contained an extra methionine residue that increased immunogenicity) and confirms structural identity with endogenous GH. Unlike all GH secretagogues in this database (CJC-1295, ipamorelin, GHRP-2, sermorelin, etc.) which stimulate the pituitary to produce more of its own GH, HGH 191AA directly replaces or supplements endogenous GH without requiring pituitary participation. It binds directly to GH receptors in the liver, adipose tissue, muscle, and bone to produce its downstream effects, including the stimulation of IGF-1 production from the liver - the primary mediator of most of HGH's anabolic effects.",
        "limitations": [
          "Active cancer or evidence of neoplastic activity - exogenous GH and IGF-1 directly accelerates tumor cell proliferation; GH should not be initiated or continued if active malignancy is detected.",
          "Intracranial lesions that are not fully inactive and treated - antitumor therapy must be complete prior to GH initiation.",
          "Insulin resistance or pre-existing type 2 diabetes - HGH is diabetogenic at therapeutic doses; progressively worsens insulin sensitivity; fasting glucose must be monitored throughout.",
          "Diabetic ketoacidosis.",
          "Proliferative or severe non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy - GH/IGF-1 elevation worsens retinal vasculopathy.",
          "Concurrent use with CJC-1295 No DAC, CJC-1295 DAC, ipamorelin, tesamorelin, GHRP-2, GHRP-6, or sermorelin - redundant and counterproductive GH axis stimulation; secretagogues stimulate a pituitary that exogenous HGH has already suppressed.",
          "Acute critical illness - increased mortality observed in critically ill patients (open heart surgery, abdominal surgery, multiple trauma, acute respiratory failure) receiving high-dose GH; do not use in ICU or perioperative settings.",
          "Pregnancy - limited human data; use only if clearly needed; associated with fetal risks in animal studies."
        ],
