        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia HGH Fragment 176-191 Chemical name Human Growth Hormone Fragment 176-191 Molecular formula C₇₈H₁₂₅N₂₃O₂₃S₂ Molecular weight 1817.12 g/mol CAS number 66004-57-7 Sequence Tyr-Leu-Arg-Ile-Val-Gln-Cys-Arg-Ser-Val-Glu-Gly-Ser-Cys-Gly-Phe Length 16 amino acids Source Synthetic (C-terminal fragment of human GH) Half-life 30-60 minutes Targets Fat cell receptors (lipolytic domain of GH) Bioavailability Subcutaneous injection Legal status Research compound only; not approved for therapeutic use HGH Fragment 176-191 (also known as AOD-9604 in its modified form) is a synthetic 16-amino acid peptide corresponding to the C-terminal region (residues 176-191) of human growth hormone (hGH). This fragment represents the specific portion of the GH molecule responsible for its fat-mobilizing (lipolytic) activity, isolated from the full 191-amino acid GH protein.",
        "evidence": "Obesity Research : Investigated as a fat-loss agent with improved safety over GH AOD-9604 Variant : A stabilized analog (with Tyr replaced by a modified residue) entered clinical trials Anti-Obesity Drug Development : Phase II/III clinical trials conducted for obesity TGA Approval : AOD-9604 received Generally Recognized as Safe (GRAS) status from FDA as a food ingredient Metabolic Research : Used to study the lipolytic domain of growth hormone in isolation Typical Range : Research protocols commonly use 250-500 mcg per administration Frequency : Typically 1-2 times daily in research settings Timing : Often administered fasting or before physical activity Route : Subcutaneous injection in abdominal area preferred Cycle Length : Research protocols often span 4-12 weeks [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] [ 5 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Ng FM, et al. \"Metabolic studies of a synthetic lipolytic domain (AOD9604) of human growth hormone.\" Horm Res . 2000;53(6):274-278. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Heffernan M, et al.",
