        "lastReviewed": "Not stated",
        "summary": "Humanin is a 21-amino acid mitochondrial-derived peptide first discovered in 2001 from the brain tissue of an Alzheimer's disease patient - researchers noticed that specific brain cells were surviving in an otherwise degenerating environment and traced the protective signal to Humanin, encoded within the 16S rRNA region of the mitochondrial genome. Along with MOTS-c and SS-31, Humanin forms the core trio of mitochondrial-targeted research peptides. Its primary mechanism is cytoprotection: Humanin works by blocking pro-apoptotic proteins - particularly Bax - at the mitochondrial membrane, preventing the release of cytochrome c that triggers programmed cell death. It also reduces beta-amyloid toxicity in neurons.",
