        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Inhibin A Chemical name Inhibin Alpha-Beta A Heterodimer Molecular formula Protein heterodimer Molecular weight ~32,000 Da (heterodimer) CAS number 57285-09-3 Sequence α subunit + βA subunit (disulfide-linked) Length α: 134 aa (mature) + βA: 116 aa (mature) Source Endogenous (granulosa cells, Sertoli cells) Half-life 30-60 minutes (circulatory) Targets Activin type II receptors, anterior pituitary gonadotrophs Bioavailability Endogenous hormone (not administered exogenously) Legal status Endogenous hormone / diagnostic reagent Inhibin A is an endogenous glycoprotein hormone belonging to the transforming growth factor-beta (TGF-β) superfamily . It is a heterodimer composed of an α subunit (18 kDa) disulfide-linked to a βA subunit (14 kDa), distinguishing it from Inhibin B which pairs the same α subunit with a βB subunit.",
        "evidence": "Population Phase/Condition Inhibin A Range Premenopausal women Early follicular <20 pg/mL Premenopausal women Mid-luteal (peak) 30-100 pg/mL Postmenopausal women - <5 pg/mL Males - <2 pg/mL (Inhibin B is predominant) Pregnancy (15-20 wk) Normal Variable by gestational age (MoM-based) Pregnancy (15-20 wk) Down syndrome ~2× normal MoM [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Groome NP et al. (1996). \"Measurement of dimeric inhibin B throughout the human menstrual cycle.\" J Clin Endocrinol Metab . 81(4):1401-5. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Muttukrishna S et al. (2005). \"Inhibin, activin and follistatin in pregnancy and early pregnancy loss.\" Hum Reprod Update . 11(3):309-22. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Lambert-Messerlian GM et al. (1998). \"Second trimester levels of maternal serum inhibin A in pregnancies affected by fetal Down syndrome.\" Prenatal Diagnosis . 18(10):1061-7. ↑ Robertson DM et al. (2012). \"Inhibins and activins in blood: predictors of quality of ovarian function.\" Hum Reprod Update . 18(5):543-62. P Pep Dex Track saturation levels, half-life decay curves, and injection schedules for 50+ peptides.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Inhibin A exerts its biological effects primarily by antagonizing activin signaling: FSH Suppression - Binds to activin type II receptors (ActRII/ActRIIB) on anterior pituitary gonadotrophs with the assistance of co-receptor betaglycan (TGF-β type III receptor), competitively blocking activin-mediated FSH synthesis and secretion Betaglycan Co-receptor Requirement - Unlike activins, inhibins require betaglycan to achieve high-affinity binding to ActRII, which explains tissue-specific effects (betaglycan expression determines inhibin responsiveness) Smad Pathway Antagonism - By competing with activin for ActRII binding, Inhibin A prevents activin-induced Smad2/3 phosphorylation and nuclear translocation, blocking FSH-β gene transcription Selective FSH Regulation - Suppresses FSH secretion without directly affecting LH release, creating the precise hormonal milieu needed for single dominant follicle selection Autocrine/Paracrine Ovarian Function - Within the ovary, modulates granulosa cell proliferation, steroidogenesis, and follicular atresia through local TGF-β superfamily signaling",
        "limitations": [],
        "safety": [
          "Endogenous hormone - not administered therapeutically; no exogenous safety profile applicable Assay interference: Heterophilic antibodies can cause false-positive results; current two-site immunoassays minimize this risk Clinical significance of abnormal levels should always be interpreted in context of gestational."
        ],
