        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "KPV (Lys-Pro-Val) is the C-terminal tripeptide fragment of alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone (α-MSH), specifically representing residues 11-13 of the 13-amino-acid parent peptide. Alpha-MSH is a neuropeptide produced in the hypothalamus and pituitary that mediates a broad spectrum of anti-inflammatory, antipyretic, and homeostatic functions through melanocortin receptors (MC1R, MC3R, MC4R, MC5R). KPV retains the full anti-inflammatory activity of α-MSH but notably lacks the melanotropic (pigmentation-stimulating) N-terminal acetyl group - meaning it produces no melanin increase or tanning effect that characterizes the full parent molecule or compounds like Melanotan-II. The primary mechanism is inhibition of NF-kB (Nuclear Factor kappa-light-chain-enhancer of activated B cells), the master transcription factor of inflammatory gene expression.",
