        "url": "https://pepuniversity.com/peptide-codex.php#l-carnitine",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "L-Carnitine is an endogenous quaternary ammonium compound (derived from lysine and methionine) that functions as the essential carrier for long-chain fatty acids (LCFAs) across the inner mitochondrial membrane. Without L-Carnitine, long-chain fatty acids - the primary fuel substrate for aerobic metabolism in skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle, and the liver - cannot enter the mitochondrial matrix for beta-oxidation. The mechanism is mediated by two transport proteins: carnitine palmitoyltransferase I (CPT-I) on the outer mitochondrial membrane converts fatty acyl-CoA to fatty acyl-carnitine, which then crosses to the inner membrane; carnitine palmitoyltransferase II (CPT-II) on the inner membrane reconverts the fatty acyl-carnitine back to fatty acyl-CoA inside the matrix, where beta-oxidation generates acetyl-CoA for the TCA cycle and ultimately ATP production.",
        "evidence": "Peptide University labels its records as aggregated from empirical research and anecdotal community data. This record is retained as supplied and is not upgraded to clinical evidence.",
        "halfLife": "",
