        "url": "https://peptidehub.bio/peptides/larazotide",
        "lastReviewed": "Not stated",
        "summary": "Larazotide acetate (AT-1001) is a synthetic 8-amino acid peptide derived from Vibrio cholerae zona occludens toxin (ZOT) that acts as a tight junction regulator - the most specific peptide tool available for intestinal barrier research. Larazotide's mechanism is precise: it blocks the action of zonulin - the endogenous protein that triggers tight junction disassembly and increases intestinal permeability ('leaky gut') - by competitively inhibiting zonulin receptor binding on intestinal epithelial cells. Unlike compounds that repair existing tissue damage, Larazotide acts at the structural barrier level, preventing the paracellular leakage of antigens, pathogens, and toxins that drives gut-systemic inflammation. Phase 2 clinical trials demonstrated significant reduction in intestinal permeability markers, anti-gliadin antibody levels, and gastrointestinal symptoms in celiac disease patients. Separate trials in non-celiac IBD populations showed favorable safety and symptom improvement.",
