        "mechanism": "Zonulin Pathway Antagonism : Competitively inhibits zonulin binding to its receptor → prevents myosin light chain kinase (MLCK) activation → preserves tight junction integrity Tight Junction Stabilization : Prevents disassembly of claudin, occludin, and ZO-1 proteins at the tight junction complex Paracellular Barrier : Reduces paracellular permeability to gluten-derived gliadin peptides (33-mer and other immunogenic fragments) Local GI Action : Acts entirely within the intestinal lumen - does not require systemic absorption for efficacy Anti-Inflammatory (Indirect) : By preventing antigen translocation, reduces downstream T-cell activation and inflammatory cytokine cascades in the lamina propria Protects Against Gluten Exposure : Does not degrade or neutralize gluten - instead prevents gluten peptides from crossing the epithelial barrier Zonulin Background : Zonulin (pre-haptoglobin 2) is the only known physiological regulator of intestinal tight junctions; elevated in celiac disease, T1DM, and IBD",
