        "mechanism": "LEAP2 modulates ghrelin signaling through multiple mechanisms: GHS-R1a Antagonism - Binds the ghrelin receptor (GHS-R1a) and blocks ghrelin-stimulated receptor activation, preventing downstream Gq/PLC/IP3/calcium signaling that drives appetite, GH secretion, and reward circuit activation GHS-R1a Inverse Agonism - Beyond simple antagonism, LEAP2 suppresses the constitutive (ligand-independent) activity of GHS-R1a, which maintains basal signaling tone; this inverse agonism produces greater suppression than a neutral antagonist Competitive Binding - Competes with acyl-ghrelin for the orthosteric binding site on GHS-R1a; the relative concentrations of ghrelin and LEAP2 determine net receptor activity (hormonal seesaw) Fed/Fasted Reciprocal Regulation - In the fed state: ↑LEAP2, ↓ghrelin → suppressed appetite, reduced GH; in fasting: ↓LEAP2, ↑ghrelin → activated appetite, increased GH mobilization Hepatic/Intestinal Expression - Produced by hepatocytes (regulated by insulin, glucose, and nutritional status) and small intestinal epithelial cells, providing both liver-derived and",
