        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Leu-Enkephalin Chemical name Leucine-Enkephalin (Leu-Enk, YGGFL) Molecular formula C₂₈H₃₇N₅O₇ Molecular weight 555.6 g/mol CAS number 58822-25-6 Sequence Tyr-Gly-Gly-Phe-Leu (YGGFL) Length 5 amino acids Source Endogenous (CNS, adrenal medulla, GI tract; derived from proenkephalin) Half-life ~1-2 minutes (rapid degradation by enkephalinase/aminopeptidases) Targets δ-opioid receptor (DOR, primary); μ-opioid receptor (MOR, secondary) Bioavailability Not orally bioavailable (rapid enzymatic degradation); ICV, intrathecal (research) Legal status Research compound (endogenous opioid peptide) Leucine-enkephalin ( Leu-enkephalin , YGGFL ) is one of the two endogenous pentapeptide opioids (the other being methionine-enkephalin, YGGFM ) discovered in 1975 by John Hughes and Hans Kosterlitz in the pig brain - a landmark finding that revealed the existence of the brain's own morphine-like substances.",
        "evidence": "Endogenous Analgesia : Modulates pain at spinal and supraspinal levels; activated during stress, exercise, and acupuncture Mood Regulation : δ-opioid pathway contributes to anxiolytic and antidepressant effects (distinct from μ-mediated euphoria) GI Motility : Inhibits intestinal motility and secretion; enkephalinergic neurons are abundant in the myenteric plexus Immune Modulation : Expressed by immune cells (T cells, macrophages); modulates inflammatory responses Cardioprotection : δ-opioid receptor activation produces ischemic preconditioning effects in cardiac tissue Reward/Reinforcement : Participates in natural reward circuitry (food, social bonding, exercise) DOR agonists (SNC80, KNT-127) produce anxiolytic and antidepressant effects in animal models DOR activation may not produce the respiratory depression, constipation, or abuse potential associated with μ-selective opioids δ-opioid pathway implicated in neuroprotection during cerebral ischemia and cardiac ischemia Enkephalin levels measured as biomarkers of endogenous pain modulation Genetic polymorphisms in PENK gene",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "δ-Opioid Receptor (DOR) Activation : Primary target; Gi/Go-coupled → inhibits adenylyl cyclase → decreases cAMP → reduces neuronal excitability μ-Opioid Receptor (MOR) Activation : Secondary target; same Gi/Go coupling → potent analgesia, euphoria, respiratory depression (at high occupancy) Potassium Channel Opening : DOR activation opens G-protein-coupled inwardly rectifying potassium channels (GIRKs), hyperpolarizing neurons Calcium Channel Inhibition : Blocks N-type and P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels, reducing neurotransmitter release Presynaptic Inhibition : Reduces GABA, glutamate, and substance P release from pain-transmitting neurons Descending Pain Modulation : Activates descending inhibitory pathways from periaqueductal gray (PAG) and rostral ventromedial medulla (RVM) Rapid Inactivation : Neprilysin (enkephalinase, NEP) and aminopeptidase N rapidly cleave Leu-enkephalin, limiting its sphere of action to the immediate synaptic vicinity",
