        "mechanism": "Linaclotide acts through the GC-C signaling pathway on intestinal epithelium: GC-C Receptor Binding - Binds guanylate cyclase-C receptors on the apical (luminal) surface of intestinal epithelial cells, stimulating intracellular cyclic GMP (cGMP) production via the receptor's intrinsic guanylate cyclase catalytic domain CFTR-Mediated Chloride Secretion - cGMP activates cGMP-dependent protein kinase II (cGKII/PKGII), which phosphorylates and opens the cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) chloride channel, driving chloride secretion into the intestinal lumen Fluid Secretion - Chloride secretion creates an osmotic gradient that draws sodium and water into the lumen, softening stool and accelerating intestinal transit Bicarbonate Secretion - CFTR-mediated bicarbonate secretion raises luminal pH, improving the mucosal microenvironment and mucus hydration Visceral Analgesia (cGMP Efflux) - A fraction of intracellular cGMP is exported to the extracellular (serosal) side of the epithelium, where it acts on colonic nociceptive afferent neurons (primary afferents",
        "limitations": [],
        "safety": [
          "Diarrhea (most common): 20% IBS-C, 16% CIC - most common adverse event and reason for discontinuation (~4.5%); typically occurs in first 2 weeks and may improve Abdominal pain: 7% (distinct from IBS symptom improvement) Flatulence: 4-6% Abdominal distension: 2-3% Minimal systemic effects - negligible absorption means."
        ],
