        "sourceId": "peptidedosage",
        "url": "https://www.peptidedosage.org/peptides/liraglutide/dosage",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-06-21",
        "summary": "Liraglutide is a once-daily glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) receptor agonist developed by Novo Nordisk under the code NN2211 and marketed as Victoza for type 2 diabetes and Saxenda for chronic weight management. It is an acylated analogue of native human GLP-1 sharing 97% sequence homology, modified with an Arg34-to-Lys substitution and a 16-carbon palmitoyl fatty-acid chain at Lys26 that drives reversible albumin binding and extends the half-life to roughly 13 hours, enabling daily subcutaneous dosing. The standard Liraglutide dosage for weight management titrates weekly from 0.6 mg to a 3.0 mg/day maintenance ceiling over five weeks; for glycemic control the Victoza ceiling is lower at 1.2 to a maximum 1.8 mg/day. Mechanistically it enhances glucose-dependent insulin secretion, suppresses glucagon, slows gastric emptying, and acts on hypothalamic appetite centers to reduce energy intake.",
        "evidence": "Gastrointestinal effects are the most common adverse events: in SCALE roughly 39% of liraglutide 3.0 mg recipients reported nausea, about 15% vomiting, with diarrhea, constipation, dyspepsia and decreased appetite also frequent. Most were mild-to-moderate, peaked during the 4-5 week titration, and improved over time [1][7].; Symptoms are minimized by the slow weekly +0.6 mg escalation; manufacturers advise delaying a dose increase by an extra week (or stepping back down) if a titration step is not tolerated, and maintaining hydration to offset fluid losses [7].; Liraglutide carries an FDA boxed warning for thyroid C-cell tumors based on rodent carcinogenicity studies; it is contraindicated in patients with a personal or family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC) or multiple endocrine neoplasia syndrome type 2 (MEN 2). A causal link in humans has not been established [7][8].; Acute pancreatitis, including fatal and non-fatal hemorrhagic or necrotizing cases, has been reported post-marketing.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Liraglutide is a 97%-homologous analogue of native human glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1 7-37). Two engineering changes convert the rapidly degraded native hormone - whose half-life is only about 1-2 minutes - into a once-daily drug: an Arg34→Lys substitution and attachment of a 16-carbon palmitoyl fatty-acid chain to Lys26 through a glutamic-acid spacer. The fatty-acid tail promotes self-association into heptamers at the injection depot and reversible, high-affinity binding to serum albumin (>98%), which together slow absorption and shield the peptide from dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) and neutral endopeptidase degradation [6][9].\\n\\nAs a GLP-1 receptor agonist, liraglutide reproduces the incretin effect. At pancreatic beta cells it stimulates glucose-dependent insulin secretion and suppresses inappropriate glucagon release, lowering blood glucose with low intrinsic hypoglycemia risk because the effect attenuates as glucose normalizes [3][9]. In the gut it slows gastric emptying, blunting post-prandial glucose excursions.",
        "limitations": [
          "Liraglutide is a once-daily glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) receptor agonist developed by Novo Nordisk under the code NN2211 and marketed as Victoza for type 2 diabetes and Saxenda for chronic weight management."
        ],
