        "sourceId": "pep-university",
        "url": "https://pepuniversity.com/peptide-codex.php#liraglutide",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "Liraglutide is the first-generation, once-daily GLP-1 receptor agonist - the compound that proved the entire incretin class was viable for serious weight management before semaglutide existed. It shares 97% structural homology with native human GLP-1 and uses a C16 fatty acid chain to bind albumin in the bloodstream, which slows its clearance and extends its effective half-life to approximately 13 hours. That half-life is why it requires daily injections rather than weekly - it simply does not stay in circulation long enough to sustain receptor activation across a full week. Mechanistically, it works identically to semaglutide: glucose-dependent insulin stimulation, glucagon suppression, delayed gastric emptying, and central appetite suppression via hypothalamic GLP-1 receptors. The difference is purely pharmacokinetic - daily dosing creates a different plasma curve and tolerability dynamic than weekly dosing.",
