        "sourceId": "pep-university",
        "url": "https://pepuniversity.com/peptide-codex.php#livagen",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "Livagen is a synthetic tetrapeptide (Lys-Glu-Asp-Ala) Khavinson bioregulator derived from liver tissue polypeptide extracts with a uniquely dual mechanism - acting simultaneously on hepatic parenchymal cells (hepatocytes) through the standard organ-specific bioregulator pathway, and on chromatin structure and epigenetic gene regulation at the nuclear level, representing the most fundamentally genomic of all Khavinson bioregulators. The chromatin-level mechanism is what makes Livagen distinctively different from all other bioregulators in this database: Livagen has been demonstrated in the Khavinson research to interact directly with nucleosomal chromatin structure - binding to histone proteins and DNA-histone complexes to influence the compaction state of chromatin in somatic cell nuclei.",
        "evidence": "Peptide University labels its records as aggregated from empirical research and anecdotal community data. This record is retained as supplied and is not upgraded to clinical evidence.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Livagen is a synthetic tetrapeptide (Lys-Glu-Asp-Ala) Khavinson bioregulator derived from liver tissue polypeptide extracts with a uniquely dual mechanism - acting simultaneously on hepatic parenchymal cells (hepatocytes) through the standard organ-specific bioregulator pathway, and on chromatin structure and epigenetic gene regulation at the nuclear level, representing the most fundamentally genomic of all Khavinson bioregulators. The chromatin-level mechanism is what makes Livagen distinctively different from all other bioregulators in this database: Livagen has been demonstrated in the Khavinson research to interact directly with nucleosomal chromatin structure - binding to histone proteins and DNA-histone complexes to influence the compaction state of chromatin in somatic cell nuclei. Aged cells accumulate heterochromatin at specific genomic loci - inappropriate silencing of genes that should remain active in youthful tissue - and this epigenetic silencing contributes directly to the cellular senescence phenotype.",
        "limitations": [
          "Active hepatocellular carcinoma or hepatic malignancy - Livagen's chromatin-decondensing activity and hepatocyte gene program normalization in malignant hepatocytes has not been evaluated; the epigenetic mechanisms underlying cancer involve both hypermethylation silencing of tumor suppressors AND hypomethylation of",
          "Active viral hepatitis with significant liver fibrosis (Metavir F3-F4) - the combination of active viral hepatic injury and chromatin bioregulator activity in severely fibrotic liver has not been characterized.",
          "Decompensated cirrhosis - insufficient viable hepatocyte mass for meaningful hepatic bioregulator response; the coagulopathy and portal hypertension of decompensated cirrhosis create an unfavorable context.",
          "Active systemic DNA damaging chemotherapy - the chromatin decondensation mechanism of Livagen could theoretically interfere with chemotherapy mechanisms that depend on chromatin accessibility for DNA-intercalating drugs or topoisomerase inhibitors.",
          "Known hypersensitivity to Livagen (Lys-Glu-Asp-Ala tetrapeptide) or formulation excipients.",
          "Pregnancy - chromatin-level gene regulatory modulation during embryonic and fetal development - when epigenetic gene programming is at its most active and tightly regulated - has not been evaluated and carries theoretical risk.",
          "Concurrent HDAC inhibitor therapy (vorinostat, romidepsin, valproic acid at chromatin-modifying doses) - the chromatin-modifying mechanisms of Livagen and HDAC inhibitors act on the same epigenetic regulatory machinery and could produce additive or unpredictable chromatin state changes.",
          "Active hematological malignancy - the chromatin-level gene regulatory activity could have unpredictable effects on malignant hematopoietic cell epigenetic programs."
        ],
