        "mechanism": "¹⁷⁷Lu-DOTATATE delivers targeted radiation to SSTR2-expressing tumor cells: SSTR2 High-Affinity Binding - The octreotate targeting vector binds SSTR2 (Kd ~0.2 nM), which is overexpressed 10-100-fold on well-differentiated NET cells compared to normal tissues Receptor-Mediated Internalization - After SSTR2 binding, the radiolabeled peptide is internalized into tumor cells via clathrin-mediated endocytosis, concentrating the therapeutic payload within the tumor β-Particle Radiation - ¹⁷⁷Lu decays with emission of β-particles (Emax 497 keV, average 133 keV) with a maximum tissue range of ~2 mm, delivering cytotoxic radiation to the tumor cell and immediate neighbors (crossfire effect) DNA Double-Strand Breaks - β-radiation induces DNA double-strand breaks in tumor cells, overwhelming repair mechanisms and triggering apoptosis, cell cycle arrest, and mitotic catastrophe Crossfire Effect - The 2 mm β-particle range irradiates neighboring tumor cells that may not have directly internalized the radiopharmaceutical, improving efficacy in heterogeneous tumors Gamma Emission for Imaging -",
        "limitations": [],
        "safety": [
          "Hematologic (most significant): Grade 3-4 lymphopenia (9%), thrombocytopenia (2%), anemia (3%); usually recoverable; rare myelodysplastic syndrome/AML (~2% at 5 years) Nausea/vomiting: Common during infusion (managed with ondansetron and amino acid infusion-related) Nephrotoxicity: Managed with amino acid co-infusion."
        ],
