        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Magainin-2 Chemical name Magainin 2 (Magainin II) Molecular formula C₁₁₉H₂₀₁N₃₃O₃₂S₁ Molecular weight 2467.9 g/mol CAS number 108433-53-0 Sequence GIGKFLHSAKKFGKAFVGEIMNS (23 aa; amphipathic α-helix; cationic +3) Length 23 amino acids (the prototypical amphipathic antimicrobial peptide from frog skin) Source Skin secretions of African clawed frog (Xenopus laevis); discovered by Michael Zasloff (1987) Half-life ~30-60 minutes (rapid proteolytic degradation in vivo; synthetic analogs more stable) Targets Bacterial membrane disruption via toroidal pore model; broad-spectrum antimicrobial Bioavailability IV, topical (research); poor systemic bioavailability due to rapid degradation Legal status Research compound (natural amphibian antimicrobial peptide; parent compound for Pexiganan ) Magainin 2 is a 23-amino acid antimicrobial peptide isolated from the skin of the African clawed frog ( Xenopus laevis ) by Michael Zasloff at the NIH in 1987.",
        "evidence": "[ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Zasloff M. \"Magainins, a Class of Antimicrobial Peptides from Xenopus Skin: Isolation, Characterization of Two Active Forms, and Partial cDNA Sequence of a Precursor.\" Proc Natl Acad Sci . 1987;84:5449-5453. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Matsuzaki K, et al. \"An Antimicrobial Peptide, Magainin 2, Induced Rapid Flip-Flop of Phospholipids Coupled with Pore Formation and Peptide Translocation.\" Biochemistry . 1996;35:11361-11368. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Ludtke SJ, et al. \"Membrane Pores Induced by Magainin.\" Biochemistry . 1996;35:13723-13728. ↑ Zasloff M. \"Antimicrobial Peptides of Multicellular Organisms.\" Nature . 2002;415:389-395. P Pep Dex Track saturation levels, half-life decay curves, and injection schedules for 50+ peptides. AI coaching, blood work correlation, and side effect monitoring. Includes MacroDex (4.39M foods) & FitDex (1,356 exercises).",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Carpet/Toroidal Pore Model : Magainin-2 initially binds parallel to the membrane surface (\"carpet model\") → at threshold concentration, reorients and inserts perpendicular to the membrane → forms toroidal pores where peptide and lipid headgroups line the pore → membrane disruption and lysis Selectivity : Amphipathic helix preferentially interacts with anionic bacterial membranes (phosphatidylglycerol, cardiolipin) over zwitterionic mammalian membranes (phosphatidylcholine, cholesterol) - basis for selective toxicity Broad-Spectrum : Active against Gram-positive, Gram-negative bacteria, fungi, and protozoa Anti-Cancer : Also selectively cytotoxic to cancer cells (which have increased anionic surface phospholipids) - potential anticancer application Resistance-Resistant : Fundamental difficulty of evolving resistance to membrane-disrupting mechanism - would require wholesale membrane lipid composition changes",
