        "mechanism": "Maritide employs a unique dual mechanism through its bispecific architecture: GIP Receptor Antagonism - The fully human anti-GIPR monoclonal antibody component binds and blocks GIP receptor signaling in adipose tissue, pancreatic islets, and brain; GIP normally promotes lipogenesis in adipocytes, and blocking this may reduce fat storage and enhance lipolysis GLP-1 Receptor Agonism - Two covalently conjugated GLP-1 analog peptides activate GLP-1R on pancreatic β-cells (insulin secretion), hypothalamic appetite centers (satiety), and gastric smooth muscle (delayed emptying) GIP Controversy Resolution - The GIP field is divided: Eli Lilly's tirzepatide ACTIVATES GIPR for weight loss, while Amgen's maritide BLOCKS it.",
