        "mechanism": "Maropitant blocks the substance P/NK1 receptor signaling axis: NK1 Receptor Antagonism - Competitively binds the neurokinin-1 (tachykinin NK1) receptor with high affinity (Ki ~0.1 nM in canine brain), preventing endogenous substance P from activating emetic and nociceptive pathways Central Antiemetic Action - Blocks NK1 receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ/area postrema) and nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS), which are key relay stations in the emetic reflex arc; substance P is the primary neurotransmitter mediating the final common pathway of emesis Peripheral Antiemetic Action - Also blocks NK1 receptors on vagal afferent nerve terminals in the GI tract, reducing peripheral emetic signaling from the gut to the brainstem Visceral Antinociception - NK1 receptor blockade in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord and visceral afferent pathways reduces substance P-mediated visceral pain transmission; contributes to analgesia for abdominal/GI pain Anti-inflammatory Component - Substance P promotes neurogenic inflammation; NK1 blockade reduces inflammatory mediator release, edema,",
