        "mechanism": "Matrixyl exerts its anti-ageing effects through a sophisticated biomimicry mechanism that leverages natural skin signalling pathways: Matrikine SignallingThe core mechanism of Matrixyl is matrikine mimicry. Matrikines are peptide fragments produced when extracellular matrix proteins ( collagen , elastin, fibronectin) are degraded by matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs). These fragments serve as signals to fibroblasts, indicating that matrix repair is needed. The KTTKS sequence in Matrixyl corresponds to a specific region of Type I procollagen. When applied to skin, Matrixyl delivers this matrikine signal to dermal fibroblasts, triggering the collagen synthesis response without requiring actual matrix degradation. TGF-β Pathway ActivationResearch indicates that Matrixyl activates the TGF-β (Transforming Growth Factor -beta) signalling pathway.",
