        "sourceId": "wikipep",
        "url": "https://wikipep.org/wiki/Matrixyl",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Matrixyl (Palmitoyl Pentapeptide-4) Chemical name Palmitoyl-Lys-Thr-Thr-Lys-Ser Molecular formula C₃₂H₅₈N₆O₉ Molecular weight 802.93 g/mol CAS number 214047-00-4 Sequence Pal-Lys-Thr-Thr-Lys-Ser-OH (Pal-KTTKS) Length 5 amino acids (with palmitoyl lipid chain) Source Synthetic Half-life 6-12 hours (topical) Targets TGF-β signaling / extracellular matrix (ECM) remodeling Bioavailability Topical (enhanced by palmitoyl lipid anchor) Legal status Cosmetic ingredient (unregulated as drug) Matrixyl , known chemically as Palmitoyl Pentapeptide-4 (Pal-KTTKS), is a synthetic lipopeptide widely employed in cosmetic and dermatological formulations for its collagen-stimulating and anti-aging properties. Developed by Sederma (now part of Croda International), Matrixyl was introduced to the cosmetic market in the early 2000s and has since become one of the most extensively studied and commercially successful peptide ingredients in the skincare industry.",
        "evidence": "Katayama et al. (1993) first identified that the KTTKS sequence from type I procollagen C-propeptide acts as a matrikine to stimulate new collagen production. Subsequent studies confirmed dose-dependent increases in type I collagen, type III collagen, and fibronectin production by cultured fibroblasts treated with Pal-KTTKS. Robinson et al. (2005) published a double-blind, placebo-controlled study demonstrating statistically significant improvements in wrinkle parameters with topical Pal-KTTKS at 3 ppm after 12 weeks of twice-daily application, as measured by digital image analysis and replica profilometry. Microarray analyses have shown that Matrixyl treatment modulates the expression of over 1,500 genes in human skin fibroblasts, with particularly strong upregulation of genes involved in ECM synthesis, wound healing, and cell proliferation.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Matrixyl exerts its anti-aging effects primarily through stimulation of extracellular matrix (ECM) production in the dermal layer of the skin. The proposed mechanism involves several interconnected pathways:",
        "limitations": [],
        "safety": [
          "Matrixyl has an excellent safety record as a cosmetic ingredient: Skin irritation: Very low potential; well-tolerated by sensitive skin types, including rosacea-prone skin Allergic reactions: Extremely rare; no significant sensitization reported in extensive patch testing programs Phototoxicity: None demonstrated."
        ],
