        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "Mazdutide (also known as IBI362) is a dual GLP-1 and glucagon receptor agonist - the world's first compound of this specific class to receive regulatory approval, having been approved in China in 2024 for weight management. Like all GLP-1 agonists, it suppresses appetite, slows gastric emptying, and drives glucose-dependent insulin release. What the glucagon receptor component adds is a distinct thermogenic effect: glucagon receptor activation directly stimulates the liver to oxidize fat, raises basal energy expenditure, and - critically - drives substantial reduction of liver fat content independent of caloric restriction alone. The two receptors work in complementary opposition: GLP-1 prevents the blood sugar spike that isolated glucagon signaling would cause, while glucagon provides the metabolic rate boost and hepatic fat clearing that GLP-1 alone cannot achieve. Mazdutide's most remarkable clinical finding is its effect on liver fat.",
