        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-10",
        "summary": "Mazdutide is a long-acting synthetic oxyntomodulin analog and dual agonist of the GLP-1 and glucagon receptors, co-developed by Innovent Biologics and Eli Lilly. It is approved as a prescription drug in China for weight management but remains investigational in the United States, with no FDA approval as of this review. Mazdutide (IBI362 / LY3305677) is a synthetic peptide analog of oxyntomodulin, a naturally occurring gut hormone with dual activity at the glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor (GLP-1R) and glucagon receptor (GCGR). Its peptide backbone incorporates a fatty-acyl modification that extends its in vivo half-life for once-weekly dosing. In published phase 3 Chinese trials, GLORY-1 (NEJM 2025) reported -12.55% body weight at 32 weeks with the 6 mg dose, and GLORY-2 (JAMA 2026) evaluated a 9 mg dose. A head-to-head phase 3 trial versus semaglutide 1 mg in early type 2 diabetes with obesity has also been conducted.",
