        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-10",
        "summary": "Melanotan II is a synthetic, non-selective melanocortin receptor agonist developed at the University of Arizona as a tanning agent. It activates MC1R, MC3R, MC4R, and MC5R with comparable affinity, producing skin pigmentation and, as an incidental finding in early trials, erectile/libido effects. It is not FDA-approved or approved by any regulatory agency for any use, and carries documented safety concerns distinct from its FDA-approved, more selective derivative bremelanotide (PT-141). Melanotan II (Ac-Nle-cyclo[Asp-His-D-Phe-Arg-Trp-Lys]-NH2) is a cyclic heptapeptide analog of alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone (alpha-MSH), originally developed as a potential skin-cancer-prevention strategy by inducing protective tanning without UV exposure. Unlike native alpha-MSH, which preferentially activates MC1R on melanocytes, MT-2 is non-selective and activates MC1R, MC3R, MC4R, and MC5R with broadly comparable affinity.",
        "evidence": "Small human pilot studies in the 1990s-2000s confirmed tanning and erectile effects, but also documented dose-limiting nausea and flushing, and the compound was not advanced to approval due to its non-selective receptor profile and safety findings. Its more selective successor, bremelanotide (PT-141), was developed instead and gained FDA approval for a specific indication. Published case reports document serious adverse events with self-administered MT-2 use, outside any regulated clinical trial setting.",
