        "evidence": "Activates muscle satellite cells (muscle stem cells) Promotes local muscle repair following damage Supports muscle hypertrophy through hyperplasia May accelerate recovery from training Neuroprotective properties in research Local tissue-specific effects Complements systemic IGF-1 signaling May reduce muscle scarring after injury MGF research spans exercise physiology, muscle biology, and regenerative medicine, with consistent findings regarding its role in muscle adaptation and repair. Human studies have confirmed MGF expression increases in skeletal muscle following exercise, particularly following damaging eccentric contractions. A 2003 Journal of Physiology study showed MGF mRNA increased substantially in muscle biopsies taken after resistance exercise, with peak expression within hours of exercise and declining over 24-48 hours. This temporal pattern suggested MGF initiates the early repair response.",
