        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Mifamurtide Chemical name Muramyl Tripeptide Phosphatidylethanolamine (MTP-PE) Molecular formula C₅₉H₁₀₉N₆O₁₉P Molecular weight 1,237.5 Da CAS number 83461-56-7 Sequence MurNAc-Ala-D-Glu-dipalmitoyl-PE Length Muramyl dipeptide conjugate Source Synthetic (muramyl dipeptide analog) Half-life 2-6 hours (liposomal formulation) Targets NOD2 receptor, macrophage/monocyte activation Bioavailability IV (liposomal formulation) Legal status EMA-approved (EU); not FDA-approved Mifamurtide (trade name Mepact ) is a synthetic analog of muramyl dipeptide (MDP), a minimal immunostimulatory component of bacterial cell wall peptidoglycan, conjugated with dipalmitoyl phosphatidylethanolamine (DPPE) to enable incorporation into liposomes. It is the only approved immunomodulatory agent specifically indicated for the adjuvant treatment of non-metastatic, resectable osteosarcoma in children, adolescents, and young adults after complete surgical resection.",
        "evidence": "[ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Meyers PA et al. (2005). \"Osteosarcoma: a randomized, prospective trial of the addition of ifosfamide and/or muramyl tripeptide to cisplatin, doxorubicin, and high-dose methotrexate.\" J Clin Oncol . 23(9):2004-11. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Meyers PA et al. (2008). \"Osteosarcoma: the addition of muramyl tripeptide to chemotherapy improves overall survival.\" J Clin Oncol . 26(4):633-8. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Nardin A et al. (2006). \"Liposomal muramyl tripeptide phosphatidylethanolamine: targeting and activating macrophages for adjuvant treatment of osteosarcoma.\" Curr Cancer Drug Targets . 6(2):123-33. ↑ Anderson PM (2016). \"Mifamurtide in osteosarcoma - a practical review.\" Drugs Today . 46(5):327-37. P Pep Dex Track saturation levels, half-life decay curves, and injection schedules for 50+ peptides. AI coaching, blood work correlation, and side effect monitoring. Includes MacroDex (4.39M foods) & FitDex (1,356 exercises).",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Mifamurtide activates the innate immune system through a well-characterized pathway: NOD2 Receptor Activation - After liposomal delivery and macrophage phagocytosis, MTP-PE is released intracellularly and binds NOD2, a cytoplasmic pattern recognition receptor that normally detects muramyl dipeptide from bacterial peptidoglycan NF-κB Signaling - NOD2 engagement activates RIP2 kinase → NF-κB transcription factor, driving expression of pro-inflammatory genes including TNF-α, IL-1β, IL-6, and IL-8 Macrophage Tumoricidal Activation - Transforms resting monocytes/macrophages into a tumoricidal phenotype (M1-polarized) capable of direct cytotoxicity against tumor cells through reactive oxygen species (ROS), reactive nitrogen species, and cytokine-mediated killing TNF-α and IL-1β Production - These cytokines exert direct anti-tumor effects (TNF-α-induced tumor cell apoptosis) and recruit additional immune effectors to tumor sites Dendritic Cell Maturation - Enhances antigen presentation and cross-priming of tumor-specific T cells, potentially generating adaptive anti-tumor immunity",
        "limitations": [],
        "safety": [
          "Mifamurtide has a well-characterized safety profile from clinical trials and post-marketing surveillance: Very common (>10%): Fever/chills (acute cytokine release, typically first 1-2 infusions), headache, tachycardia, nausea/vomiting, fatigue Common (1-10%): Myalgia, arthralgia, hypertension, dyspnea, constipation."
        ],
