        "halfLifeContext": "PepCodex dossier summary. Route and species must be checked in the cited pharmacokinetic paper.",
        "mechanism": "MK-0616 is an oral macrocyclic peptide that inhibits PCSK9 (proprotein convertase subtilisin/kexin type 9), a protein that degrades LDL receptors on liver cells. By blocking PCSK9, MK-0616 increases the number of LDL receptors available to clear LDL cholesterol from the blood. Unlike existing injectable PCSK9 inhibitors (evolocumab, alirocumab), MK-0616 achieves this through oral administration - a significant advance for patient adherence. Scientific Pathways PCSK9 Inhibition: MK-0616 binds to PCSK9 in the circulation, preventing it from binding to and degrading hepatic LDL receptors. This increases LDL receptor density on hepatocytes, enhancing LDL-C clearance from plasma. Macrocyclic Peptide Technology: The compound uses a constrained macrocyclic peptide scaffold that provides oral bioavailability - typically a major challenge for peptide therapeutics. This technology platform could enable oral delivery of other peptide drugs.",
