        "url": "https://wikipep.org/wiki/Motilin",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Motilin Chemical name Motilin Molecular formula C₁₁₅H₁₇₅N₃₃O₃₅S Molecular weight 2698.1 g/mol CAS number 52906-92-0 Sequence FVPIFTYGELQRMQEKERNKGQ (human, 22 aa) Length 22 amino acids Source Endogenous (Mo cells of duodenal and jejunal mucosa) Half-life ~4-5 minutes Targets Motilin receptor (GPR38/MLNR) - Gq-coupled GPCR → smooth muscle contraction Bioavailability IV (research); oral motilin agonists in development Legal status Research compound (endogenous hormone; erythromycin acts as motilin agonist) Motilin is a 22-amino acid peptide hormone produced by enteroendocrine Mo cells in the duodenal and jejunal mucosa. It is the primary hormonal regulator of the migrating motor complex (MMC) - the cyclical pattern of gastrointestinal motility that sweeps undigested material, bacteria, and cellular debris through the small intestine during fasting periods (the \"interdigestive housekeeper\"). Motilin was discovered by Brown et al.",
        "evidence": "Prokinetic Target : Motilin receptor agonism is a validated strategy for gastroparesis treatment GI Housekeeping : MMC prevents small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO) by sweeping bacteria distally Erythromycin Bridge : Low-dose erythromycin (motilin agonist) is an established off-label gastroparesis treatment Drug Development Target : Motilides (non-antibiotic motilin agonists) avoid antibiotic resistance concerns Biomarker Potential : Altered motilin cycling in gastroparesis, SIBO, and functional dyspepsia [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] [ 5 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Brown JC, et al. \"A Gastric Motor Stimulating Substance from the Mucosa of the Small Intestine.\" Can J Physiol Pharmacol . 1966;44:475-480. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Sanger GJ, Furness JB. \"Ghrelin and Motilin Receptors as Drug Targets for Gastrointestinal Disorders.\" Nat Rev Gastroenterol Hepatol . 2016;13(1):38-48. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Itoh Z. \"Motilin and Clinical Application.\" Peptides . 1997;18(4):593-608. ↑ Broad J, et al. \"Motilin Receptors.\" In: Handbook of Experimental Pharmacology . 2016;239:55-88. ↑ Tack J, et al.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Motilin Receptor Activation : Motilin binds to MLNR (GPR38) on smooth muscle cells and enteric neurons → Gq-coupled → phospholipase C → IP3 → intracellular Ca²⁺ release → smooth muscle contraction MMC Phase III Initiation : Triggers powerful propagating contractions beginning in the gastric antrum and sweeping through the small intestine at ~5-10 cm/min Gastric Emptying : Accelerates gastric emptying of solids and liquids (basis for prokinetic use) Lower Esophageal Sphincter : Increases LES pressure (may protect against reflux during MMC) Gallbladder Contraction : Stimulates gallbladder motility during fasting Enteric Nervous System : Acts on both myenteric and submucosal plexus neurons; cholinergic pathways amplify motor response Cyclical Release : Motilin released in pulses every ~90-120 min during fasting; immediately suppressed by food intake",
