        "sourceId": "wikipep",
        "url": "https://wikipep.org/wiki/Motixafortide",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Motixafortide Chemical name BL-8040 / BKT140 Molecular formula C₁₀₂H₁₅₅N₃₃O₂₂S₂ Molecular weight ~2,300 Da CAS number 664334-36-5 Sequence Cyclic 14-amino acid peptide (4F-benzoyl-TN14003 derivative) Length 14 amino acids (cyclic, modified) Source Synthetic Half-life 6-12 hours Targets CXCR4 (CXC chemokine receptor 4) Bioavailability High (subcutaneous) Legal status FDA-approved (2023, Aphexda - stem cell mobilization) Motixafortide (trade name Aphexda , development codes BL-8040 and BKT140 ) is a synthetic cyclic 14-amino acid peptide that acts as a high-affinity CXCR4 antagonist . It was developed by BioLineRx and received FDA approval in September 2023 for use in combination with filgrastim (G-CSF) for the mobilization of hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) to the peripheral blood for collection and subsequent autologous transplantation in patients with multiple myeloma .",
        "evidence": "[ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Chabannon C et al. (2023). \"Motixafortide and G-CSF for stem cell mobilization in multiple myeloma: GENESIS Phase 3 trial.\" Blood . ↑ 2.0 2.1 Abraham M et al. (2007). \"The CXCR4 inhibitor BL-8040 induces apoptosis and inhibits proliferation in AML cells.\" Leukemia . 21(7):1549-54. ↑ 3.0 3.1 BioLineRx (2023). \"Aphexda Prescribing Information.\" FDA. ↑ Borthakur G et al. (2021). \"BL-8040 CXCR4 antagonist in combination with nelarabine for relapsed/refractory T-ALL.\" Blood Adv . P Pep Dex Track saturation levels, half-life decay curves, and injection schedules for 50+ peptides. AI coaching, blood work correlation, and side effect monitoring. Includes MacroDex (4.39M foods) & FitDex (1,356 exercises). Saturation Tracking Half-Life Curves AI Coach 24hr Free Trial Try Free for 24 Hours → Retrieved from \" https://wikipep.org/index.php?title=Motixafortide&oldid=3993 \" Categories : Peptides Approved Drugs CXCR4 Antagonists Hematology Synthetic Peptides Motixafortide Add topic",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Motixafortide disrupts CXCR4-mediated signaling through multiple mechanisms: CXCR4 Antagonism - Binds with high affinity (IC₅₀ ~1 nM) to the transmembrane chemokine receptor CXCR4, competitively blocking its natural ligand CXCL12 (SDF-1α) from binding and activating the receptor Stem Cell Mobilization - CXCL12 produced by bone marrow stromal cells normally retains HSCs in the marrow niche through CXCR4-mediated adhesion; blocking this interaction releases HSCs into peripheral blood, peaking at 4-6 hours post-injection Rapid-Onset Mobilization - Produces a burst of stem cell release that is faster and often larger than G-CSF alone, with synergistic effects when combined with G-CSF CXCR4 Internalization - Unlike plerixafor (the other approved CXCR4 antagonist), motixafortide induces CXCR4 receptor internalization and degradation, producing longer-lasting target inhibition Anti-tumor Immune Effects - CXCR4 blockade sensitizes tumors to immune attack by: (a) mobilizing effector T cells from bone marrow to tumor, (b) reducing CXCR4+ regulatory T cell and MDSC infiltration in tumors, (c)",
        "limitations": [],
        "safety": [
          "Most common (≥20%): Injection site reactions (65%, mostly mild erythema/pain), musculoskeletal pain (20%), headache (18%), GI symptoms (nausea, diarrhea ~15%) Injection site reactions: Characteristically include erythema, pruritus, warmth at the injection site; predominantly Grade 1-2; self-limiting within 24 hours."
        ],
