        "mechanism": "Mitochondrial-to-Nuclear Signaling Under metabolic stress, MOTS-c translocates from mitochondria to the cell nucleus, where it binds stress-response transcription factors NRF2 and ATF1. This retrograde signaling allows mitochondria to directly communicate their metabolic state to the genome. AMPK Activation MOTS-c's primary signaling output is AMPK activation - the master metabolic switch triggered by low energy states like exercise or fasting. AMPK drives glucose uptake, fatty acid oxidation, and mitochondrial biogenesis, mimicking the cellular effects of intense exercise. Pancreatic Protection A 2025 Nature study demonstrated that MOTS-c prevents pancreatic islet cell senescence - cellular aging that impairs insulin production. This finding positions MOTS-c as a potential target for diabetes prevention research, complementing its established metabolic roles.",
