        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia N-Acetyl Selank Amidate Chemical name N-Acetyl-Thr-Lys-Pro-Arg-Pro-Gly-Pro-amide Molecular formula C₃₅H₅₉N₁₁O₁₀ Molecular weight 793.92 g/mol CAS number 2212313-10-6 Sequence Ac-Thr-Lys-Pro-Arg-Pro-Gly-Pro-NH₂ Length 7 amino acids Source Synthetic (modified tuftsin-PGP analog) Half-life 4-24 hours (enhanced vs. parent Selank) Targets GABAergic system, BDNF, serotonin (5-HT), enkephalinase Bioavailability Intranasal (enhanced stability vs. Selank) Legal status Research chemical (not FDA-approved; Selank parent is approved in Russia) N-Acetyl Selank Amidate is a synthetic heptapeptide and a structurally enhanced analog of Selank , an anxiolytic and nootropic peptide developed at the Institute of Molecular Genetics of the Russian Academy of Sciences. Selank is based on the endogenous immunomodulatory tetrapeptide tuftsin (Thr-Lys-Pro-Arg) with a C-terminal Pro-Gly-Pro (PGP) extension that improves metabolic stability and biological activity.",
        "evidence": "N-Acetyl Selank Amidate is used in research settings to investigate: GABAergic and enkephalinergic mechanisms of anxiolysis Non-benzodiazepine approaches to anxiety treatment Peptide stability optimization through terminal modifications Intranasal delivery of neuropeptides Immune-neuro interactions Zozulya et al. (2008) reported that intranasal Selank (0.15%) significantly reduced anxiety scores (Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale) in patients with generalized anxiety disorder, with efficacy comparable to medazepam (a benzodiazepine) but without sedation or cognitive impairment. Kost et al. (2007) demonstrated that Selank modulates the expression of genes related to the immune response, inflammation, and neurotransmitter metabolism in human blood cells, providing molecular-level evidence for its multi-target mechanism. Research has demonstrated that Selank normalizes dysregulated cytokine profiles in both animal models and human subjects, supporting its dual anxiolytic-immunomodulatory mechanism. [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] [ 5 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Zozulya AA, Kost NV, Sokolov OY, et al. (2008).",
