        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) is a critical coenzyme found in every living cell that serves as the essential electron carrier for cellular respiration and the primary substrate for a family of NAD+-consuming enzymes that regulate DNA repair, inflammation, and longevity. In its oxidized form (NAD+), it accepts electrons from metabolic substrates in glycolysis and the TCA cycle, becoming NADH, which then delivers electrons to Complex I of the mitochondrial electron transport chain to drive ATP synthesis.",
