        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Neuropeptides are small protein-like molecules (typically 3-100 amino acids) used by neurons to communicate with each other. Unlike classical neurotransmitters (glutamate, GABA, dopamine), neuropeptides are synthesized in the cell body, packaged into dense-core vesicles, and released at higher stimulation frequencies. They act as neuromodulators - adjusting the sensitivity and responsiveness of neural circuits rather than directly opening ion channels - and play critical roles in pain, appetite, stress, sleep, mood, and cognition. This article serves as a hub linking to all neuropeptide articles on WikiPep.",
