        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Neuropeptide S Chemical name Neuropeptide S (NPS) Molecular formula C₉₃H₁₄₇N₂₃O₂₅S Molecular weight 2055.4 g/mol CAS number Not assigned Sequence SFRNGVGTGMKKTSFQRAKS (human, 20 aa) Length 20 amino acids Source Endogenous (brainstem - parabrachial nucleus, locus coeruleus, Kölliker-Fuse nucleus) Half-life ~5-10 minutes (in vivo, rapid proteolytic degradation) Targets Neuropeptide S receptor (NPSR1/GPR154) - Gq/Gs-coupled GPCR Bioavailability ICV, intranasal (research only; no systemic formulation) Legal status Research compound (not approved for clinical use) Neuropeptide S ( NPS ) is a highly conserved 20-amino acid neuropeptide that selectively activates the G-protein coupled receptor NPSR1 (formerly designated GPR154 or GPRA).",
        "evidence": "Unique Pharmacological Profile : Simultaneous anxiolytic + arousal-promoting effects - no other known compound reliably produces both Fear Extinction Enhancement : Could complement exposure therapy for PTSD and phobias Non-Sedating Anxiolytic Target : Potential for anxiolytics without the sedation, cognitive impairment, or abuse potential of benzodiazepines Memory Enhancement : Could address cognitive deficits in anxiety disorders, PTSD, and age-related memory decline Insomnia Target : NPSR1 antagonists could promote sleep without morning sedation Metabolic Benefits : Appetite suppression and weight reduction effects Genetic Validation : GWAS data linking NPSR1 variants to panic disorder provides human genetic evidence supporting the target [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] [ 5 ] [ 6 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Xu YL, et al. \"Neuropeptide S: A Neuropeptide Promoting Arousal and Anxiolytic-Like Effects.\" Neuron . 2004;43(4):487-497. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Reinscheid RK. \"Neuropeptide S: Anatomy, Pharmacology, Genetics and Physiological Functions.\" Results Probl Cell Differ . 2008;46:145-158. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Okamura N, et al.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "NPS signals through NPSR1, a class A GPCR with dual Gq and Gs coupling that produces broad neuromodulatory effects: NPSR1 Activation (Gq pathway) : NPS binding activates phospholipase C → IP3 production → intracellular Ca²⁺ release from ER stores; primary signaling pathway NPSR1 Activation (Gs pathway) : Concurrent activation of adenylyl cyclase → cAMP elevation → PKA activation; amplifies downstream signaling Anxiolytic Effects : NPS reduces anxiety-related behavior through amygdala modulation; decreases CRF (corticotropin-releasing factor) signaling; promotes extinction of conditioned fear Arousal Promotion : Activates wake-promoting circuits; increases c-Fos in orexin/hypocretin neurons; suppresses NREM and REM sleep Memory Enhancement : Facilitates long-term memory consolidation via hippocampal mechanisms; enhances extinction learning (relevant to PTSD therapy) Appetite Suppression : Reduces food intake and body weight in animal models; may interact with melanocortin and orexin systems Respiratory Regulation : Modulates breathing patterns; NPSR1 variants linked to asthma",
