        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Neurotensin Chemical name Neurotensin (NT) Molecular formula C₇₈H₁₂₁N₂₁O₂₀ Molecular weight 1672.9 g/mol CAS number 39379-15-2 Sequence pGlu-Leu-Tyr-Glu-Asn-Lys-Pro-Arg-Arg-Pro-Tyr-Ile-Leu (13 aa) Length 13 amino acids Source Endogenous (N cells of small intestinal mucosa; CNS - hypothalamus, amygdala, nucleus accumbens, VTA) Half-life ~1-3 minutes (metalloendopeptidase degradation) Targets NTS1 (Gq/Gi-coupled, high affinity), NTS2 (Gq-coupled), NTS3/sortilin (sorting receptor) Bioavailability IV (clinical); ICV, intrathecal (research); no oral bioavailability Legal status Research compound (endogenous neuropeptide) Neurotensin ( NT ) is a 13-amino acid neuropeptide first isolated from bovine hypothalamus by Carraway and Leeman in 1973.",
        "evidence": "Non-Opioid Analgesics : NT analogs (PD149163, JMV431) produce analgesia without respiratory depression, tolerance, or addiction potential - major unmet medical need Schizophrenia : Neurotensin deficits in CSF of schizophrenic patients; NTS1 agonists may complement antipsychotic therapy Obesity/Metabolic Syndrome : Pro-NT as cardiovascular/metabolic risk biomarker; NT pathway modulation for metabolic disease Cancer : NTS1 overexpressed in colorectal, pancreatic, prostate, and lung cancers; NTS1 antagonists as potential anti-cancer agents Addiction : Neurotensin modulates dopamine reward circuits; potential for treating substance use disorders [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Carraway R, Leeman SE. \"The Isolation of a New Hypotensive Peptide, Neurotensin, from Bovine Hypothalami.\" J Biol Chem . 1973;248:6854-6861. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Mustain WC, et al. \"Neurotensin: Physiology, Role in Cancer Biology, and Potential Clinical Applications.\" In: Advances in Pharmacology . 2019;85:275-300. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Melander O, et al.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "NTS1 Receptor Activation : High-affinity binding (Kd ~0.1-0.3 nM); Gq-coupled → PLC → Ca²⁺ → diverse downstream effects depending on cell type Dopamine Modulation : In VTA/nucleus accumbens, neurotensin inhibits dopamine release and blocks dopamine D2 receptor function - \"endogenous neuroleptic\" activity Non-Opioid Analgesia : ICV or intrathecal neurotensin produces potent analgesia comparable to morphine, but not reversed by naloxone - entirely non-opioid mechanism Hypothermia : Central neurotensin produces dose-dependent body temperature reduction of 3-5°C - one of the most potent hypothermic agents known GI Effects : Peripheral release → stimulates pancreatic secretion, bile flow, colonic motility; inhibits gastric acid and gastric motility Fat Absorption : Released by luminal fat → facilitates lipid digestion and absorption Mitogenic : NTS1 activation promotes cell proliferation in intestinal epithelium and cancers via MAPK/ERK signaling",
