        "evidence": "Food Safety : Prevents growth of Listeria , Clostridium botulinum , Staphylococcus aureus in dairy, meat, canned foods, and beverages GRAS Status : One of the few antimicrobial peptides recognized as safe for human consumption Natural Origin : Produced by food-grade Lactococcus lactis ; aligns with \"clean label\" consumer preferences Heat Stability : Survives pasteurization (stable at pH <5 up to 121°C) - ideal for processed foods Synergistic Potential : Synergizes with chelators (EDTA), organic acids, lysozyme, and physical treatments (high pressure, pulsed electric fields) Antibiotic Alternative : Clinical potential for MRSA, VRE, and biofilm-associated infections Nisin shows selective cytotoxicity against head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) cells at concentrations non-toxic to normal cells Mechanism: disrupts tumor cell membranes (elevated negative charge on cancer cell surface) Phase I clinical trials in oral cancer being explored 🧪 Vial Dex The World's Most Advanced Peptide Calculator 100% FREE 💧 Reconstitution Vial + BAC water mixing 📉 Dose & Units Syringe units to",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Nisin kills gram-positive bacteria through a unique dual mechanism that simultaneously targets an essential cell wall precursor and disrupts membrane integrity: Lipid II Binding : The N-terminal rings (A and B) of nisin bind with high affinity to Lipid II (undecaprenyl-pyrophosphate-MurNAc-pentapeptide-GlcNAc), the essential peptidoglycan precursor on the outer leaflet of the cytoplasmic membrane Pore Formation : After Lipid II binding, the C-terminal portion of nisin inserts into the membrane, recruiting additional nisin-Lipid II complexes to form stable transmembrane pores (~2 nm diameter; stoichiometry: 8 nisin + 4 Lipid II per pore) Ion Efflux : Pores cause rapid efflux of K⁺, amino acids, and ATP, collapsing the proton motive force Cell Wall Biosynthesis Inhibition : Lipid II sequestration independently blocks peptidoglycan synthesis - even without pore formation Rapid Bactericidal Activity : Cell death occurs within seconds to minutes at bactericidal concentrations Spore Outgrowth Inhibition : Prevents germination and outgrowth of bacterial endospores (critical for food",
