        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Nociceptin Chemical name Nociceptin/Orphanin FQ (N/OFQ) Molecular formula C₈₆H₁₃₀N₂₂O₂₀ Molecular weight 1808.1 g/mol CAS number 164650-44-6 Sequence FGGFTGARKSARKLANQ (17 aa) Length 17 amino acids Source Endogenous (CNS - cortex, hippocampus, hypothalamus, amygdala, spinal cord; derived from prepronociceptin) Half-life ~5-15 minutes (endopeptidase degradation) Targets Nociceptin/Orphanin FQ peptide receptor (NOP, ORL-1) - Gi-coupled GPCR Bioavailability ICV, intrathecal, intranasal (research) Legal status Research compound (endogenous opioid-related peptide) Nociceptin (also called orphanin FQ , N/OFQ ) is a 17-amino acid neuropeptide that was independently discovered in 1995 by two groups - Meunier et al. (who named it nociceptin due to its hyperalgesic effects at supraspinal level) and Reinscheid et al. (who named it orphanin FQ after its receptor, the \"orphan opioid receptor-like 1\").",
        "evidence": "Fourth Opioid System : NOP represents a functionally distinct branch of the opioid receptor family Pain Without Addiction : NOP agonists produce analgesia (spinal) without reward/dependence liability Dual Pain Action : Unique pro-nociceptive (supraspinal) vs. analgesic (spinal) profile enables nuanced pain modulation Substance Abuse Target : NOP agonists reduce rewarding effects of cocaine, alcohol, and opioids - potential addiction therapy [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Meunier JC, et al. \"Isolation and Structure of the Endogenous Agonist of Opioid Receptor-Like ORL1 Receptor.\" Nature . 1995;377:532-535. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Reinscheid RK, et al. \"Orphanin FQ: A Neuropeptide That Activates an Opioidlike G Protein-Coupled Receptor.\" Science . 1995;270:792-794. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Lambert DG. \"The Nociceptin/Orphanin FQ Receptor: A Target with Broad Therapeutic Potential.\" Nat Rev Drug Discov . 2008;7:694-710. ↑ Toll L, et al. \"Nociceptin/Orphanin FQ Receptor Structure, Signaling, Ligands, Functions, and Interactions with Opioid Systems.\" Pharmacol Rev . 2016;68:419-457.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "NOP Receptor Activation : Binds NOP receptor (Gi/Go-coupled) → inhibits adenylyl cyclase → reduces cAMP; opens K⁺ channels; inhibits Ca²⁺ channels Supraspinal Pro-Nociceptive : ICV nociceptin at low doses produces hyperalgesia by functionally antagonizing stress-induced analgesia and endogenous opioid tone - hence the name \"nociceptin\" Spinal Analgesic : Intrathecal nociceptin produces analgesia - opposite to supraspinal effect; inhibits substance P and glutamate release in dorsal horn Anti-Opioid : NOP activation in PAG antagonizes μ-opioid-mediated analgesia; may contribute to opioid tolerance Anxiolytic/Anxiogenic : Dose-dependent - low doses anxiogenic, moderate doses anxiolytic (similar to other stress-modulatory systems) Appetite Stimulation : Central nociceptin increases food intake; NOP KO mice are resistant to diet-induced obesity Memory Modulation : Impairs learning at supraspinal level; NOP antagonists enhance cognition - potential for Alzheimer's treatment Reward/Anti-Reward : Blocks dopamine release in nucleus accumbens; NOP agonists reduce cocaine and alcohol",
