        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Opiorphin Chemical name Opiorphin (QRFSR) Molecular formula C₃₀H₄₉N₁₁O₈ Molecular weight 693.8 g/mol CAS number 864485-24-3 Sequence Gln-Arg-Phe-Ser-Arg (QRFSR) Length 5 amino acids Source Endogenous (human saliva; homolog sialorphin found in rat submandibular gland) Half-life ~5-10 minutes (susceptible to aminopeptidases) Targets Dual enkephalinase inhibitor - inhibits both neprilysin (NEP) and aminopeptidase N (APN) → elevates endogenous enkephalins Bioavailability IV, SC (research); sublingual potential under investigation Legal status Research compound (endogenous peptide) Opiorphin ( QRFSR ) is a 5-amino acid peptide naturally occurring in human saliva, discovered in 2006 by Catherine Rougeot and colleagues at the Pasteur Institute.",
        "evidence": "Pain : Equipotent to morphine (1 mg/kg IV) in the rat pin-pain behavioral model; active in inflammatory and neuropathic pain models Chemical Analgesia Test : Effective in formalin and CFA inflammatory pain at 1-2 mg/kg IV Reduced Tolerance : Preliminary evidence suggests slower tolerance development compared to direct opioid agonists Mood Effects : Anti-anxiety and antidepressant-like effects in rodent models (consistent with δ-receptor activation by elevated enkephalins) Saliva Levels : Opiorphin levels in human saliva vary with emotional and pain states; reduced in chronic pain patients [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Wisner A, et al. \"Human Opiorphin, a Natural Antinociceptive Modulator of Opioid-Dependent Pathways.\" Proc Natl Acad Sci USA . 2006;103:17979-17984. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Rougeot C, et al. \"Sialorphin, a Natural Inhibitor of Rat Membrane-Bound Neutral Endopeptidase That Displays Analgesic Activity.\" Proc Natl Acad Sci USA . 2003;100:8549-8554. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Javelot H, et al.",
        "halfLife": "",
