        "mechanism": "Neprilysin (NEP) Inhibition : Blocks the zinc metalloendopeptidase responsible for cleaving the Gly³-Phe⁴ bond in enkephalins; IC50 ~3-5 μM Aminopeptidase N (APN) Inhibition : Blocks the aminopeptidase that removes the N-terminal Tyr from enkephalins; IC50 ~5-8 μM Enkephalin Potentiation : Combined NEP + APN inhibition increases local enkephalin concentrations 2-5x at active synapses Site-Specific Action : Only enhances opioid signaling where enkephalins are being actively released - pain sites, reward circuits, GI tract Indirect Opioid Effect : Does not directly bind opioid receptors; all effects are mediated through elevated endogenous enkephalins Multiple Receptor Activation : Elevated enkephalins activate both δ-opioid (primary) and μ-opioid (secondary) receptors Reduced Side Effect Potential : Theoretical advantage over direct agonists - avoids excessive receptor stimulation",
