        "summary": "A lipopeptide signal peptide that mimics collagen breakdown fragments to stimulate new collagen and elastin production for anti-ageing skincare.",
        "evidence": "Limited human evidence Cosmetic peptide (lipopeptide form of GHK) regulated under UK cosmetics legislation. Component of the Matrixyl 3000 complex. Topical only. Human evidence grade C, Limited human evidence. Citation quality grade C, Limited human evidence.",
        "halfLife": "Topical application; systemic pharmacokinetics not relevant. Effects depend on continuous application.",
        "halfLifeContext": "Peptide Authority profile summary. Species and route must be checked in the cited pharmacokinetic paper.",
        "mechanism": "Palmitoyl Tripeptide-1 works through matrikine signalling: Matrikine SignallingThe GHK sequence mimics the fragments produced when collagen is broken down by matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs). These fragments serve as \"alarm signals\" that trigger fibroblasts to produce new collagen, effectively turning the natural degradation signal into a regenerative stimulus. Collagen StimulationActivates fibroblasts to increase production of Type I collagen-the primary structural protein responsible for skin firmness and resistance to wrinkling. Elastin SupportIn addition to collagen, Palmitoyl Tripeptide-1 may stimulate elastin production, supporting skin elasticity and resilience. Glycosaminoglycan ProductionResearch suggests the peptide promotes production of glycosaminoglycans (including hyaluronic acid) in the dermal extracellular matrix, supporting skin hydration and volume. Enhanced PenetrationThe palmitic acid conjugation enables the peptide to cross the stratum corneum lipid barrier, reaching the dermis where fibroblasts reside.",
