        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Palopegteriparatide Chemical name TransCon PTH (PEGylated PTH(1-34)) Molecular formula PTH(1-34) + TransCon linker + 40 kDa PEG Molecular weight ~44,000 Da (prodrug complex) CAS number 2171665-56-8 Sequence PTH(1-34) prodrug with transient PEG conjugation Length 34 amino acids (active moiety) Source Synthetic / recombinant Half-life ~60 hours (prodrug); ~2-4 min (released PTH(1-34)) Targets PTH1 receptor (PTH1R) Bioavailability Subcutaneous (prodrug depot) Legal status FDA-approved (2024, Yorvipath) Palopegteriparatide (trade name Yorvipath , development code TransCon PTH ) is an FDA-approved long-acting prodrug of parathyroid hormone fragment PTH(1-34) developed by Ascendis Pharma for the treatment of chronic hypoparathyroidism in adults. It received FDA approval in December 2024 and EMA approval in 2024, becoming the first hormone replacement therapy specifically designed for sustained, physiological PTH replacement in hypoparathyroidism.",
        "evidence": "Calcium/calcitriol independence: ~80% of patients eliminated need for oral calcium and calcitriol supplements Serum calcium normalization: Maintained stable, physiological calcium levels with minimal fluctuations Reduced hypercalciuria: 24-hour urine calcium decreased significantly, reducing nephrolithiasis and nephrocalcinosis risk Phosphate normalization: Corrected hyperphosphatemia in majority of patients Quality of life: Significant improvements in HypoPT-specific and general quality of life measures [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Khan AA et al. (2023). \"Palopegteriparatide (TransCon PTH) in chronic hypoparathyroidism: PaTHway Phase 3 trial.\" J Clin Endocrinol Metab . ↑ 2.0 2.1 Ascendis Pharma (2024). \"Yorvipath Prescribing Information.\" FDA. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Mannstadt M et al. (2020). \"TransCon PTH, a novel long-acting PTH prodrug for hypoparathyroidism: Phase 2 results.\" J Bone Miner Res . ↑ Bilezikian JP et al. (2020). \"Hypoparathyroidism: current concepts.\" Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol .",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Palopegteriparatide works through a prodrug activation mechanism: TransCon Prodrug Design - The active PTH(1-34) peptide is covalently linked via a pH-sensitive and temperature-sensitive linker to a 40 kDa PEG carrier; while conjugated, the PTH moiety is pharmacologically inert (the large PEG shields receptor binding) Sustained Release - After subcutaneous injection, the linker undergoes slow hydrolytic cleavage at physiological pH/temperature, continuously releasing unmodified PTH(1-34) over ~24 hours; the release rate is engineered to produce steady-state PTH levels PTH1R Activation - Released PTH(1-34) binds the PTH type 1 receptor (PTH1R) on osteoblasts and renal tubular cells, activating Gαs-cAMP-PKA signaling cascade Calcium Homeostasis - PTH1R activation in kidney increases renal calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule, stimulates 1α-hydroxylase converting 25(OH)D to active 1,25(OH)₂D (calcitriol), and reduces phosphate reabsorption Bone Metabolism - At physiological (tonic) PTH levels, maintains balanced bone remodeling with appropriate osteoblast and osteoclast activity;",
        "limitations": [],
        "safety": [
          "Most common (≥10%): Headache (23%), hypocalcemia during titration (19%), nausea (15%), paresthesia (12%), diarrhea (11%) Hypocalcemia: Transient episodes during dose titration; managed with temporary calcium supplementation Hypercalcemia: Less frequent than with teriparatide/natpara due to smoother PK profile; dose."
        ],
