        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia PEG-Loxenatide Chemical name Polyethylene Glycol Loxenatide (PEGylated Exendin-4 Analog) Molecular formula Exendin-4 analog + 40 kDa PEG Molecular weight ~44,000 Da (conjugated) CAS number 1186065-46-4 Sequence Modified exendin-4 + PEG conjugation Length 39 amino acids (peptide moiety) Source Synthetic Half-life ~7 days (enabling once-weekly dosing) Targets GLP-1 receptor Bioavailability Subcutaneous injection Legal status Approved (China NMPA, 2019) PEG-Loxenatide (trade name Fulaite 孚来美, also known as polyethylene glycol loxenatide ) is a PEGylated exendin-4 analog GLP-1 receptor agonist approved by China's National Medical Products Administration (NMPA) in May 2019 for the treatment of type 2 diabetes mellitus . Developed by Hansoh Pharmaceutical (Jiangsu, China), it became the first domestically developed once-weekly GLP-1 receptor agonist approved in China.",
        "evidence": "[ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Gao Y, et al. \"Efficacy and safety of polyethylene glycol loxenatide in type 2 diabetes: Phase 3 results.\" Diabetes Obes Metab . 2020. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Hansoh Pharmaceutical. \"PEG-Loxenatide (Fulaite) prescribing information.\" NMPA 2019. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Drucker DJ. \"Advances in oral peptide therapeutics.\" Nat Rev Drug Discov . 2020;19:277-289. ↑ Wang W, et al. \"PEGylation strategies for GLP-1 receptor agonists.\" J Control Release . 2019. P Pep Dex Track saturation levels, half-life decay curves, and injection schedules for 50+ peptides. AI coaching, blood work correlation, and side effect monitoring. Includes MacroDex (4.39M foods) & FitDex (1,356 exercises). Saturation Tracking Half-Life Curves AI Coach 24hr Free Trial Try Free for 24 Hours → Retrieved from \" https://wikipep.org/index.php?title=PEG-Loxenatide&oldid=4024 \" Categories : Peptides GLP-1 Receptor Agonists Approved Drugs Diabetes Medications PEGylated Peptides PEG-Loxenatide Add topic",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "PEG-Loxenatide acts through GLP-1 receptor activation with PEG-mediated pharmacokinetic enhancement: GLP-1 Receptor Agonism - The exendin-4 peptide moiety binds and activates GLP-1R with similar affinity to native GLP-1 but with resistance to DPP-4 enzymatic degradation (exendin-4 contains a Gly2 substitution that prevents DPP-4 cleavage) Glucose-Dependent Insulin Secretion - Stimulates pancreatic β-cell insulin release only when blood glucose is elevated, minimizing hypoglycemia risk through glucose-dependent cAMP/PKA pathway activation Glucagon Suppression - Reduces inappropriate glucagon secretion from pancreatic α-cells in the postprandial state, decreasing hepatic glucose output Gastric Emptying Delay - Slows gastric motility through vagal afferent signaling, reducing postprandial glucose excursions and contributing to satiety PEGylation Pharmacokinetics - 40 kDa PEG conjugation reduces renal clearance (molecular size exceeds glomerular filtration threshold), shields the peptide from proteolytic degradation, and creates a subcutaneous depot effect - collectively extending",
