        "url": "https://peptidejournal.org/peptides/pemvidutide-dual-agonist-for-nash-profile",
        "lastReviewed": "December 21, 2025",
        "summary": "The liver disease formerly known as NASH -- now called MASH (metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis) -- affects roughly 350 million people worldwide. Most treatments target the liver directly.",
        "evidence": "The liver disease formerly known as NASH -- now called MASH (metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis) -- affects roughly 350 million people worldwide. Most treatments target the liver directly. IMPACT Phase 2b Trial (MASH) The IMPACT trial ( NCT05989711 ) enrolled 212 patients with biopsy-confirmed MASH and fibrosis stages F2-F3 across 83 sites in the U.S. and Australia. Patients received once-weekly pemvidutide at 1.2 mg, 1.8 mg, or placebo ( The Lancet, 2025 ). 24-week results: Endpoint 1.2 mg 1.8 mg Placebo MASH resolution (no worsening fibrosis) 59.1% (p 52.1% (p 19.1% Fibrosis improvement (no worsening MASH) 31.8% (NS) 34.5% (NS) 25.9% Liver fat normalization (below 5%) 31% 44% 4% Weight loss -4.8% -5.8% -0.5% The MASH resolution rates were among the strongest in any MASH trial. The fibrosis endpoint did not reach statistical significance at 24 weeks, but AI-based analysis showed 30.6% of patients on 1.8 mg achieved 60% or greater fibrosis reduction vs. 8.2% placebo (p Altimmune, AASLD 2025 ). 48-week results showed continued improvement in fibrosis markers.",
