        "evidence": "1999 Trial : Pexiganan 1% cream vs. ofloxacin 400mg BID oral for mildly infected DFUs → pexiganan was non-inferior (clinical cure rates comparable); FDA CRL - wanted superiority over vehicle 2016 Trial (Dipexium/Locilex) : Pexiganan 0.8% cream vs. vehicle cream → failed to demonstrate superiority over vehicle alone for DFU resolution; FDA CRL issued Lesson Learned : High placebo response in wound trials + standard wound care (debridement, offloading) is highly effective → difficult to demonstrate drug effect above standard care [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Zasloff M. \"Magainins, a Class of Antimicrobial Peptides from Xenopus Skin: Isolation, Characterization of Two Active Forms, and Partial cDNA Sequence of a Precursor.\" Proc Natl Acad Sci . 1987;84:5449-5453. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Ge Y, et al. \"In Vitro Antibacterial Properties of Pexiganan, an Analog of Magainin.\" Antimicrob Agents Chemother . 1999;43:782-788. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Lipsky BA, et al.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Toroidal Pore Formation : Pexiganan adopts α-helical conformation in membrane → inserts into lipid bilayer → forms toroidal pores → rapid membrane disruption and bacterial lysis Broad-Spectrum : Active against Gram-positive (including MRSA), Gram-negative, and polymicrobial wound infections Resistance-Proof : After 18+ passages of bacteria in sub-inhibitory pexiganan concentrations, no resistance development observed - fundamental advantage over conventional antibiotics Rapid Killing : Bactericidal within minutes (vs. hours for conventional antibiotics) - reduces bacterial load before host defenses can be overwhelmed Anti-Biofilm : Disrupts bacterial biofilms in wound beds - critical for chronic wound infections",
