        "url": "https://wikipep.org/wiki/Plecanatide",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Plecanatide Chemical name Plecanatide (Trulance) Molecular formula C₆₅H₁₀₄N₁₈O₂₆S₄ Molecular weight 1681.9 g/mol CAS number 851199-60-5 Sequence NDECELCVNVACTGCL (disulfide bonds: Cys4-Cys12, Cys7-Cys15) Length 16 amino acids Source Synthetic (uroguanylin analog) Half-life Minimal systemic exposure (acts locally in GI tract) Targets Guanylate cyclase-C (GC-C) receptor agonist → intracellular cGMP elevation Bioavailability Oral (locally active; <1% systemic absorption) Legal status FDA Approved (2017) Plecanatide (brand name Trulance ) is a synthetic 16-amino acid peptide analog of human uroguanylin , FDA-approved in January 2017 for the treatment of chronic idiopathic constipation (CIC) in adults and subsequently approved in 2018 for irritable bowel syndrome with constipation (IBS-C). Developed by Synergy Pharmaceuticals, plecanatide was the second guanylate cyclase-C (GC-C) agonist approved by the FDA, following linaclotide (Linzess) in 2012.",
        "evidence": "Increased Bowel Frequency : Significant improvement in complete spontaneous bowel movements (CSBMs) within the first week Reduced Straining : Decreased straining scores and improved stool consistency (Bristol Stool Scale) Abdominal Pain Relief : Clinically meaningful reduction in abdominal pain in IBS-C patients Bloating Reduction : Improvement in abdominal bloating and discomfort scores Once-Daily Dosing : Convenient single 3 mg tablet daily, with or without food Minimal Systemic Exposure : Locally active in the GI tract; plasma concentrations below quantifiable limits Rapid Onset : Therapeutic benefit observed within the first week of treatment [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] [ 5 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Miner PB Jr, et al. \"Plecanatide, a Novel Uroguanylin Analog: A 12-Week, Randomized, Double-Blind, Placebo-Controlled, Dose-Ranging Trial to Evaluate Efficacy and Safety in Patients With Irritable Bowel Syndrome With Constipation (IBS-C).\" Am J Gastroenterol . 2014;109(11):1803-1812. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Brenner DM, et al.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Plecanatide operates through the guanylate cyclase-C signaling pathway, the same pathway used by the endogenous peptides uroguanylin and guanylin to regulate intestinal fluid homeostasis: GC-C Receptor Binding : Plecanatide binds to guanylate cyclase-C receptors on the luminal surface of intestinal epithelial cells, with pH-dependent activity that peaks at pH 5.0 (matching duodenal conditions) cGMP Elevation : Receptor activation catalyzes conversion of GTP to cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP) intracellularly CFTR Activation : Elevated cGMP activates protein kinase G-II (PKGII), which phosphorylates the cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) chloride channel Chloride/Bicarbonate Secretion : Activated CFTR drives Cl⁻ and HCO₃⁻ secretion into the intestinal lumen, creating an osmotic gradient Fluid Secretion : Water follows the osmotic gradient into the intestinal lumen, softening stool and accelerating transit Visceral Pain Reduction : cGMP also modulates visceral afferent nerve signaling, reducing abdominal pain and discomfort associated with IBS-C",
        "limitations": [
          "Pediatric Risk : Contraindicated in patients less than 2 years of age based on nonclinical data showing deaths in young juvenile mice. Avoid use in patients 2 through less than 6 years of age. Safety and effectiveness not established in patients less than 18 years of age."
        ],
        "safety": [
          "Diarrhea (5.0% vs. 1.3% placebo in CIC trials; lower than linaclotide's ~16%) Abdominal distension (1.2%) Flatulence (1.0%) Abdominal tenderness (0.8%) Nausea (rare, <1%).",
          "Severe diarrhea (0.4%): may require discontinuation Dehydration: rare in adults; however, contraindicated in pediatric patients <2 years due to risk of serious dehydration No clinically significant drug interactions identified No effects on QTc prolongation at therapeutic or supratherapeutic doses."
        ],
