          "Nausea: Most common adverse effect (28-48%); typically transient, peaking during initiation and resolving with continued use and gradual titration. Headache: Reported in 5-13% of patients. Anorexia/decreased appetite: 5-9% incidence. Vomiting: 7-11% incidence. Abdominal pain: 2-8% incidence.",
          "Severe hypoglycemia: Boxed warning for insulin-induced severe hypoglycemia, typically occurring within 3 hours of injection. Most common when mealtime insulin is not appropriately reduced at initiation. Risk is higher in type 1 diabetes.",
          "Known hypersensitivity to pramlintide or metacresol (excipient) Confirmed diagnosis of gastroparesis requiring therapy Hypoglycemia unawareness HbA1c >9% (relative contraindication; suggests suboptimal insulin management) Use in patients unable or unwilling to perform frequent blood glucose monitoring."
