        "evidence": "Published February 7, 2026 Updated July 22, 2026 Quick Brief Explore the science behind peptides for sexual health, including PT-141 (Bremelanotide), Melanotan II, and Kisspeptin. Learn what research shows about mechanisms, efficacy, and safety. Best Peptides for Libido & Sexual Health: Complete Research Guide Procurement PT-141 (10MG) Ships from USA $24.50 $49.00 Get PT-141 (10MG) - with PEPTIDEDECK Sexual health peptides represent one of the most intriguing areas of modern pharmaceutical research. Unlike traditional approaches that focus on vascular function, peptides like PT-141 work through the central nervous system-targeting the brain's desire pathways rather than just physical mechanics. This fundamental difference has generated significant scientific interest and led to FDA approval of the first peptide-based treatment for low sexual desire. This comprehensive guide examines what research reveals about peptides that may influence libido and sexual function, their mechanisms of action, and important safety considerations.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Unlike medications that target blood flow and vascular function, peptides affecting sexual health primarily work through the melanocortin receptor system -a network of receptors in the brain involved in regulating numerous functions including skin pigmentation, appetite, and sexual arousal. The discovery that melanocortin peptides influence sexual function was somewhat accidental. During tanning research in the 1990s, scientists at the University of Arizona noticed that test subjects experienced increased sexual arousal as a side effect. This observation launched an entirely new field of pharmaceutical research. ℹ️ The Melanocortin Connection: The melanocortin system includes five receptor subtypes (MC1-MC5). While MC1 receptors control skin pigmentation, MC3 and MC4 receptors appear central to sexual function regulation. Peptides that activate these receptors can influence both tanning and libido-explaining why early tanning peptides had sexual side effects. PT-141 acts as an agonist at melanocortin receptors, particularly MC4R and MC3R in the brain.",
