        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "PT-141 (Bremelanotide) is a synthetic cyclic heptapeptide melanocortin receptor agonist derived from Melanotan II, developed specifically to retain the central sexual arousal activity of MT-II while eliminating the tanning activity. It is the first and only FDA-approved melanocortin-based treatment for hypoactive sexual desire disorder (HSDD) in premenopausal women (approved 2019 as Vyleesi), and the first approved pharmacological agent that acts centrally - directly on the brain - rather than peripherally to produce sexual arousal. PT-141's mechanism is fundamentally different from phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil): those drugs act peripherally on penile vasculature to facilitate erection by blocking cGMP degradation, requiring sexual arousal to already be present; PT-141 acts on MC4R and MC3R receptors in the hypothalamus and limbic system to generate the central neurological state of sexual desire itself.",
        "evidence": "Peptide University labels its records as aggregated from empirical research and anecdotal community data. This record is retained as supplied and is not upgraded to clinical evidence.",
