        "lastReviewed": "2024-09",
        "summary": "A gut-derived peptide hormone released after meals that suppresses appetite by acting on Y2 receptors in the hypothalamus and gut. Studied as one of the body's natural satiety signals and of research interest in obesity and appetite neuroscience.",
        "evidence": "moderate. PYY 3-36 has direct human research involving appetite and food intake, including intravenous administration in obese subjects, an intranasal clinical study that did not establish an effective approved obesity treatment, and supraphysiologic intravenous exposure associated with nausea without additional food-intake reduction. These are study-specific findings, not a routine treatment protocol. AM833, cagrilintide, and other analog or combination programs are separate evidence and must not be attributed to native PYY 3-36.",
        "halfLife": "No validated product-specific pharmacokinetic half-life for exogenous PYY 3-36 has been established.",
        "halfLifeContext": "The site's summary value. Species and route are included only when the site states them.",
        "mechanism": "Peptide YY 3-36 (PYY 3-36) is a gut hormone fragment released postprandially by L-cells of the ileum, colon, and rectum. It is produced by DPP-4 cleavage of the full PYY(1-36) precursor and acts primarily at the Y2 receptor. Direct human research has examined appetite and food intake after study-specific PYY 3-36 administration, including intranasal and intravenous exposure, but PYY 3-36 is not an approved obesity treatment. AM833, cagrilintide, and other long-acting or oral analog programs are separate entities and are not native PYY 3-36 evidence.",
