        "sourceId": "wikipep",
        "url": "https://wikipep.org/wiki/PYY(3-36)",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia PYY(3-36) Chemical name Peptide YY(3-36) (PYY3-36) Molecular formula C₁₇₂H₂₅₆N₄₄O₅₃ Molecular weight 4050 g/mol CAS number 123583-37-9 Sequence IKPEAPGEDASPEELNRYYASLRHYLNLVTRQRY-NH₂ (34 aa; truncated from PYY1-36) Length 34 amino acids (truncated form of full-length PYY) Source Endogenous (L cells of distal ileum and colon; released postprandially) Half-life ~15-20 minutes Targets Y2 receptor (NPY2R) - Gi-coupled GPCR; appetite suppression via arcuate nucleus Bioavailability IV infusion (clinical research); SC (investigational); intranasal (Phase II) Legal status Research compound (endogenous satiety hormone; intranasal formulation in clinical trials) PYY(3-36) ( Peptide YY 3-36 ) is the physiologically active truncated form of peptide YY, a 36-amino acid gut hormone released from enteroendocrine L cells in the distal ileum and colon following meal ingestion.",
        "evidence": "Batterham et al. (2002) : PYY(3-36) IV infusion reduced caloric intake by 33% in lean subjects over 24 hours - landmark Nature paper establishing PYY as satiety hormone Batterham et al. (2003) : Obese subjects retained sensitivity to exogenous PYY(3-36) - appetite was reduced by 30%, and obese individuals had lower endogenous PYY levels Bariatric Surgery : Post-RYGB PYY levels increase 5-10x; this exaggerated PYY response is considered a major mechanism for sustained weight loss after surgery Combination Therapy : PYY(3-36) + GLP-1 co-administration produces additive appetite suppression (>50% reduction in food intake in some studies) Intranasal Delivery : Achieves sufficient CNS exposure for appetite effects without IV infusion; Novo Nordisk advanced intranasal PYY to Phase II [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Batterham RL, et al. \"Gut Hormone PYY(3-36) Physiologically Inhibits Food Intake.\" Nature . 2002;418:650-654. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Batterham RL, et al. \"Inhibition of Food Intake in Obese Subjects by Peptide YY3-36.\" N Engl J Med . 2003;349:941-948. ↑ 3.0 3.1 le Roux CW, et al.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Y2 Receptor Activation : PYY(3-36) selectively binds Y2R (presynaptic autoreceptor) on NPY/AgRP neurons in the arcuate nucleus → inhibits NPY/AgRP release → removes tonic appetite stimulation POMC/α-MSH Activation : Indirect disinhibition of anorexigenic POMC neurons → increased α-MSH signaling → appetite suppression Vagal Afferent Signaling : PYY(3-36) also activates vagal afferents in the gut wall → signals to nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) → brainstem satiety circuits Ileal Brake : Slows gastric emptying, reduces intestinal motility, and inhibits pancreatic and gastric secretion - optimizes nutrient absorption while signaling satiety Dose-Dependent Satiety : IV infusion of PYY(3-36) at physiological postprandial levels reduces food intake by 30-36% in humans",
