        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Relaxin-2 Chemical name Human Relaxin-2 (H2 relaxin; Serelaxin/RLX030) Molecular formula C₂₅₆H₃₉₈N₇₂O₇₇S₆ (approximate) Molecular weight ~5963 g/mol CAS number 9002-69-1 (relaxin); 159519-65-0 (serelaxin) Sequence A-chain (24 aa) + B-chain (29 aa) connected by 2 inter-chain and 1 intra-chain disulfide bonds Length 53 amino acids total (heterodimeric; A-chain 24 aa + B-chain 29 aa) Source Endogenous (corpus luteum, decidua, prostate, heart, brain); recombinant form = serelaxin Half-life ~6-10 minutes (IV); very short - requires continuous infusion Targets RXFP1 (relaxin family peptide receptor 1) - Gs-coupled GPCR → cAMP, NO, VEGF pathways Bioavailability IV infusion only (rapid enzymatic degradation); no oral or SC bioavailability at therapeutic levels Legal status Research compound; serelaxin (recombinant) - Phase III for acute heart failure (failed primary endpoint in RELAX-AHF-2) Relaxin-2 (also called H2 relaxin ) is an endogenous peptide",
        "evidence": "RELAX-AHF (2013) : Serelaxin 30mcg/kg/day IV × 48h in acute heart failure → improved dyspnea at 6 hours and reduced 180-day all-cause mortality by 37% (p=0.02) - published in Lancet RELAX-AHF-2 (2019) : Confirmatory Phase III (N=6,545) → failed primary endpoints (CV death + worsening HF through day 5); development discontinued Anti-Fibrotic Potential : Despite AHF failure, relaxin's anti-fibrotic mechanism remains promising for pulmonary fibrosis, liver fibrosis, and chronic kidney disease [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Teerlink JR, et al. \"Serelaxin, Recombinant Human Relaxin-2, for Treatment of Acute Heart Failure (RELAX-AHF): A Randomised, Placebo-Controlled Trial.\" Lancet . 2013;381:29-39. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Metra M, et al. \"Effect of Serelaxin on Cardiac, Renal, and Hepatic Biomarkers in the Relaxin in Acute Heart Failure (RELAX-AHF) Development Program.\" J Am Coll Cardiol . 2013;61:196-206. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Samuel CS, et al. \"Relaxin-1-Deficient Mice Develop an Age-Related Progression of Renal Fibrosis.\" Kidney Int . 2004;65:2054-2064. ↑ Bathgate RA, et al.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "RXFP1 Activation : Binds RXFP1 → Gs → cAMP → PKA → nitric oxide synthase activation → vasodilation; also activates PI3K/Akt pathway Vasodilation : Potent vasodilator via endothelin-B receptor upregulation → NO release → reduced systemic and renal vascular resistance Anti-Fibrotic : Inhibits TGF-β signaling → reduces collagen deposition → prevents/reverses fibrosis in heart, kidney, liver, lung Anti-Inflammatory : Reduces inflammatory cytokine production; inhibits neutrophil activation; promotes M2 macrophage polarization Renal Effects : Increases renal blood flow by 20-40% and GFR by 20% - important for pregnancy adaptation and heart failure Pregnancy Role : Softens cervix (collagen remodeling), inhibits uterine contractions, increases cardiac output by 30-50%, promotes decidual angiogenesis",
        "limitations": [],
