        "summary": "A 27-amino-acid intestinal hormone used clinically as synthetic human secretin (ChiRhoStim) for diagnostic procedures involving pancreatic secretion, gastrin secretion, and ERCP. Controlled clinical trials did not demonstrate efficacy for autism.",
        "evidence": "strong-human. Secretin has established human clinical use as a diagnostic agent. Current ChiRhoStim labeling supports stimulation of pancreatic secretions, including bicarbonate, to aid diagnosis of exocrine pancreas dysfunction; stimulation of gastrin secretion to aid diagnosis of gastrinoma; and stimulation of pancreatic secretions to facilitate ERCP. Its established use is diagnostic rather than chronic therapeutic. Historical interest in autism was not supported by controlled evidence: a double-blind, placebo-controlled trial of synthetic human secretin did not demonstrate improvement versus placebo, and a Cochrane systematic review concluded that randomized controlled trials did not show improvement in core autism-spectrum-disorder features. Multiple randomized controlled trials and systematic reviews failed to demonstrate clinical benefit for autism.",
