        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Secretin Chemical name Secretin (human, porcine) Molecular formula C₁₃₀H₂₂₀N₄₄O₃₉ Molecular weight 3055.5 g/mol CAS number 17034-35-4 (porcine); 108153-74-8 (human synthetic) Sequence HSDGTFTSELSRLREGARLQRLLQGLV-NH₂ (human) Length 27 amino acids Source Endogenous (S cells of duodenal mucosa); synthetic for clinical use Half-life ~2.5-5 minutes (rapid hepatic/renal clearance) Targets Secretin receptor (SCTR) - class B GPCR → Gs-coupled → cAMP elevation Bioavailability IV injection (clinical); SC (research) Legal status FDA Approved (2004 - synthetic human secretin; SecreFlo/ChiRhoStim) Secretin is a 27-amino acid peptide hormone that holds a landmark position in the history of endocrinology - it was the first substance to be called a \"hormone,\" identified by Bayliss and Starling in 1902. Secretin is produced by enteroendocrine S cells in the duodenal and jejunal mucosa in response to acidic chyme entering the small intestine.",
        "evidence": "Diagnostic Utility (Pancreatic Function) : Gold standard stimulant for pancreatic function testing; quantifies exocrine reserve Gastrinoma Diagnosis : Secretin stimulation test has >90% sensitivity for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome MRCP Enhancement : Secretin-enhanced magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (S-MRCP) improves visualization of pancreatic duct anatomy ERCP Facilitation : Aids duodenal relaxation and papilla identification during endoscopic procedures Historical Significance : First hormone ever discovered; foundational to modern endocrinology Safety : Excellent safety profile at diagnostic doses; serious adverse events extremely rare [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] [ 5 ] [ 6 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Bayliss WM, Starling EH. \"The Mechanism of Pancreatic Secretion.\" J Physiol . 1902;28(5):325-353. [Historical landmark - first use of the word \"hormone\"] ↑ 2.0 2.1 Chey WY, Chang TM. \"Secretin: Historical Perspective and Current Status.\" Pancreas . 2014;43(2):162-182. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Dreiling DA, Janowitz HD. \"The Measurement of Pancreatic Secretory Function.\"",
