        "evidence": "moderate. Selank has been evaluated in several small human studies, including research in generalized anxiety disorder, neurasthenia, phobic-anxiety and somatoform disorders. These studies reported anxiolytic effects and some cognitive-related findings. Human research has also examined immune signaling and brain functional connectivity. Mechanistic studies suggest effects involving GABAergic signaling, neurotransmission-related gene expression, and BDNF-related pathways, although much of this mechanistic work is preclinical. The main limitation is the relatively small and geographically concentrated human evidence base, with limited large-scale independent replication.",
