        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "Selank is a synthetic heptapeptide (Thr-Lys-Pro-Arg-Pro-Gly-Pro) developed by the Institute of Molecular Genetics of the Russian Academy of Sciences as a stabilized analog of the endogenous immunomodulatory tetrapeptide tuftsin (Thr-Lys-Pro-Arg). Tuftsin is a natural fragment of IgG immunoglobulin that activates macrophage function and has CNS activity; Selank appends a Pro-Gly-Pro tripeptide tail that dramatically extends the half-life by protecting against rapid enzymatic degradation, converting a peptide with seconds-scale activity into one with meaningful pharmacokinetic duration. Selank was developed under Russia's Institute of Molecular Genetics program as an anxiolytic agent and received approval in Russia for the treatment of generalized anxiety disorder.",
        "evidence": "Peptide University labels its records as aggregated from empirical research and anecdotal community data. This record is retained as supplied and is not upgraded to clinical evidence.",
