        "evidence": "Supports significant reduction in body weight through appetite suppression and reduced caloric intake Helps regulate blood glucose levels by enhancing insulin secretion when blood sugar rises May reduce cardiovascular risk factors including blood pressure and inflammatory markers Promotes feelings of fullness and satiety that persist between meals Supports metabolic health improvements including reduced visceral fat accumulation The clinical evidence supporting semaglutide spans dozens of large, well-designed trials. The STEP (Semaglutide Treatment Effect in People with Obesity) program alone enrolled thousands of participants across multiple studies. Results consistently show substantial, sustained weight loss with acceptable safety profiles. The SELECT trial represented a landmark moment for obesity treatment. This massive study enrolled over 17,000 people with established cardiovascular disease and overweight or obesity but without diabetes. After following participants for over four years, semaglutide reduced major adverse cardiovascular events by 20% compared to placebo.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Understanding how semaglutide produces its effects requires looking at three interconnected systems: the brain's appetite centers, the digestive tract, and metabolic hormone signaling. The compound influences all three simultaneously, creating its powerful impact on weight and blood sugar. StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf (2024)",
