        "summary": "Semaglutide is a GLP-1 receptor agonist - meaning it mimics a hormone your body naturally produces after eating called GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1). That hormone tells your pancreas to release insulin when blood sugar rises, signals your brain that you are full, and slows down how quickly food moves out of your stomach. Semaglutide does all of those things simultaneously, but at a far greater magnitude and duration than your body does on its own. The result is a powerful and sustained reduction in appetite, improved blood sugar regulation, and significantly slower gastric emptying - all from a single weekly injection. In research, semaglutide has become the most studied and clinically validated peptide compound in the weight loss space. Subjects in the landmark STEP trials lost an average of 15-17% of total body weight over 68 weeks, which represented a category-defining result at the time.",
