        "summary": "Semax is a synthetic heptapeptide analog of the ACTH(4-10) fragment, developed at the Institute of Molecular Genetics of the Russian Academy of Sciences. It is studied as a neuroprotective nootropic that upregulates the neurotrophins BDNF and NGF without stimulating cortisol. It is used clinically in Russia for some neurological indications but is not FDA-approved in the United States. Semax has the sequence Met-Glu-His-Phe-Pro-Gly-Pro (MEHFPGP), built by appending a C-terminal Pro-Gly-Pro tripeptide to the ACTH(4-7) fragment to resist enzymatic degradation. Unlike full ACTH, it does not stimulate cortisol release. Preclinical and Russian clinical research has examined it for cognitive enhancement, stroke/ischemia neuroprotection, and attention, with proposed mechanisms centered on rapid upregulation of BDNF and its receptor TrkB in the hippocampus, modulation of nitric oxide and lipid peroxidation, and effects on monoaminergic signaling. In the U.S.",
        "evidence": "Has Russian clinical use and a preclinical literature base (BDNF/NGF, ischemia neuroprotection), but lacks the large Western randomized controlled trials required for FDA approval. Evidence for general 'cognitive enhancement' in healthy people is limited.",
