        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Sermorelin Chemical name Sermorelin acetate; GRF(1-29)-NH₂; GHRH(1-29) Molecular formula C₁₄₉H₂₄₆N₄₄O₄₂S Molecular weight 3357.9 g/mol CAS number 86168-78-7 Sequence Tyr-Ala-Asp-Ala-Ile-Phe-Thr-Asn-Ser-Tyr-Arg-Lys-Val-Leu-Gly-Gln-Leu-Ser-Ala-Arg-Lys-Leu-Leu-Gln-Asp-Ile-Met-Ser-Arg-NH₂ Length 29 amino acids Source Synthetic analog of human GHRH(1-29) Half-life 10-20 minutes (plasma) Targets Growth hormone-releasing hormone receptor (GHRHR) Bioavailability Subcutaneous bioavailability ~95%; not orally bioavailable Legal status Previously FDA-approved (Geref); currently available through compounding pharmacies in some jurisdictions Sermorelin (also known as GRF(1-29)-NH₂ or GHRH(1-29) ) is a synthetic peptide analog consisting of the first 29 amino acids of the naturally occurring 44-amino acid growth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH).",
        "evidence": "Growth hormone receptors are expressed in the hippocampus and other brain regions involved in learning and memory. GH/IGF-1 axis stimulation has been associated with improvements in cognitive function, particularly in memory and concentration, as well as improvements in overall sense of well-being and mood in GH-deficient populations. The foundational clinical evidence for sermorelin includes the original FDA registration trials demonstrating its efficacy in stimulating growth hormone release in children with GH deficiency. A pivotal study published in the Journal of Clinical Endocrinology and Metabolism demonstrated that sermorelin administration produced a dose-dependent increase in serum GH levels in GH-deficient children, with the 30 mcg/kg dose producing a mean GH increase comparable to physiological levels. ↑ Thorner MO, Rochiccioli P, Colle M, et al. Once daily subcutaneous growth hormone-releasing hormone therapy accelerates growth in growth hormone-deficient children during the first year of therapy. Journal of Clinical Endocrinology and Metabolism .",
