        "summary": "Sermorelin is the oldest and most clinically well-characterized GHRH analog in this database. It consists of the first 29 amino acids of endogenous human growth hormone-releasing hormone (hGHRH 1-29), which constitute the minimum biologically active fragment required for full GHRH receptor binding and pituitary GH stimulation. Native GHRH is 44 amino acids long, but truncation to the 29-amino acid fragment preserves complete receptor affinity while producing a shorter, more manufacturable peptide. Sermorelin was FDA-approved in the 1990s under the brand name Geref for the diagnosis of GH deficiency in children and later for the treatment of adult GH deficiency and HIV-associated wasting, giving it one of the longest and most robust human clinical safety records among all compounds in this database. As the mildest GHRH analog in this database - relative to CJC-1295 No DAC and especially tesamorelin - sermorelin produces the most physiologically conservative GH stimulation.",
