        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Somatostatin Chemical name Somatotropin release-inhibiting factor Molecular formula C₇₆H₁₀₄N₁₈O₁₉S₂ Molecular weight 1637.9 g/mol CAS number 51110-01-1 Sequence Ala-Gly-Cys-Lys-Asn-Phe-Phe-Trp-Lys-Thr-Phe-Thr-Ser-Cys Length 14 amino acids (SST-14) Source Naturally occurring neuropeptide hormone Half-life 1-3 minutes Targets Somatostatin receptors (SSTR1-5) Bioavailability Intravenous, subcutaneous injection Legal status Prescription medication (approved analogs) Somatostatin (also known as somatotropin release-inhibiting factor or SRIF) is a naturally occurring cyclic peptide hormone that functions as a powerful inhibitor of growth hormone secretion and numerous other physiological processes. Originally discovered in 1973 by Roger Guillemin and colleagues while searching for growth hormone-releasing factors, somatostatin was found to have the opposite effect - potently suppressing growth hormone release from the pituitary gland.",
