        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "SS-31 (elamipretide, Szeto-Schiller peptide 31) is a tetrapeptide (D-Arg-2'6'-dimethylTyr-Lys-Phe-NH₂) that selectively accumulates in the inner mitochondrial membrane (IMM) through electrostatic interaction driven by the large negative mitochondrial membrane potential (approximately -180 mV). This selectivity is the defining pharmacological property of SS-31: while most molecules cannot penetrate the double-membrane architecture of mitochondria, SS-31's cationic charge and alternating aromatic residue design drives it specifically to the IMM at concentrations 5,000-fold higher than cytoplasmic levels. The molecular target at the IMM is cardiolipin - a unique tetra-acyl phospholipid found almost exclusively in the inner mitochondrial membrane that is essential for structuring the electron transport chain complexes (particularly Complex I and Complex IV) and stabilizing the curvature of the IMM cristae.",
