        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-10",
        "summary": "SS-31 (elamipretide) is a mitochondria-targeted tetrapeptide that binds and stabilizes cardiolipin in the inner mitochondrial membrane, improving mitochondrial function and reducing reactive oxygen species. It is an investigational drug (developed by Stealth BioTherapeutics) studied in mitochondrial diseases such as Barth syndrome, mitochondrial myopathy, and Friedreich's ataxia, as well as dry age-related macular degeneration. It is not FDA-approved. SS-31 (elamipretide; development codes MTP-131, Bendavia) is a small aromatic-cationic tetrapeptide, D-Arg-Dmt-Lys-Phe-NH2 (where Dmt is 2,6-dimethyltyrosine), with limited blood-brain-barrier penetration. It concentrates in the inner mitochondrial membrane and binds cardiolipin, stabilizing membrane structure and the electron transport chain, thereby improving mitochondrial energetics and reducing toxic reactive oxygen species.",
        "evidence": "Studied in multiple registered clinical trials across mitochondrial disorders and dry AMD, generally well tolerated with preliminary evidence of benefit, but without an FDA approval to date. Mechanistic and animal evidence for mitochondrial protection is substantial.",
