        "url": "https://wikipep.org/wiki/Substance_P",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Substance P Chemical name Substance P; Neurokinin A; SP Molecular formula C₆₃H₉₈N₁₈O₁₃S Molecular weight 1347.6 g/mol CAS number 33507-63-0 Sequence Arg-Pro-Lys-Pro-Gln-Gln-Phe-Phe-Gly-Leu-Met-NH₂ Length 11 amino acids Source Endogenous neuropeptide; encoded by TAC1 gene (preprotachykinin A) Half-life ~1-2 minutes (plasma) Targets Neurokinin-1 receptor (NK1R; primary), NK2R and NK3R (lower affinity) Bioavailability Not orally bioavailable; rapid enzymatic degradation Legal status Research compound; not approved as therapeutic agent Substance P ( SP ) is an endogenous neuropeptide belonging to the tachykinin family, consisting of 11 amino acids (an undecapeptide) with the sequence Arg-Pro-Lys-Pro-Gln-Gln-Phe-Phe-Gly-Leu-Met-NH₂. It was originally discovered in 1931 by Ulf von Euler and John Gaddum as an unidentified substance in equine brain and intestinal extracts that caused smooth muscle contraction.",
        "evidence": "Despite strong preclinical rationale, NK1 receptor antagonists have generally failed to demonstrate analgesic efficacy in clinical trials of various pain conditions. This paradoxical failure has been attributed to species differences in NK1R pharmacology between rodents and humans, redundancy in pain transmission pathways, and the finding that conventional NK1R antagonists may not adequately block endosomal NK1R signaling, which sustains pain. Novel endosome-targeted NK1R antagonists represent a promising new approach to overcoming this limitation. === Psychiatric and Neurological Research === [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] NK1R antagonists have shown antidepressant and anxiolytic effects in preclinical models, and early clinical trials of the NK1R antagonist MK-869 showed promising antidepressant efficacy. However, subsequent larger trials failed to confirm these findings. Research continues into the role of substance P in PTSD, anxiety disorders, and substance abuse.",
