        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Survodutide (BI 456906) Chemical name Survodutide; BI 456906; BI-456906 Molecular formula C₁₉₂H₂₈₉N₄₇O₆₁ Molecular weight 4231.63 g/mol CAS number 2805997-46-8 Sequence Ac4c-QGTFTSDYSKYLDERAAKDFI-K(GGSGSG-γGlu-C₁₈ diacid)-WLESA-NH₂ (modified 29-aa glucagon analog with C18 fatty acid) Length 29 amino acids (with lipidation) Source Synthetic (modified glucagon analog) Half-life ~6-7 days (enabling once-weekly dosing) Targets Glucagon receptor (GCGR) and GLP-1 receptor (GLP-1R); dual agonist Bioavailability Subcutaneous (estimated high bioavailability) Legal status Investigational (Phase 3 clinical trials) Survodutide , also known by its development code BI 456906 , is an investigational dual glucagon receptor (GCGR) and glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor (GLP-1R) agonist being developed by Boehringer Ingelheim for the treatment of obesity, nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH/MASH), and type 2 diabetes.",
        "evidence": "GLP-1R agonism offsets glucagon's hyperglycemic effects through insulin secretion GCGR agonism adds energy expenditure and lipolytic effects not achievable with GLP-1R alone Combined mechanism produces greater caloric deficit than either agonist alone Particularly effective for hepatic steatosis through direct GCGR-mediated lipid metabolism SYNCHRONIZE-1: Phase 3 trial in adults with obesity or overweight with at least one weight-related comorbidity SYNCHRONIZE-2: Phase 3 trial in adults with obesity and type 2 diabetes LIVERAGE-1/2: Phase 3 trials in adults with MASH Route: Subcutaneous injection Frequency: Once weekly Dose Escalation: Gradual up-titration over several weeks to minimize gastrointestinal side effects Target Doses: 0.6, 2.4, 3.6, and 4.8 mg evaluated in Phase 2; Phase 3 doses to be confirmed Injection Sites: Abdomen, thigh, or upper arm Pre-Filled Pens: Anticipated for commercial use (similar to other injectable GLP-1 agonists) Hepatic function (particularly important for MASH trials) Pancreatic enzymes Heart rate and cardiovascular parameters Blood glucose",
        "halfLife": "",
