        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "Survodutide (BI 456906) is a dual GLP-1 and glucagon receptor agonist developed by Boehringer Ingelheim, and it takes a structurally distinct approach from other dual agonists like mazdutide. While mazdutide is derived from a GLP-1 backbone, survodutide is built on a glucagon peptide backbone with modifications added to confer GLP-1 receptor activity - meaning it leads with glucagon agonism and adds GLP-1 on top, rather than the reverse. The practical effect of this design is a strongly glucagon-weighted dual mechanism: aggressive hepatic fat oxidation, meaningful increases in resting energy expenditure and thermogenesis, combined with the appetite suppression and gastric slowing of GLP-1 signaling. The two pathways counterbalance each other metabolically - the GLP-1 component prevents the hyperglycemia that pure glucagon activation would cause, while glucagon drives the calorie-burning effects that GLP-1 alone cannot produce.",
        "evidence": "Peptide University labels its records as aggregated from empirical research and anecdotal community data. This record is retained as supplied and is not upgraded to clinical evidence.",
