        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "Published July 25, 2026 Updated September 12, 2026 Quick Brief FDA advisers backed BPC-157, KPV, TB-500, MOTS-c, Epitalon and Semax while rejecting Emideltide/DSIP. See every tally, what the non-binding recommendations mean, and what FDA does next. FDA Peptide Vote 2026: 6 of 7 Backed for Compounding Procurement BPC-157 (10mg) Ships from USA $24.50 $49.00 Get BPC-157 (10mg) - with PEPTIDEDECK Updated July 25, 2026: The FDA peptide vote 2026 is complete. Across the July 23-24 meeting, the Pharmacy Compounding Advisory Committee recommended six of the seven peptides for the federal 503A Bulks List: BPC-157 , KPV, TB-500, MOTS-c, Epitalon and Semax. Emideltide, also called DSIP, was the only peptide rejected. Every tally remains non-binding, and FDA has not yet issued its final agency decision. BPC-157, KPV and TB-500 each received favorable 8-6 votes with one abstention on Day 1. MOTS-c received favorable 7-5 votes with two abstentions.",
        "evidence": "The nominated clinical use FDA evaluated was ulcerative colitis. Agency reviewers concluded that the submitted information did not provide enough reliable evidence to show effectiveness for that use. The available clinical material was small, old, brief, incompletely reported, and not sufficient to establish a dependable benefit-risk conclusion. FDA also explained that evidence for other highly promoted uses was outside the committee evaluation or too incomplete to support review. A successful vote on the clinical-need question should not be rewritten as proof that BPC-157 heals tendons, ligaments, muscles, or the gastrointestinal tract in people. FDA’s briefing found no adequate human studies for the commonly proposed oral, subcutaneous, nasal, or transdermal routes. That does not prove those routes cannot work. It means the agency did not have reliable human data showing what exposure they produce, whether they help, how often adverse effects occur, or how product variability affects results.",
