        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "TB-500 is the synthetic analog of Thymosin Beta-4 (Tβ4) - a 43 amino acid protein that is among the most abundant peptides in human cells and is present in virtually every mammalian tissue. TB-500 represents the active sequence region of Tβ4 responsible for its primary biological functions. Thymosin Beta-4 was originally identified in thymic tissue as part of the immune regulation machinery, but its most critical role - and the one relevant to this context - is as the principal regulator of G-actin sequestration. Actin is the structural protein that drives cellular motility: every cell that migrates, every wound edge that contracts, and every tissue remodeling event in the body requires the dynamic assembly and disassembly of actin filaments. TB-500 binds G-actin (the monomeric form) with extremely high affinity, maintaining a pool of available actin monomers that cells can rapidly mobilize for migration.",
