        "summary": "An FDA-approved 34-amino-acid fragment of human parathyroid hormone used as an anabolic, bone-building therapy for osteoporosis in people at high fracture risk.",
        "evidence": "strong-human. Teriparatide has strong human evidence from randomized trials and long clinical experience. In the pivotal study of 1,637 postmenopausal women with prior vertebral fractures, daily PTH(1-34) reduced the relative risk of new vertebral fractures by about 65% with the 20 mcg dose and reduced nonvertebral fragility fractures by about 53% compared with placebo. Teriparatide also increased bone mineral density at the lumbar spine, femoral neck, and other skeletal sites. Later studies expanded its use to men with osteoporosis and to glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis. Osteosarcoma occurred in rat studies, but human observational studies have not shown an increased risk. Current FDA labeling allows consideration of treatment beyond 2 years when a patient remains or returns to high fracture risk.",
