        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Tesamorelin Chemical name Modified Growth Hormone Releasing Hormone Molecular formula C₂₂₁H₃₆₆N₇₂O₆₇S Molecular weight 5135.89 g/mol CAS number 218949-48-5 Sequence Trans-3-hexenoyl-GHRH(1-44)-NH₂ Length 44 amino acids + hexenoyl modification Source Synthetic GHRH analog Half-life 26-38 minutes Targets Growth hormone releasing hormone receptor Bioavailability Subcutaneous injection Legal status FDA approved for HIV lipodystrophy Tesamorelin is a synthetic analog of growth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH) consisting of all 44 amino acids of human GHRH with the addition of a trans-3-hexenoic acid group at the N-terminus. FDA-approved under the brand name Egrifta for the treatment of HIV-associated lipodystrophy, tesamorelin has gained significant attention in performance enhancement and anti-aging communities for its selective reduction of visceral adipose tissue while preserving subcutaneous fat and lean muscle mass.",
        "evidence": "Abdominal Fat Loss : Average 15-20% reduction in visceral adipose tissue Liver Fat Reduction : Decreased hepatic steatosis (fatty liver) Lipid Profile Improvement : Better cholesterol and triglyceride levels IGF-1 Elevation : Sustained increase in insulin-like growth factor-1 Cognitive Enhancement : Potential improvements in memory and cognition Phase III Trials : Demonstrated 15-20% visceral fat reduction Long-term Studies : Sustained benefits over 26-52 weeks Safety Studies : Well-tolerated with minimal side effects Comparison Studies : Superior to placebo for fat reduction Insulin Sensitivity : Improved glucose metabolism markers Lipid Profiles : Reduced triglycerides and improved HDL Inflammatory Markers : Decreased CRP and IL-6 levels Adipokine Regulation : Improved adiponectin and leptin balance DEXA Scanning : Documented visceral fat reduction MRI Studies : Confirmed selective abdominal fat loss CT Imaging : Measured liver fat reduction Bioimpedance Analysis : Preserved lean mass during fat loss Memory Studies : Potential improvements in verbal memory Executive Function :",
