        "summary": "Tesamorelin is a GHRH analog consisting of the full 44 amino acids of endogenous GHRH with a trans-3-hexenoic acid group attached to the N-terminus - a modification that protects it from enzymatic degradation and extends its biological half-life while preserving the complete native GHRH receptor binding architecture. Unlike CJC-1295 No DAC (which is derived from the first 29 amino acids of GHRH), tesamorelin uses the entire GHRH sequence, making its receptor activation more faithful to the natural hormone and producing potent GH and IGF-1 responses. It is FDA-approved under the brand name Egrifta specifically for the treatment of HIV-associated lipodystrophy - a metabolic condition characterized by visceral fat accumulation caused by antiretroviral therapy - making it one of the very few GHRH analogs to have completed the full FDA approval pathway. The defining clinical characteristic of tesamorelin is its specificity for visceral adipose tissue (VAT) reduction.",
