        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "Tesofensine is a triple monoamine reuptake inhibitor - meaning it simultaneously blocks the reuptake transporters for serotonin, norepinephrine, and dopamine (hence SNDRI: serotonin-noradrenaline-dopamine reuptake inhibitor). What that means in plain terms: when you eat, your brain releases these three neurotransmitters to signal satisfaction and reduce the urge to keep eating. Normally they are rapidly cleared by reuptake transporters. Tesofensine blocks those transporters, so serotonin, norepinephrine, and dopamine all remain elevated and active for longer - producing a powerful, sustained suppression of appetite, a meaningful reduction in food-seeking behavior driven by dopaminergic pathways, and an increase in resting energy expenditure driven by the norepinephrine component. It also silences GABAergic neurons in the lateral hypothalamus - a feeding-control center in the brain - which independently reduces eating behavior.",
