        "mechanism": "Thymalin's proposed mechanisms of action relate to its mimicry of endogenous thymic peptide factors and their effects on immune regulation: T-Cell Maturation and DifferentiationThe thymus gland produces several peptide factors (including thymulin, thymopoietin, and thymosin fractions) that guide the maturation of T-lymphocyte precursors into functional T-cells. Thymalin is proposed to supplement these declining thymic factors, thereby: - Promoting the differentiation of immature thymocytes into functional CD4+ helper T-cells and CD8+ cytotoxic T-cells - Supporting the thymic selection process that eliminates self-reactive T-cells (central tolerance ) - Enhancing the output of naive T-cells with diverse receptor repertoires Immunomodulatory EffectsRather than simply stimulating immune function, Thymalin appears to act as a modulator - capable of both enhancing suppressed immunity and tempering overactive immune responses: - Restoration of T-helper to T-suppressor cell ratios toward normal values - Enhanced natural killer (NK) cell activity - Modulation of cytokine production profiles",
