        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "Thymalin is a natural polypeptide bioregulator extracted from the thymic tissue of young calves through a proprietary aqueous extraction process developed by the St. Petersburg Institute of Bioregulation and Gerontology - the same research group responsible for the Khavinson bioregulator system. Unlike the synthetic dipeptide and tetrapeptide Khavinson bioregulators (Vilon, Epithalon, Pinealon), Thymalin is a heterogeneous mixture of naturally occurring thymic peptides rather than a single defined sequence - making it analogous to Cerebrolysin in the neural space: a complex biological extract delivering multiple synergistic peptide signals rather than a single molecular entity. The core active components of Thymalin include thymosin alpha-1 precursor fragments, thymulin, thymopoietin fragments, and other thymus-derived immunoregulatory peptides.",
