        "sourceId": "wikipep",
        "url": "https://wikipep.org/wiki/Thymulin",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Thymulin (FTS/Serum Thymic Factor) Chemical name Thymulin; Facteur Thymique Serique (FTS); Serum Thymic Factor; Nonathymulin Molecular formula C₃₃H₅₄N₁₂O₁₅ (free peptide); C₃₃H₅₂N₁₂O₁₅Zn (zinc-bound active form) Molecular weight 858.86 g/mol (free peptide); 922.21 g/mol (zinc complex) CAS number 63958-90-7 Sequence pGlu-Ala-Lys-Ser-Gln-Gly-Gly-Ser-Asn (pyroGlu-AKSQGGSN) Length 9 amino acids Source Endogenous (secreted by thymic epithelial cells) Half-life Minutes (rapidly cleared from circulation) Targets T-lymphocyte precursors; zinc-dependent immune modulation; GABA-A receptors (analgesic pathway) Bioavailability Low oral bioavailability; requires parenteral administration Legal status Research compound; available as research peptide Thymulin , also known as Serum Thymic Factor ( FTS , from the French Facteur Thymique Sérique ) or nonathymulin , is a 9-amino acid peptide hormone produced exclusively by thymic epithelial cells.",
        "evidence": "Thymulin is predominantly used in research settings: Immunology: Studying T-cell development and thymic function Aging Research: Investigating immunosenescence and thymic involution Zinc Biology: Understanding zinc-dependent immune regulation Pain Research: Exploring GABAergic analgesic pathways Gene Therapy: Thymulin gene (METCAM) delivery via viral vectors as a therapeutic strategy Reconstitution: Dissolve in sterile water or phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) Zinc Complexation: Incubate equimolar thymulin with zinc sulfate (ZnSO₄) for 30 minutes at room temperature to form the active zinc complex Concentration: Stock solutions typically 1-10 mM pH: Optimal activity at physiological pH (7.2-7.4) [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] [ 5 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Bach JF, Dardenne M, Pleau JM, Rosa J. \"Biochemical characterisation of a serum thymic factor.\" Nature . 1977;266(5597):55-57. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Dardenne M, Pleau JM, Nabarra B, et al. \"Contribution of zinc and other metals to the biological activity of the serum thymic factor.\" Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences . 1982;79(17):5370-5373.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "T-Cell Differentiation: Promotes maturation of immature T-cell precursors (CD4⁻CD8⁻) into mature CD4⁺ and CD8⁺ T-lymphocytes T-Cell Function: Enhances T-cell cytotoxic activity and helper T-cell function T-Cell Markers: Induces expression of T-cell surface markers (CD2, CD3, Thy-1) Cytokine Modulation: Modulates IL-2 production and IL-2 receptor expression Suppressor T-Cell Activation: Enhances suppressor T-cell activity, contributing to immune regulation NK Cell Modulation: Influences natural killer cell activity",
        "limitations": [
          "Rapid clearance necessitates frequent administration or sustained-release formulations Zinc must be co-administered for activity Limited human clinical trial data"
        ],
