        "sourceId": "pep-university",
        "url": "https://pepuniversity.com/peptide-codex.php#tirzepatide",
        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "Tirzepatide is the first dual incretin agonist - often called a \"twincretin\" - meaning it activates two separate hormone receptors at once: the GLP-1 receptor and the GIP receptor. If semaglutide is a single-key lock pick, tirzepatide picks two locks simultaneously. The GLP-1 side handles what you already know from semaglutide: appetite suppression, slower gastric emptying, and glucose-dependent insulin release. The GIP side adds something different - it acts directly on fat tissue to improve how the body stores and breaks down fat, enhances insulin sensitivity beyond what weight loss alone explains, and appears to amplify the appetite-suppressing effects of GLP-1 signaling in the brain. Critically, tirzepatide is deliberately engineered with stronger affinity for the GIP receptor than the GLP-1 receptor, which is intentional - it lets the compound push hard on the GIP side without triggering the severe nausea ceiling that limits aggressive GLP-1 dosing.",
