        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Vasopressin Chemical name Arginine vasopressin (AVP); Argipressin; Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) Molecular formula C₄₆H₆₅N₁₅O₁₂S₂ Molecular weight 1084.2 g/mol CAS number 11000-17-2 Sequence Cys-Tyr-Phe-Gln-Asn-Cys-Pro-Arg-Gly-NH₂ (disulfide bridge: Cys¹-Cys⁶) Length 9 amino acids (nonapeptide) Source Endogenous hormone; synthesized in hypothalamic magnocellular neurons Half-life 10-35 minutes (plasma) Targets V1a (AVPR1A), V1b/V3 (AVPR1B), V2 (AVPR2), oxytocin receptor (partial) Bioavailability Intravenous: 100%; not orally bioavailable Legal status FDA-approved (Vasostrict for vasodilatory shock; Pitressin historically) Vasopressin , also known as arginine vasopressin ( AVP ), argipressin , or antidiuretic hormone ( ADH ), is an endogenous nonapeptide hormone produced by magnocellular neurosecretory cells of the supraoptic nucleus and paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus .",
        "evidence": "The landmark Vasopressin and Septic Shock Trial (VASST) randomized 778 patients with septic shock to receive low-dose vasopressin (0.01-0.03 units/min) versus norepinephrine in addition to open-label vasopressors. While there was no significant difference in 28-day mortality overall (35.4% vs 39.3%, p=0.26), a pre-specified subgroup analysis showed that vasopressin reduced mortality in patients with less severe septic shock (norepinephrine dose 5-14 mcg/min at randomization): 26.5% vs 35.7% (p=0.05). The VANISH trial compared early vasopressin with norepinephrine as the first-line vasopressor in septic shock. Vasopressin did not improve kidney failure-free days (the primary endpoint) but was associated with a reduced need for renal replacement therapy, suggesting a potential renal-protective effect mediated through V1a-mediated efferent arteriolar constriction improving glomerular filtration.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Vasopressin exerts its effects through four receptor subtypes, each with distinct tissue distribution and signaling mechanisms. === V1a Receptor (AVPR1A) === [ 1 ] The V1a receptor is the most widely distributed vasopressin receptor, expressed on vascular smooth muscle cells, hepatocytes , platelets , myometrium , and in multiple brain regions including the lateral septum , hippocampus , and cortex . V1a couples to G q/11 proteins, activating phospholipase C (PLC) and triggering the IP₃/DAG/Ca²⁺ signaling cascade. Vascular effects: V1a activation on vascular smooth muscle produces potent vasoconstriction, increasing systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure. This effect is particularly important in the splanchnic circulation, skin, and skeletal muscle vasculature. In septic shock, the vasoconstrictive effect of vasopressin through V1a receptors is exploited therapeutically to restore vascular tone. Hepatic effects: V1a activation in hepatocytes stimulates glycogenolysis, contributing to glucose mobilization during stress.",
        "limitations": [],
        "safety": [
          "Vasopressin: Type IIB von Willebrand disease (may worsen thrombocytopenia); significant coronary or mesenteric artery disease (relative contraindication) Desmopressin: Moderate to severe renal impairment (CrCl <50 mL/min; risk of hyponatremia); habitual or psychogenic polydipsia; hyponatremia; Type IIB von Willebrand."
        ],
