        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Vasostatin Chemical name Chromogranin A (1-76) Fragment Molecular formula Large polypeptide Molecular weight ~8,600 Da CAS number N/A (endogenous fragment) Sequence N-terminal 76 amino acids of CgA Length 76 amino acids Source Endogenous (chromogranin A cleavage) Half-life 15-30 minutes (estimated) Targets Endothelial cells, cardiomyocytes, smooth muscle Bioavailability Requires parenteral administration Legal status Research compound (unscheduled) Vasostatin (also designated vasostatin-1 or CgA₁₋₇₆ ) is a 76-amino acid bioactive peptide derived from the N-terminal region of Chromogranin A (CgA), a 439-amino acid acidic glycoprotein stored in secretory granules of neuroendocrine, endocrine, and neuronal cells. Vasostatin is generated through proteolytic processing of CgA by prohormone convertases and other endopeptidases within the regulated secretory pathway.",
        "evidence": "Application Route Dose Range Model Cardiovascular research Intravenous 1-10 nmol/kg Rodent (rat/mouse) Antimicrobial studies Topical/in vitro 5-50 μM Cell culture / ex vivo Cardioprotection Intravenous 2-5 nmol/kg Ischemia-reperfusion models For detailed research protocols, see PepDex Wiki . [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ] ↑ 1.0 1.1 Aardal S, Helle KB (1992). \"The vasoinhibitory activity of bovine chromogranin A fragment (vasostatin) and its independence of extracellular calcium in isolated segments of human blood vessels.\" Regul Pept . 41(1):9-18. ↑ 2.0 2.1 Tota B et al. (2008). \"Chromogranin A-derived vasostatins: new players in the endocrine heart.\" Curr Med Chem . 15(14):1444-51. ↑ 3.0 3.1 Lugardon K et al. (2000). \"Antibacterial and antifungal activities of vasostatin-1.\" J Biol Chem . 275(15):10745-53. ↑ Helle KB et al. (2007). \"Chromogranin A as a calcium-binding precursor for a multitude of regulatory peptides.\" Ann N Y Acad Sci . P Pep Dex Track saturation levels, half-life decay curves, and injection schedules for 50+ peptides.",
        "halfLife": "",
        "halfLifeContext": "",
        "mechanism": "Vasostatin exerts its biological effects through multiple pathways: Endothelin-1 Suppression - Inhibits endothelin-1 (ET-1) secretion from endothelial cells, reducing vasoconstriction and vascular smooth muscle proliferation Nitric Oxide Modulation - Promotes endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNOS) activity, enhancing NO-mediated vasorelaxation Cardioprotection - Directly acts on cardiomyocytes to reduce β-adrenergic overstimulation; activates PI3K/Akt/NO survival pathway; counteracts isoproterenol-induced cardiac hypertrophy Anti-angiogenic Activity - Inhibits endothelial cell proliferation, migration, and tube formation through suppression of bFGF and VEGF signaling Antimicrobial Peptide Function - Adopts amphipathic α-helical structure that disrupts bacterial and fungal membranes; active against Staphylococcus aureus , Escherichia coli , and Candida albicans at micromolar concentrations Anti-inflammatory Effects - Reduces NF-κB activation and pro-inflammatory cytokine release (TNF-α, IL-6) from macrophages Catecholamine Counter-Regulation - Co-released with catecholamines but",
