        "summary": "From Wikipep - The Peptide Encyclopedia Vesugen Chemical name Vesugen (Lys-Glu-Asp) Molecular formula C₁₅H₂₅N₃O₈ Molecular weight 391.4 g/mol CAS number Not officially assigned Sequence Lys-Glu-Asp (KED) Length 3 amino acids Source Synthetic (Khavinson peptide bioregulator) Half-life ~10-30 minutes (estimated; small peptide) Targets Vascular endothelial gene regulation (proposed epigenetic mechanism) Bioavailability Oral (sublingual) or injection Legal status Dietary supplement (Russia); Research compound (elsewhere) Vesugen (Lys-Glu-Asp, KED ) is a synthetic tripeptide developed by Professor Vladimir Khavinson at the Saint Petersburg Institute of Bioregulation and Gerontology as part of the Russian \"peptide bioregulator\" paradigm. Vesugen is designed to target vascular tissue, specifically aiming to normalize endothelial function and vascular homeostasis through proposed gene-regulatory mechanisms.",
