        "lastReviewed": "2026-09-17",
        "summary": "Vilon is a synthetic dipeptide (Lys-Glu) developed by Vladimir Khavinson as the minimized bioactive core of thymus gland polypeptide extracts - the result of systematic fractionation work that identified the smallest peptide sequence retaining the full immunoregulatory bioregulator activity of the thymic extract epithalamin-family compounds. As a dipeptide, Vilon is the simplest and most bioavailable of all Khavinson bioregulators: at two amino acids it is below the threshold where proteolytic degradation becomes a significant pharmacokinetic challenge, allowing it to reach target tissues intact even without the modified amino acid chemistry required for larger peptides. Vilon's primary mechanism operates through the thymus - the central lymphoid organ responsible for T-cell maturation, selection, and export into the peripheral immune system.",
